// Runtime/SlabRuntime.maxon — ⭐⭐ **THE OBJECT LAYER: SIZE CLASSES, SPANS, AND THE HYBRID FREE LIST.**
// `SlabArena.maxon` beneath this file answers *"where does an 8 KiB chunk come from?"*; this file answers
// *"which slot of which span serves a 37-byte request, and where does it go when it dies?"*.
//
// ⭐⭐ **THE GLOBAL LOCK, THE PER-P REMOTE-FREE MPSC QUEUES AND THE `owning_p` GATE ARE NOT OPTIONAL ONCE
// REAL Ps EXIST, AND THE SLAB WAS MEASURED BREAKING WITHOUT THEM.**
// `scripts/multicore-stress/alloc-torture.maxon` at `MAXON_MAX_PROCS=2` died with **exit 86,
// `slabSpanExhaustedPastItsEnd`** — INV-1 below, tripped because two OS threads were popping slots out of
// ONE unsharded mcache row. At 7 it segfaulted outright. The allocator naming its own invariant is what
// localises such a fault to this file rather than leaving a mystery fault in the scheduler.
//
// ⭐⭐⭐ **THE INVARIANT THE SHARDING RESTS ON:**
//
// > **A P HAS AT MOST ONE M AT ANY INSTANT, AND ONLY THE M *HOLDING* A P ALLOCATES FROM THAT P's ROW. AN M
// > THAT HOLDS NO P TAKES `SlabRawShardRow`, WHICH IS SERIALISED UNDER THE LOCK.**
//
// It is weaker than *"a P is bound to exactly one M for the M's life"*, which does not hold: a P is handed
// from an M blocked in a kernel call to another M. The row does not care about the M's LIFE — it cares that
// at any instant one thread writes it, which the sentence above says and the stronger one only implied.
// `SlabRawShardRow` carries how the P-less arm is made true rather than assumed, and
// `SchedRuntime`'s header carries the scheduler's half.
//
// **THE SHAPE, IN FOUR SENTENCES.** Each P owns its own mcache ROW (`emitSlabResolveShard` answers the P, the
// row is its clamped `id`) and is the SOLE WRITER of every span cached there, so the pop is lockless. A span
// carries the P that owns it (`MspanOwningPOffset`), and a cached span whose owner is not me is a MISS — not
// something to pop — which is the one check a `free_count` test structurally cannot make. A free by a P that
// does not own the slot's span CAS-pushes it onto the owner's Treiber queue at `POffRemoteFreeHead`, and the
// owner replays the whole chain on its next alloc slow path. Everything that touches state more than one P
// can reach — refill, the mcentral lists, the metadata slab, the arena, the scavenger — runs under ONE
// recursive lock in the state region; the fast path never takes it.
//
// ⚠ **ALL OF IT IS COMPILED INTO EVERY HEAP PROGRAM, AND THE ONLY BUILD-TIME FORK IS THE TARGET'S MACHINE
// MODEL.** Whether a second thread can exist is a RUN-TIME fact — the scheduler word in the state head
// (`SlabStateSchedulerOffset`) is 0 until `__sched_init_procs` publishes it — so one body serves a program
// with no scheduler and the same program after its first `async`. On a `singleMachine` target (wasm) the
// walk that reads that word is a constant, because the backend has no TLS slot to read.
//
// ⚠ **A FULLY FREE SPAN IS DESTROYED RATHER THAN HAVING ITS FOOTPRINT DECOMMITTED.** Its chunks go back to
// the arena and the decommit happens at the arena's 64 KiB commit granule. The reason is not taste: a span's
// footprint is 8 KiB-granular, `madvise` refuses an address that is not page-aligned, and arm64-macOS's
// page is 16 KiB — see `runtime/SlabArena.maxon`'s `__slab_arena_scavenge` for the two ways a
// per-span decommit goes wrong there.
//
// ⭐⭐ **THE CENTREPIECE: A SPAN CARRIES BOTH A FREE LIST AND A VIRGIN BUMP REGION.**
// A slot reaches a caller from one of two places, and they differ in whether the memory is DIRTY:
//
//   * the FREE LIST — a RECYCLED slot. It holds the previous occupant's bytes plus the link written into
//     its first word when it was freed, so it MUST be zeroed before the caller sees it.
//   * the BUMP REGION `[bump_next, bump_end)` — slots that have NEVER been handed out. Their pages came
//     straight from `__slab_arena_alloc_chunks`, and every chunk it hands out reads ZERO — fresh address
//     space, or a released run the arena zeroed (INV-4, `__slab_arena_free_chunks`). They cost NOTHING
//     to hand out.
//
// ⛔⛔ **THIS IS WHY THERE IS NO `__slab_build_freelist`.** Threading an intrusive
// free list through a fresh span writes a next-pointer into slot[0] of EVERY slot, which DIRTIES every one
// of them — so building the list up front was not merely wasted work, it was the thing that would FORCE a
// memzero on every first-ever allocation. **Leaving the region pristine is the precondition for any
// zero-elision at all.** It is FINER-GRAINED than Go, whose `needzero` is per-SPAN: Go re-zeroes even
// never-used slots in a span that has seen a single free, while this cursor is per-SLOT.
//
// **THE THREE INVARIANTS. Every mutation site below re-establishes them:**
//   INV-1  free_count == |free_list| + (bump_end - bump_next) / slot_size
//   INV-2  bump_end   == base_addr + slot_size * total_slots   (DERIVED, never stored)
//   INV-3  every byte in [bump_next, bump_end) is ZERO
//
// **INV-1 TRAPS** (`RuntimeAbort.slabSpanExhaustedPastItsEnd`): a `free_count > 0` with an empty free list
// and an exhausted bump region would hand out a slot PAST the span's end, silently corrupting whatever the
// arena placed in the next chunk.
//
// ⭐ The case that looks wrong and is not: a span may be returned to mcentral carrying BOTH an unconsumed
// bump region AND a populated free list. (Take 3 slots from a fresh 1024-slot span, free all 3 →
// `free_count == 1024 == total_slots` → returned, holding 3 free-list entries and 1021 never-used slots.)
// Both survive in the mspan and the next taker keeps using both; INV-1 holds throughout.
//
// ⭐ **WHO MAY WRITE WHAT — the whole concurrency argument, in one table, because every `if` below is an
// instance of it.** Before a scheduler exists there is one thread and every row reads "anyone".
//
//   | state                                   | writer                                   | protection      |
//   |-----------------------------------------|------------------------------------------|-----------------|
//   | a span's free list / free_count         | the P in its `owning_p`, ONLY            | sole ownership  |
//   | `mcache[row][class]`                    | the P whose id clamps to `row`           | sole ownership  |
//   | a P's remote-free queue head            | any P (push) / the owner (detach)        | `atomicCas`     |
//   | a row's traffic columns                 | the P whose id clamps to `row`           | sole ownership  |
//   | the raw row's traffic columns           | any P-less thread, any clamped P         | `atomicRmw`     |
//   | mcentral heads, metadata slab, arena,   | anyone                                   | `__slab_lock`   |
//   |   the arena map's LEVELS, os-direct     |                                          |                 |
//   | the arena map's ENTRIES (read side)     | —                                        | aligned words   |
//
// ⚠ **THE RAW ROW BREAKS THE SECOND ROW OF THAT TABLE AND IS WHY THE LOCK REACHES THE FAST PATH AT ALL.**
// `SlabRawShardRow` is shared by every OS thread that owns no P, so *"the P whose id clamps to this row"* is
// not one thread there. Both ends therefore serialise: a class-alloc whose row IS the raw row takes the lock
// (`buildSlabAllocEntry`), and a free of a span whose owner is `SpanOwnerState.none` takes it too
// (`buildSlabFree`) — but only once a scheduler exists, because before that the lone main thread is the only
// writer and would be paying for a race it cannot have.

// A machine word, for the COMPILE-TIME size arithmetic below — the mspan record's width, the mcache row's
// stride, and where the state region's second array starts. The EMITTED `base + index · 8` is not spelled
// here at all: it is `MmRuntime.emitWordSlotAddr`, exported so a third copy of that
// arithmetic did not get written. What stays private is the LAYOUT constant, because a shared "8" is not a
// shared fact — these are strides of tables this file owns.
let SlabRecordWordBytes = 8 as ByteOffset

// --- The mspan record ---
//
// ⭐⭐ **THE CLASS IS STORED ONCE.** `slot_size` is `__slab_class_size(class_idx)` and `total_slots` is
// `__slab_objs_per_span(class_idx)`, so storing either beside `class_idx` would be a derived fact written
// down beside the fact it derives from. Only `class_idx` is stored and the geometry is read from the
// `.rdata` table below.
//
// ⚠ **THAT IS A TRADE TO MEASURE, NOT A WIN.** A per-span `slot_size` reaches the hot path in ONE load from
// a record it already holds, where this shape costs one load of `class_idx` plus one indexed `.rdata` load
// — but the `.rdata` line is shared by every span of every class, so it is the same cache line for the whole
// program where per-span copies are not. Which of those dominates is a measurement nobody here has taken;
// what IS certain is that two numbers that cannot disagree stand where three that could would.
let MspanBaseAddrOffset = 0
let MspanFreeListOffset = 8
let MspanFreeCountOffset = 16
let MspanNextSpanOffset = 24
let MspanClassIdxOffset = 32
let MspanBumpNextOffset = 40

// Who owns this span: a P pointer, or one of the negative states below.
//
// A span recycled to another P keeps `free_count != 0`, so nothing but this field tells a span an mcache
// row still owns from one it merely still points at — and popping from the second hands one slot to two
// processors.
//
// It holds a P POINTER rather than a P id, because a cross-processor free would otherwise turn the id
// back into a P through a bounded array lookup on every free. The one thing an id buys — the mcache row —
// is one load away (`POffId`), on the alloc path that needs the row anyway.
let MspanOwningPOffset = 48 as ByteOffset

// Slots freed by a thread that does not hold this span cached. A Treiber stack anyone may CAS-push and
// only the caching P pops, which is what lets the owner's own free list stay a single-writer structure
// while a span is reachable from every processor at once.
//
// The span carries this rather than the P, so a span can move between processors without stranding the
// frees queued against it. A per-P queue is drained only by that P, so a worker that goes idle holds every
// slot the main thread later frees into its spans.
let MspanRemoteHeadOffset = 56

// How many slots are on that list. `free_count` cannot answer, because only the caching processor writes
// it and a queued slot arrived from a thread that was not it — so a census reading `total - free_count`
// alone reports a queued slot as live, and a span whose queue nobody has collected as fully in use.
//
// ⚠ A push steps it up before it moves the head, and a collect steps it down by a whole detached chain
// before replaying it, so a reader can find it short of the slots in flight but never counting one twice.
// A census under `__slab_lock` with no free or collect in flight is exact.
let MspanRemoteCountOffset = 64 as ByteOffset

// ⭐ **THE RECORD'S WIDTH, DERIVED FROM ITS LAST FIELD — and stated HERE rather than at the check that
// reads it.** A width spelled over there as `<last field> + SlabRecordWordBytes` stops being the width the moment a
// field is appended past it, and the guard against overlapping headers then silently measures 56 bytes of a
// 64-byte record. Naming the width once, beside the field that determines it, is what makes the next
// appended field move it.
let MspanRecordBytes = MspanRemoteCountOffset + SlabRecordWordBytes

// One mspan header's slot in the metadata slab. 80 keeps successive headers 16-byte aligned inside the
// 8 KiB metadata chunk, which holds 102 of them. `checkSlabRuntimeGeometry` asserts the record fits rather
// than trusting this number: a stale value here silently overlaps adjacent mspan headers.
let MspanSlotBytes = 80

// A span is owned only while it is cached, and this word is the whole state machine. A P pointer means the
// span sits in that P's mcache row and only that P pops its free list; every other value is a state it is
// in while no processor holds it.
//
//   `None`     the shared raw row — a real owner, whose spans belong to whichever P-less OS thread
//              reaches them and whose accesses serialise under `__slab_lock`.
//   `Full`     exhausted, on no list. The first free back into one links it onto its class's partial
//              list.
//   `Partial`  on its class's partial list, with at least one slot free or queued. Any P may take it.
//   `Freed`    the header is on the metadata free list. Only the census walk reads this.
//
// `Full` is a state rather than an absence because `__slab_refill` takes the partial list's HEAD and hands
// it straight to its caller without testing whether it has a slot. An exhausted span left on that list
// would be handed out with nothing to allocate from; giving the refill a search instead would make every
// refill in an allocate-heavy program walk a list of spans it cannot use.
//
// ⚠ Every negative value is outside the P-pointer domain and needs no array lookup to be recognised.
enum SpanOwnerState
	none = 0
	partial = -1
	freed = -2
	full = -3
end 'SpanOwnerState'

// --- Sharding ---
//
// ⭐⭐ **THE mcache IS 256 ROWS OF ONE SPAN POINTER PER CLASS.** The rows cost 256 × 68 × 8 = 139,264
// bytes of an arena that is committed in whole 2 MiB units either way (`SlabArena.maxon`), so the
// width is paid for out of chunks the process already owns.
let SlabMaxShards = 256

// ⭐⭐ **ROW 255 IS THE RAW-THREAD ROW, AND A NEGATIVE ID MAPS TO IT — NOT TO 0.** A raw OS thread sharing
// row 0 with P[0] would race that P's LOCKLESS pop and hand one slab slot to both, which is heap
// corruption with no diagnostic. The reservation costs one row
// in 256 and it is what makes "no live P" a state of its own rather than an alias for the first one.
//
// ⭐⭐⭐ **THE RULE DOES NOT DEPEND ON A ROSTER OF THREADS, WHICH IS WHAT MAKES IT HOLD AS THE RUNTIME GROWS
// ONE.**
//
// > **A THREAD WITH NO P TAKES THIS ROW, WHETHER IT IS NOT YET AN M OR IS AN M HOLDING NO PROCESSOR.**
//
// ⚠⚠ **THE TWO HALVES OF THAT RULE HAVE VERY DIFFERENT COVERAGE, AND CONFLATING THEM WOULD BE CLAIMING A
// GATE NOBODY HAS WATCHED MOVE.**
//   * **A THREAD THAT IS NOT YET AN M IS EXERCISED CONSTANTLY.** `emitSchedStartM` publishes its TLS slot
//     LAST, so its own `__slab_alloc` runs with the slot still 0 and lands here. ⚠ **That is the
//     main thread's bring-up**: a worker's M struct is built by its CREATOR (`emitSchedAllocM`,
//     because a thread cannot learn its own joinable handle) and so comes from that machine's
//     shard. Every green-thread program in the suite drives it, and it is the arm that keeps the
//     ROW itself honest.
//   * **AN M HOLDING NO P IS EXERCISED.** The poller releases its processor before it waits and, back from
//     the wait with `M->currentP == 0`, runs `__gt_timer_check_held` — **allocating, through this arm, with no
//     processor to key a shard by.** Every green-thread program with a timer or a child drives it, and the
//     predicate below absorbs it by construction.
//
// ⚠ **THE MECHANISM COSTS NOTHING NEW, WHICH IS WHY THE SECOND HALF IS TRUE BY CONSTRUCTION RATHER THAN BY
// TEST.** `emitSlabResolveShard` sends *"no scheduler"*, *"no M"* and *"an M holding no P"* to this row
// through one walk, so the third needs no arm of its own and cannot be forgotten when it first becomes
// reachable.
//
// ⭐ **AND WHAT THE ROW BUYS IS THE ALLOCATOR'S HALF OF THE RESTATED SHARD INVARIANT.** `emitSlabResolveShard`
// keys `mcache[row]` on `P->id` and every span carries `MspanOwningPOffset`, and that rests on **"a P has
// at most one M at any instant, and only the M HOLDING a P allocates from that P's row"** — weaker than
// *"a P is bound to one M for life"*, and what the lockless pop actually needs. A thread between Ps has no row of its own to race; it comes here, where the rule is the
// LOCK rather than single ownership.
let SlabRawShardRow = SlabMaxShards - 1

// --- The `.rdata` tables ---
//
// ⭐⭐ **ONE PACKED WORD PER CLASS: `slot_size | (objects_per_span << 32)`.** Both numbers are pure
// functions of the class index (`SlabClassTable`), and the hot path wants `slot_size` on every allocation
// while `objects_per_span` is wanted only by INV-1's guard and by the fully-free test. Packing them into
// ONE 8-byte entry makes the span geometry ONE indexed load instead of two, which is the whole of what the
// "store `class_idx` alone" decision costs.
//
// ⚠ **THE SHIFT IS 32 AND NOT THE 16 THE WIDTHS WOULD ALLOW.** A slot size needs 16 bits (32768) and an
// object count 11 (1024), so both would fit either side of a 16-bit split — and then the day a class ladder
// grows past 65535 the two fields would silently overlap. At 32 the fields have 4× the headroom their
// domains need and the extraction masks read as what they are.
let SlabClassGeomSlotShift = 32
let SlabClassGeomSlotMask = 0xFFFFFFFF
let SlabClassGeomHalfBits = 32
let SlabClassGeomSlotSizeOffset = 0
let SlabClassGeomSlotsOffset = (SlabClassGeomSlotShift / BitsPerByte) as ByteOffset

// The label this file registers each table under.
let SlabSizeToClass8Label = "__slab_size_to_class8"
let SlabSizeToClass128Label = "__slab_size_to_class128"
let SlabClassGeomLabel = "__slab_class_geom"

// ⚠ The same three spellings as bytes, and the pair cannot collapse into `GlobalDataTable.labelBytesOf`.
// `lowerRawIntrinsic` emits a `rdataAddr` for each of these from tier source, inside `pipeline.run()`,
// and the payloads are not registered until `installSlabRuntime` several steps later
// (`afterPipeline`) — so a shared record fetched by label would be a lookup of a payload
// that does not exist yet. A drift between a pair is not silent: nothing would register the payload the
// emitted op names, and `GlobalDataTable.rdataOffsetOf` panics by name on it at relocation time.
export let SlabSizeToClass8LabelBytes = b"__slab_size_to_class8"
export let SlabSizeToClass128LabelBytes = b"__slab_size_to_class128"
export let SlabClassGeomLabelBytes = b"__slab_class_geom"

// ⭐ **THE TWO REVERSE LOOKUPS' DIVISORS, AS SHIFTS.** `SlabClasses.maxon` states the divisors
// (`SlabSmallSizeDiv` 8, `SlabLargeSizeDiv` 128); the emitted code divides by them, and a divide by a power
// of two is a shift. A width and the shift that divides by it are ONE fact written twice by necessity, so
// `checkSlabRuntimeGeometry` asserts the pair agrees — the rule `SlabArena.requireShiftMatchesWidth` states
// in full for its own four pairs.
let SlabSmallSizeDivShift = 3
let SlabLargeSizeDivShift = 7

// ⭐ **THE SMALL TABLE'S UPPER BOUND, WHICH IS GO'S AND IS NOT `SlabSmallSizeMax`.** Go routes
// `size <= smallSizeMax - smallSizeDiv` (1016) through the 8-stepped table and everything above it through
// the 128-stepped one, because index `(1024+7)/8 == 128` is the 8-table's LAST entry and the 128-table's
// entry 0 already answers for 1017..1024. Written as the derivation rather than as 1016 so a changed
// divisor moves it.
let SlabSmallTableMaxSize = SlabSmallSizeMax - SlabSmallSizeDiv

// --- The one `.data` word this family owns ---
//
// ⭐ **ALL OF THE ALLOCATOR'S MUTABLE STATE LIVES BEHIND ONE POINTER.** `.data` slots are laid out into
// every heap program's image, so each one is a cost paid by
// every program that allocates a single box. The mcentral heads, the mcache rows and the metadata slab's
// bump cursor are all ARRAYS or PAIRS — none of them can live in a `.data` word anyway (`layOut` orders
// single words in an order this table may not depend on) — so they share one lazily-committed region and
// this word is its base. Zero means "not yet committed", which is the state the `.data` image already
// expresses; there is nothing to initialise and so nothing to guard.
export let SlabStateLabel = b"__slab_state"

// The region's fixed head, then the three arrays. `SlabStateMcentralOffset` onwards are DERIVED from the
// class count so a regenerated ladder moves them rather than leaving a stale stride behind.
//
// ⭐ **`SlabStateMetaFreeOffset` EXISTS BECAUSE SOMETHING DESTROYS SPANS — the free that returns a
// span's last slot.** Without it every span reclaimed that way would leak its header for the life
// of the process.
// ⚠ **THE THREE ARE `runtime/SlabRuntime.maxon`'s TO READ AND WRITE, AND ARE DECLARED HERE BECAUSE THE
// LOCK OFFSET BELOW IS DERIVED FROM THEM.** The tier restates the head; `checkSlabRuntimeGeometry` pins
// the lock offset and the totals both sides derive, which is what a drift moves.
// Chained rather than written as 0/8/16 so that inserting a head word moves every offset after it — the
// lock's included, since it is derived from the last of them.
let SlabStateMetaBumpOffset = 0 as ByteOffset
let SlabStateMetaEndOffset = SlabStateMetaBumpOffset + SlabRecordWordBytes
let SlabStateMetaFreeOffset = SlabStateMetaEndOffset + SlabRecordWordBytes

// ⭐⭐ **THE SCHEDULER WORD — the TLS slot's TEB offset once `__sched_init_procs` has published it, 0
// before, and never 0 again.** One word answers the two questions every allocation and every free ask:
// *"may another OS thread be inside the allocator"* (non-zero) and *"where is my machine"* (the offset the
// `tlsSlotLoad` takes). It lives HERE and not in a `.data` word because `__sched_tls_teb_offset` is laid
// out only in a program with a scheduler, while every heap program's allocator reads this one.
// `emitSlabPublishScheduler` is its single writer.
let SlabStateSchedulerOffset = SlabStateMetaFreeOffset + SlabRecordWordBytes

// Where a thread with no machine keeps the lock depth it steps around `__slab_lock` — the allocator's
// twin of `SchedRuntime.SchedLockDepthSinkLabel`, in the region rather than in `.data` for the scheduler
// word's reason. Nothing reads it; it is an address that is not zero.
let SlabStateLockDepthSinkOffset = SlabStateSchedulerOffset + SlabRecordWordBytes

// ⭐⭐ **THE GLOBAL SLOW-PATH LOCK LIVES *HERE*, IN THE STATE REGION, AND NOT IN A `.data` WORD —
// WHICH IS A `TargetIr`-PIN ARGUMENT, NOT A TIDINESS ONE.** Every `TargetIr` pin renders the whole
// `data { … }` table with each label's byte OFFSET, so one new `.data` word shifts every label
// after it and re-mints **every heap program's pin**. A recursive lock word inside the region
// `__slab_state` already points at costs the image nothing and the pins nothing, and the region's
// zeroing is what makes it a free lock.
//
// ⛔⛔ **THE LOCK ORDER IS `__sched_lock` → `__slab_lock`, AND IT HOLDS BECAUSE THIS SIDE NEVER TAKES THE
// OTHER ONE.** The scheduler allocates while holding its run-queue lock (`__gt_spawn`, `__gt_init`), so that
// nesting is routine; nothing under `__slab_alloc`/`__slab_free` reaches a scheduler entry point, so the
// reverse nesting does not exist and there is no order for two files to remember. **A future caller that
// makes the allocator call into `GtRuntime` while holding this lock introduces a deadlock**, which is why
// the direction is written down here rather than left to be re-derived.
let SlabStateLockOffset = SlabStateLockDepthSinkOffset + SlabRecordWordBytes
let SlabStateLockBytes = RecursiveLockBytes
let SlabStateMcentralOffset = SlabStateLockOffset + (SlabStateLockBytes as ByteOffset)

// ⭐⭐ **THE TRAFFIC COLUMNS — six per mcache row, past the mcache.** A row's columns have the row's
// writer: the P whose id clamps to it steps them with a plain add, and the raw row — every P-less thread
// and every clamped P — steps them atomically once a scheduler exists. Before one exists the lone main
// thread steps the raw row plainly. A reader walks the rows (`emitSlabTrafficRead`); the walk's answer is
// the process-wide figure and no `.data` word carries one.
//
// The RAW trio counts the slab requests of the counted doors (`__slab_alloc`, `__slab_alloc_raw`,
// `__slab_free`). The TRACKED trio counts managed boxes: the tracked doors (`__slab_alloc_tracked`,
// `__slab_free_tracked`, and `__slab_free_box` under `--poison-freed`) credit it and the raw trio not at
// all. The scaffold pair (`SlabScaffoldAllocName`) credits neither. The layers are disjoint, and the
// process-wide figures add them (`MmRuntime.processWideFigure`).
//
// The tracked byte column holds each box's request less `boxPrefixBytes`, so a byte figure is a plain sum
// of monotone non-negative words and cannot read a transient negative across threads.
//
// ⭐⭐⭐ **EVERY COLUMN IS MONOTONIC, AND A LIVE COUNT IS DERIVED AT READ TIME AS `total − frees`.** An
// allocation steps two columns and a free steps one, so the hot path carries one read-modify-write per
// layer fewer than a maintained live word costs; the readers are cold and a subtraction there is free.
// The derivation is exact wherever the maintained word was: both columns are stepped by the row's own
// writer under the row's own discipline, and `emitSlabTrafficRead` samples a row's columns together, so
// nothing a single word ruled out is admitted here.
enum SlabTrafficColumn
	trackedFrees
	trackedTotal
	trackedBytes
	rawTotal
	rawFrees
	rawBytes
end 'SlabTrafficColumn'

enum SlabTrafficLayer
	tracked
	raw
end 'SlabTrafficLayer'

// What one allocator event moves: an allocation credits the layer's total and byte columns, a free its
// frees column. Every column only ever rises, which is what lets a phase delta be taken over any of them.
union SlabTrafficEvent
	alloc(bytes ValueId)
	free
end 'SlabTrafficEvent'

// The value the state `.data` word holds until the region is committed. Named rather than left a bare 0
// because `emitSlabStateBase`'s probe TESTS it while `runtime/SlabRuntime.maxon` both tests and CLEARS it,
// and a literal at each site is a chance for one to stop meaning the same thing as the others.
let SlabStateUncommitted = 0

// ⭐ **THE ADDRESS A CALL MUST STEP `__slab_lock`'s DEPTH DOWN AT, OR THIS — *"I hold no lock"*.** The
// sole-writer arm never takes the lock, so carrying a separate flag beside the address would be two values
// across the pop where the address alone answers both *"is there a `Leave` outstanding"* and *"whose depth
// word"*. No lock-depth word can live at address 0 — every one of them is a field of the state region or of
// a machine — so the value is outside the domain it stands in for.
let SlabNoLockDepth = 0

// A P-less refill stamps this as its span's owner, and `__slab_free` takes the raw-row arm only on
// `SpanOwnerState.none`.
let SlabNoProcessor = SpanOwnerState.none

// --- Entry points ---

let SlabAllocName = b"__slab_alloc"

// ⭐ **THE NON-ZEROING DOOR — Go's `mallocgc(…, needzero: false)`.**
//
// ⚠⚠ **AUDIT RULE — read this before adding a caller.** `__slab_alloc_raw` may ONLY be used where the
// caller provably writes EVERY BYTE of the returned region before anything else can read it, AND the region
// is never walked as MANAGED POINTERS. A region is "walked as managed pointers" if an element walk, a
// hash-table teardown or an `array.set` old-occupant decref will ever run over it; such a buffer MUST come
// from `__slab_alloc`. A non-zeroed slot read as a pointer and decref'd is exactly the bug the zeroing
// contract exists to prevent — a sparsely-filled hash table whose unwritten slots hold the previous
// occupant's garbage, decref'd as if live.
//
// ⚠ **IT HAS NO CALLER IN the compiler TODAY, AND SO COSTS NOTHING**: `DeadFunctionElimination` runs after every
// `install*Runtime` and drops it from every program. It is built anyway for the reason `SlabArena.maxon`
// built the page layer before anything was wired to it — the door and its audit rule exist before the first
// caller has to invent them, and the ZEROING is a build-time argument (`emitSlabPop`'s `zeroed`), so
// admitting a caller is an argument rather than a body.
let SlabAllocRawName = b"__slab_alloc_raw"
let SlabFreeName = b"__slab_free"
let SlabTrackedAllocName = b"__slab_alloc_tracked"
let SlabTrackedFreeName = b"__slab_free_tracked"
let SlabPastEndName = b"__slab_past_end"
let SlabEvictUnownedName = b"__slab_evict_unowned"

// `__slab_alloc_scaffold(size) -> ptr` and `__slab_free_scaffold(ptr)` — the zeroing door and its
// free, crediting neither the raw row nor the calling green thread's columns. `slabCountingOf`
// decides which buffers take them.
let SlabScaffoldAllocName = b"__slab_alloc_scaffold"
let SlabScaffoldFreeName = b"__slab_free_scaffold"

// `__slab_free_box(ptr)` — the tracked free that fills a freed managed box; built only under
// `--poison-freed`, see `slabFreeDoorOf` and `buildSlabFreedBoxPoison`.
let SlabFreeBoxName = b"__slab_free_box"

let SlabRoundedSizeName = b"__slab_rounded_size"
let SlabStateName = b"__slab_state_base"
let SlabMetaAllocName = b"__slab_meta_alloc"
let SlabMetaFreeName = b"__slab_meta_free"
let SlabRefillName = b"__slab_refill"
let SlabFreeToSpanName = b"__slab_free_to_span"

// Collect one span's remote free list onto the free list its caching P pops. Called where a P caches a
// span and where a cached span looks empty, so a slot another thread queued is never invisible to the P
// that owns the span now.
let SlabCollectRemoteName = b"__slab_collect_remote"

// The two slot roads that take `__slab_lock`: an exhausted span returned to its class's partial list, which
// is once per span rather than once per free, and a span of the shared raw row, which any P-less thread
// frees into.
let SlabFreeToFullName = b"__slab_free_to_full"
let SlabFreeToRawName = b"__slab_free_to_raw"
let SlabSpanDestroyName = b"__slab_span_destroy"

let SlabDoorHandled = 1
let SlabDoorOwnerMoved = 0

// `0x3F` is loud when a use-after-free reads the dead memory it should not have: as a small integer it is
// 63, never the 0 a correct zeroed slot holds, and a word of it as a pointer is `0x3F3F3F3F3F3F3F3F`, a
// non-canonical x64 address that faults on dereference.
let SlabFreedBoxPoisonByte = 0x3F

// ⭐⭐ **THE FIVE THIS FILE NAMES BUT NO LONGER BUILDS.** Their bodies are `runtime/SlabRuntime.maxon`
// source — the OS-direct pair, the state region's commit and the metadata slab's two cursors — and what
// stays here is the `StdOp.call` sites the builders above mint, which is why the names do. That mint is also
// the whole reach evidence the from-`main` walk has about the tier file, so
// `StdlibSource.runtimeTierFileIsCompilerCalled` names it — see `SlabRuntimeTierFileName`.
let SlabOsDirectAllocName = b"__slab_os_direct_alloc"
let SlabAllocOversizeName = b"__slab_alloc_oversize"
let SlabFreeOversizeName = b"__slab_free_oversize"
let SlabTrackedAllocOversizeName = b"__slab_alloc_tracked_oversize"
let SlabTrackedFreeOversizeName = b"__slab_free_tracked_oversize"
let SlabScaffoldAllocOversizeName = b"__slab_alloc_scaffold_oversize"
let SlabScaffoldFreeOversizeName = b"__slab_free_scaffold_oversize"

// `counted` credits the raw columns, `tracked` the tracked columns and `scaffolding` neither; a door
// pays one row walk whichever it credits.
enum SlabCounting
	counted
	tracked
	scaffolding
end 'SlabCounting'

export enum SlabBufferKind
	managedBox
	cownRecord
	processorState
	environmentValue
	sharedValue
	hostText
	subprocessState
	awaitAnyScratch
	cownBehaviour
	timerStore
	subprocessTable
	subprocessSlotBuffer
	netpollTable
	netpollDescriptor
	hostSnapshot
end 'SlabBufferKind'

// A buffer whose allocation count follows the schedule or the environment is scaffolding: through a
// counted door it would reach the allocation counts `scale-test` and `tests/parallelCompile` require
// to be identical between two compiles of one program.
//
// `hostSnapshot` holds the lists the WASI host writes through `cabi_realloc`, which are never
// freed: through a counted door they would fail the leak gate and move the raw `HeapStats`
// counters, which `specs/command-line-args.md`'s
// `reading-the-arguments-leaves-no-raw-allocation-live` pins at 0.
function slabCountingOf(kind SlabBufferKind) returns SlabCounting
	return match kind 'kind'
		managedBox gives SlabCounting.tracked
		cownRecord or
		processorState or
		environmentValue or
		sharedValue or
		hostText or
		subprocessState gives SlabCounting.counted
		awaitAnyScratch or
		cownBehaviour or
		timerStore or
		subprocessTable or
		subprocessSlotBuffer or
		netpollTable or
		netpollDescriptor or
		hostSnapshot gives SlabCounting.scaffolding
	end 'kind'
end 'slabCountingOf'

type SlabDoors
	let alloc as immutable ByteArray
	let free as immutable ByteArray
	let allocOversize as immutable ByteArray
	let freeOversize as immutable ByteArray

	static function of(counting SlabCounting) returns SlabDoors
		return match counting 'counting'
			counted gives Self{alloc: SlabAllocName, free: SlabFreeName, allocOversize: SlabAllocOversizeName, freeOversize: SlabFreeOversizeName}
			tracked gives Self{alloc: SlabTrackedAllocName, free: SlabTrackedFreeName, allocOversize: SlabTrackedAllocOversizeName, freeOversize: SlabTrackedFreeOversizeName}
			scaffolding gives Self{alloc: SlabScaffoldAllocName, free: SlabScaffoldFreeName, allocOversize: SlabScaffoldAllocOversizeName, freeOversize: SlabScaffoldFreeOversizeName}
		end 'counting'
	end 'of'
end 'SlabDoors'

union SlabFreeDoor
	plain(counting SlabCounting)
	poisoning

	function name() returns immutable ByteArray
		return match self 'door'
			plain(counting) gives SlabDoors.of(counting).free
			poisoning gives SlabFreeBoxName
		end 'door'
	end 'name'

	function counting() returns SlabCounting
		return match self 'door'
			plain(counting) gives counting
			poisoning gives SlabCounting.tracked
		end 'door'
	end 'counting'

	function poisons() returns bool
		return match self 'door'
			plain gives false
			poisoning gives true
		end 'door'
	end 'poisons'
end 'SlabFreeDoor'

// The one decision of which door frees a buffer of `kind`. `installSlabRuntime` builds the box door and
// `emitSlabFree` calls it from this same answer, so a call never names a door the program lacks.
function slabFreeDoorOf(module StdModule, kind SlabBufferKind) returns SlabFreeDoor
	if module.poisonFreed and kind == SlabBufferKind.managedBox 'poisonsTheBox'
		return SlabFreeDoor.poisoning
	end 'poisonsTheBox'

	return SlabFreeDoor.plain(slabCountingOf(kind))
end 'slabFreeDoorOf'

function slabThreadTrafficOf(counting SlabCounting, traffic ThreadTraffic) returns ThreadTraffic
	return match counting 'counting'
		counted or
		tracked gives traffic
		scaffolding gives ThreadTraffic.none
	end 'counting'
end 'slabThreadTrafficOf'

// A tracked credit's bytes are the request less `boxPrefixBytes`; `MmRuntime.processWideFigure` adds the
// same prefix back per box, so changing one without the other skews the process-wide byte count with no
// failure.
function emitSlabCountingCredit(module StdModule, block IrBlock, rowBase ValueId, event SlabTrafficEvent, atomic bool, counting SlabCounting, ids ValueMinter)
	match counting 'counting'
		scaffolding then return
		counted then emitSlabTrafficCredit(module, block: block, rowBase: rowBase, layer: SlabTrafficLayer.raw, event: event, atomic: atomic, ids: ids)
		tracked then emitSlabTrafficCredit(module, block: block, rowBase: rowBase, layer: SlabTrafficLayer.tracked, event: emitSlabTrackedEvent(module, block: block, event: event, ids: ids), atomic: atomic, ids: ids)
	end 'counting'
end 'emitSlabCountingCredit'

function emitSlabTrackedEvent(module StdModule, block IrBlock, event SlabTrafficEvent, ids ValueMinter) returns SlabTrafficEvent
	return match event 'event'
		alloc(bytes) gives SlabTrafficEvent.alloc(emitBin(module, block: block, a: bytes, b: emitConst(module, block: block, value: boxPrefixBytes(module.debugStream), ids: ids), opcode: StdBinOpcode.sub, ids: ids))
		free gives SlabTrafficEvent.free
	end 'event'
end 'emitSlabTrackedEvent'

function emitSlabCountingCreditBothArms(module StdModule, arms SlabShardArms, event SlabTrafficEvent, counting SlabCounting, next IrBlock, ids ValueMinter)
	emitSlabCountingCredit(module, block: arms.sole.block, rowBase: arms.sole.trafficRow, event: event, atomic: false, counting: counting, ids: ids)
	emitSlabCountingCredit(module, block: arms.serial.block, rowBase: arms.serial.trafficRow, event: event, atomic: true, counting: counting, ids: ids)
	emitSlabArmsJoin(module, arms: arms, next: next)
end 'emitSlabCountingCreditBothArms'

function emitSlabArmsJoin(module StdModule, arms SlabShardArms, next IrBlock)
	module.setTerminator(arms.sole.block, op: StdOp.branch(next.id))
	module.setTerminator(arms.serial.block, op: StdOp.branch(next.id))
end 'emitSlabArmsJoin'

let SlabOsDirectFreeName = b"__slab_os_direct_free"

// The tier file holding those two bodies, for the declared reach edge. Stated beside the names it covers so
// a reader of either finds the other; `SlabArena.SlabArenaTierFileName` is the same statement one layer down.
export let SlabRuntimeTierFileName = "SlabRuntime.maxon"

// ⭐ **THE SCAVENGER'S PUBLIC DOOR.** `__Builtins.scavengeMemory()` lowers straight to it and reads its
// answer — the bytes this call handed back to the OS. Exported because `RuntimeUsage` matches the callee to
// decide whether the program carries the allocator at all: a program whose ONLY heap contact is this call
// still needs every layer under it.
export let SlabScavengeName = b"__slab_scavenge"

// `__slab_remote_free_count() -> i64` — how many slots have been freed onto a span another processor holds
// cached, summed over every P. `__Builtins.slabRemoteFreeCount()` lowers straight to it, and it is the
// only way a program can observe that the cross-processor free road was TAKEN: a regression routing every
// free down the local arm keeps the suite green otherwise.
//
// ⚠ It wears the `__slab_` prefix but its body is built by `SchedRuntime.installSchedRuntime`. The NUMBER is
// the allocator's, so the name is; the STORAGE is a per-P word (`SchedRuntime.POffRemoteFreeCount`, which
// carries why it is not a `.data` counter and which side is credited) and the walk that sums it is
// `buildSchedPerProcSum`, shared with the scheduler's own two counters. A third copy of that loop in
// `installSlabRuntime` to keep a prefix tidy would be one fact written twice.
//
// ⚠ **AND THAT IS WHY `RuntimeUsage.recordCallUsage` ROUTES IT TO `usesGt` AND NOT TO `usesHeap`**, unlike
// every other name in this band: the question is answered by walking `__sched_procs`, an array only
// `__sched_init_procs` builds, so asking it IS asking for the scheduler. It has no bit of its own for
// `SlabScavengeName`'s reason — `DeadFunctionElimination` drops the body from every program that never calls
// it.
export let SlabRemoteFreeCountName = b"__slab_remote_free_count"

// The five census doors, on the family's argumentless-integer shape. What each counts:
//
//   * `live` — slots handed out and not yet given back by any road.
//   * `free` — slots on some span's free list, plus the slots queued against it for a collector that has
//     not run. `live + free` is every slot of every live span.
//   * `cachedFree` — the subset of `free` an mcache row can still reach.
//   * `parked` — whole spans on a class's list with no live slot: the backlog only `__slab_scavenge`
//     returns to the arena.
//   * `committed` — arena granules backed by physical memory, plus the OS-direct road's live mappings.
//
// `runtime/SlabRuntime.maxon` owns the walks; what is built here is the lock around each.
// `specs/slab-census.md` owns the contract.
export let SlabLiveBytesName = b"__slab_live_bytes"
export let SlabFreeBytesName = b"__slab_free_bytes"
export let SlabCachedFreeBytesName = b"__slab_cached_free_bytes"
export let SlabParkedBytesName = b"__slab_parked_bytes"
export let SlabCommittedBytesName = b"__slab_committed_bytes"

export let SlabCensusTallyName = b"__slab_census_tally"
export let SlabCensusBucketCountName = b"__slab_census_count"
export let SlabCensusBucketBytesName = b"__slab_census_bytes"

// `runtime/SlabRuntime.maxon` holds the other copy of every kind below. A drift swaps two figures over,
// which no run would notice.
let SlabCensusName = b"__slab_census"

// A locked entry point and the tier body it calls are named separately. The tier file is INPUT to the
// build running now, so `callee` must name a body that file already carries — a tier body and the
// emitter constant naming it cannot be respelled by one build.
let SlabCensusTallyWalkName = b"__slab_census_tally_walk"
let SlabCensusBucketCountWalkName = b"__slab_census_bucket_count"
let SlabCensusBucketBytesWalkName = b"__slab_census_bucket_bytes"

let SlabRuntimeCallPrefix = b"__slab_"

// One membership, so `RuntimeUsage.recordCallUsage` reads a roster this file owns rather than keeping a
// second list free to drift from it.
function isSlabCensusCallee(callee ByteArray) returns bool
	if not bytesStartWith(callee, prefix: SlabRuntimeCallPrefix) 'notTheAllocatorsBand'
		return false
	end 'notTheAllocatorsBand'

	return callee.equals(SlabLiveBytesName) or callee.equals(SlabFreeBytesName) or callee.equals(SlabCachedFreeBytesName) or callee.equals(SlabParkedBytesName) or callee.equals(SlabCommittedBytesName) or callee.equals(SlabCensusTallyName) or callee.equals(SlabCensusBucketCountName) or callee.equals(SlabCensusBucketBytesName)
end 'isSlabCensusCallee'

let SlabCensusLiveBytes = 0
let SlabCensusFreeBytes = 1
let SlabCensusParkedBytes = 2
let SlabCensusCachedFreeBytes = 3
let SlabCensusCommittedBytes = 4

export let SlabCensusTagMode = 0
export let SlabCensusClassMode = 1

export let SlabCensusTagBuckets = 2048
let SlabCensusBucketWords = 2
let SlabCensusSlotAlignBytes = 8
let SlabCensusSlotAlignShift = 3
let SlabCensusSpanMarkBytes = 10240
export let SlabCensusImplausibleBucket = SlabCensusTagBuckets - 2
export let SlabCensusOverflowBucket = SlabCensusTagBuckets - 1

// --- The OS-direct tier ---
//
// ⛔ **ITS TWO BODIES ARE `runtime/SlabRuntime.maxon` SOURCE.** What stays here is the SHAPE they and
// `__slab_rounded_size` share — the boundary above which a request is its own mapping, and the prefix
// width both sides address the mapping through.
//
// ⭐⭐ **ABOVE `SlabMaxSmallSize` A REQUEST IS ITS OWN MAPPING, AND ITS SIZE RIDES IN A 16-BYTE PREFIX.**
// A free has to know how many bytes to hand back, and the pointer→span reverse map cannot answer: a map
// MISS *is* the OS-direct sentinel (`SlabArena.ArenaMapNoSpan`), so registering these pages would destroy
// the very signal that identifies them.
//
// ⛔ **A FIXED-SIZE TRACKING ARRAY IS NOT AN OPTION — IT HAS A CEILING.** `__mm_alloc` routes every box
// above 32 KiB here, so a program holding more large buffers than the array has entries would abort in the
// ALLOCATOR for no reason of its own. A prefix word has no ceiling, frees in O(1), and costs 16 bytes on an
// allocation that is at least 32 KiB — under 0.05%.
//
// The prefix is 16 bytes rather than 8 so the pointer handed back keeps 16-byte alignment, which is what
// every record layout above this file assumes.
//
// ⚠ **THE TIER CARRIES THE OTHER COPY.** `runtime/SlabRuntime.maxon`'s two entry points reserve the
// prefix and address the two words inside it; the one reader left here is `__slab_rounded_size`, which
// subtracts the same width to report the bytes a caller may actually use. The pair must agree or that
// answer names more room than the mapping holds.
let OsDirectPrefixBytes = 16

// --- Installation ---

// Install the object layer. Gated by `usesHeap` at the call site (`afterPipeline`) exactly
// as `installMmRuntime` is, because this IS the allocator that file's boxes come from.
//
// ⚠ It takes `globalData` because it REGISTERS as well as emits: the three `.rdata` tables must be in the
// table before the backend lays the section out. They are registered here rather than at their reader for
// `installUnicodeCategoryRuntime`'s reason — a `rdataAddr` naming a label nothing registered is a link
// failure, so the register and the emit belong to one call.
//
// The bodies read no usage bit: every heap program carries the sharded allocator, and the one fork is the
// target's `MachineModel`, read once here and handed down.
export function installSlabRuntime(module StdModule, globalData GlobalDataTable, usage RuntimeUsage, target Target, signatures ProgramSignatures, tierPath FilePath)
	if not usage.usesHeap 'noAllocator'
		return
	end 'noAllocator'

	// This check sits BEHIND the gate where the arena's runs ahead of every compile, and the difference is
	// the gate rather than the check. `usesHeap` is set by every program that allocates a single box, so a
	// breakage still fails the next compile that allocates anything; and a program with no allocator carries
	// no size-class table, no reverse lookup and no span, so no geometry of theirs can be wrong for it. It
	// builds both reverse tables and walks the ladder, which is why it is worth asking who it protects.
	checkSlabRuntimeGeometry(signatures, tierPath: tierPath)

	_ = globalData.registerRdataConstantLabeled(SlabSizeToClass8Label, bytes: slabSizeToClass8Table(), kind: RdataPayloadKind.slabTable)
	_ = globalData.registerRdataConstantLabeled(SlabSizeToClass128Label, bytes: slabSizeToClass128Table(), kind: RdataPayloadKind.slabTable)
	_ = globalData.registerRdataConstantLabeled(SlabClassGeomLabel, bytes: slabClassGeometryTable(), kind: RdataPayloadKind.slabTable)

	let model = machineModel(target)
	let firstFuncIndex = module.funcCount()

	buildSlabCollectRemote(module)
	buildSlabRoundedSize(module)
	buildSlabRefill(module, model: model)
	buildSlabAbortDoor(module, name: SlabPastEndName, abort: RuntimeAbort.slabSpanExhaustedPastItsEnd)
	buildSlabAbortDoor(module, name: SlabEvictUnownedName, abort: RuntimeAbort.slabEvictOfUnownedSpan)

	for counting in SlabCounting.allCases 'eachAllocDoor'
		buildSlabAllocDoor(module, name: SlabDoors.of(counting).alloc, zeroed: true, counting: counting, model: model)
	end 'eachAllocDoor'

	buildSlabAllocDoor(module, name: SlabAllocRawName, zeroed: false, counting: SlabCounting.counted, model: model)
	guardSlabStateBase(module)

	for counting in SlabCounting.allCases 'eachOversizeDoor'
		buildSlabAllocOversize(module, counting: counting, model: model)
		buildSlabFreeOversize(module, counting: counting, model: model)
	end 'eachOversizeDoor'

	buildSlabSpanDestroy(module)
	buildSlabFreeToFull(module)
	buildSlabFreeToRaw(module)
	buildSlabScavenge(module, model: model)
	buildSlabFreeToSpan(module)

	for counting in SlabCounting.allCases 'eachFreeDoor'
		buildSlabFreeDoor(module, door: SlabFreeDoor.plain(counting), model: model)
	end 'eachFreeDoor'

	let boxDoor = slabFreeDoorOf(module, kind: SlabBufferKind.managedBox)

	if boxDoor.poisons() 'buildsTheBoxDoor'
		buildSlabFreeDoor(module, door: boxDoor, model: model)
	end 'buildsTheBoxDoor'

	buildSlabCensusEntry(module, name: SlabLiveBytesName, callee: SlabCensusName, argument: SlabCensusArgument.constant(SlabCensusLiveBytes), model: model)
	buildSlabCensusEntry(module, name: SlabFreeBytesName, callee: SlabCensusName, argument: SlabCensusArgument.constant(SlabCensusFreeBytes), model: model)
	buildSlabCensusEntry(module, name: SlabCachedFreeBytesName, callee: SlabCensusName, argument: SlabCensusArgument.constant(SlabCensusCachedFreeBytes), model: model)
	buildSlabCensusEntry(module, name: SlabParkedBytesName, callee: SlabCensusName, argument: SlabCensusArgument.constant(SlabCensusParkedBytes), model: model)
	buildSlabCensusEntry(module, name: SlabCommittedBytesName, callee: SlabCensusName, argument: SlabCensusArgument.constant(SlabCensusCommittedBytes), model: model)
	buildSlabCensusEntry(module, name: SlabCensusTallyName, callee: SlabCensusTallyWalkName, argument: SlabCensusArgument.forwarded, model: model)
	buildSlabCensusEntry(module, name: SlabCensusBucketCountName, callee: SlabCensusBucketCountWalkName, argument: SlabCensusArgument.forwarded, model: model)
	buildSlabCensusEntry(module, name: SlabCensusBucketBytesName, callee: SlabCensusBucketBytesWalkName, argument: SlabCensusArgument.forwarded, model: model)

	assertSpanOwnerAccessIsOrdered(module, firstFuncIndex: firstFuncIndex)
end 'installSlabRuntime'

// In an untraced build `__mm_alloc` and `__mm_free` carry the tracked doors' hit path, so those doors are
// built once, as their full bodies under their own names. Every other door is a leaf under its own name in
// front of its full body, `<door>_slow`.
function slabBoxDoorsAreInlined(module StdModule) returns bool
	return not module.debugStream
end 'slabBoxDoorsAreInlined'

function buildSlabAllocDoor(module StdModule, name ByteArray, zeroed bool, counting SlabCounting, model MachineModel)
	if counting == SlabCounting.tracked and slabBoxDoorsAreInlined(module) 'inlinedIntoTheBoxAlloc'
		buildSlabAllocEntry(module, name: name, zeroed: zeroed, counting: counting, model: model)

		return
	end 'inlinedIntoTheBoxAlloc'

	buildSlabAllocFast(module, name: name, zeroed: zeroed, counting: counting, model: model)
	buildSlabAllocEntry(module, name: slabSlowDoorName(name), zeroed: zeroed, counting: counting, model: model)
end 'buildSlabAllocDoor'

function buildSlabFreeDoor(module StdModule, door SlabFreeDoor, model MachineModel)
	if door.counting() == SlabCounting.tracked and slabBoxDoorsAreInlined(module) 'inlinedIntoTheBoxFree'
		buildSlabFree(module, door: door, name: door.name(), model: model)

		return
	end 'inlinedIntoTheBoxFree'

	buildSlabFreeFast(module, door: door, model: model)
	buildSlabFree(module, door: door, name: slabSlowDoorName(door.name()), model: model)
end 'buildSlabFreeDoor'

// The one `.data` slot the object layer owns. `runtime/SlabRuntime.maxon`'s `__slab_state_base` is its only
// reader, so it is laid out exactly where that body survives (`DataReach.walked`). Its one write is the
// process's first allocation, which precedes any scheduler, so it is sealed before a worker M can
// exist.
export function slabRuntimeGlobals() returns DataSectionEntryArray
	let out = DataSectionEntryArray.create(0)
	pushRuntimeGlobal(out, label: SlabStateLabel, sharing: MultiMSharing.sealedBeforeWorkers, reach: DataReach.walked)
	return out
end 'slabRuntimeGlobals'

// ⭐⭐ **THE RECORD LAYOUT IS INTERNALLY COHERENT — asserted, not trusted.** `checkSlabClassTable` and
// `checkSlabArenaGeometry` make the same argument for the ladder and the arena, and state it in full: a
// table whose source changed shape produces a working-LOOKING allocator that indexes past its own bounds.
// It panics rather than returning a verdict, for those checks' reason — a broken layout cannot produce a
// working allocator, so no caller could sensibly continue.
function checkSlabRuntimeGeometry(signatures ProgramSignatures, tierPath FilePath)
	// Reading the ladder here is what makes the derived strides below statements about THIS table rather
	// than about whatever table happened to be generated when they were written.
	checkSlabClassTable()

	if (1 shl SlabSmallSizeDivShift) != SlabSmallSizeDiv 'smallDivShiftDisagrees'
		panic("slab runtime: 1 shl {SlabSmallSizeDivShift} is {1 shl SlabSmallSizeDivShift}, not the small-table divisor {SlabSmallSizeDiv}")
	end 'smallDivShiftDisagrees'

	if (1 shl SlabLargeSizeDivShift) != SlabLargeSizeDiv 'largeDivShiftDisagrees'
		panic("slab runtime: 1 shl {SlabLargeSizeDivShift} is {1 shl SlabLargeSizeDivShift}, not the large-table divisor {SlabLargeSizeDiv}")
	end 'largeDivShiftDisagrees'

	// The mspan record must fit its metadata slot, or successive headers overlap.
	//
	// ⚠ `MspanRecordBytes` is stated at the layout, beside the field that determines it, so appending
	// a field moves it; a check reading one field's offset instead goes on passing for a record
	// outgrowing its slot.
	if MspanRecordBytes > MspanSlotBytes 'recordOverflowsSlot'
		panic("slab runtime: an mspan record is {MspanRecordBytes} bytes and its metadata slot is {MspanSlotBytes}")
	end 'recordOverflowsSlot'

	// The metadata slab hands slots out of ONE chunk at a time, so a slot larger than a chunk could never be
	// served and the bump would loop forever asking for fresh chunks.
	if MspanSlotBytes > SlabChunkBytes 'slotOverflowsChunk'
		panic("slab runtime: an mspan slot is {MspanSlotBytes} bytes and a metadata chunk is {SlabChunkBytes}")
	end 'slotOverflowsChunk'

	// The packed geometry word must actually hold both fields. `SlabMaxSmallSize` is the widest slot size
	// and `SlabChunkBytes / SlabSmallSizeDiv` bounds the object count (the smallest class in a one-page span).
	if SlabClassGeomSlotShift != SlabClassGeomHalfBits or SlabClassGeomSlotMask != (1 shl SlabClassGeomHalfBits) - 1 'halvesAreNotWords'
		panic("slab runtime: the packed geometry word splits at bit {SlabClassGeomSlotShift} with low mask {SlabClassGeomSlotMask}, but the pop reads its two fields as {SlabClassGeomHalfBits}-bit loads")
	end 'halvesAreNotWords'

	if SlabMaxSmallSize > SlabClassGeomSlotMask 'slotSizeOverflowsField'
		panic("slab runtime: a slot size of {SlabMaxSmallSize} does not fit the packed geometry's low field (mask {SlabClassGeomSlotMask})")
	end 'slotSizeOverflowsField'

	// The state region's own arithmetic: the two arrays must not overlap, and the whole must fit the chunk
	// run committed for it.
	if slabStateMcacheOffset() <= (SlabStateMcentralOffset as FullRangeInt) 'arraysOverlap'
		panic("slab runtime: the mcache starts at {slabStateMcacheOffset()}, not past the mcentral heads at {SlabStateMcentralOffset}")
	end 'arraysOverlap'

	if slabStateBytes() > slabStateChunks() * SlabChunkBytes 'stateOverflowsChunks'
		panic("slab runtime: {slabStateBytes()} bytes of allocator state do not fit {slabStateChunks()} chunk(s) of {SlabChunkBytes}")
	end 'stateOverflowsChunks'

	let widestSpanChunks = slabWidestSpanChunks()

	requireMarkTableCoversTheWidestSpan(widestSpanChunks)

	requireTierGeometryAgrees(slabTierPins(), signatures: signatures, tierPath: tierPath, emitterFile: "Compiler/Runtime/SlabRuntime.maxon", layer: "slab runtime")

	// ⭐⭐ **AND THE TWO REQUESTS THIS FILE MAKES OF THE PAGE LAYER MUST BE SATISFIABLE ON BOTH LANES.**
	// `slabStateChunks()` is 19 chunks and the non-reserving arena has 63
	// allocatable ones; nothing asserted the pair, so raising `SlabMaxShards` or regenerating a longer class
	// ladder would abort **at run time, on wasm only, in a build whose native twin is perfect**, with
	// `slabChunkRunUnsatisfiable` — a true statement about the arena and a baffling one about the change that
	// caused it. It is asked of BOTH lanes for `checkArenaGeometryForLane`'s stated reason: a rule that only
	// holds for whichever target happens to be compiling is the check that never runs.
	requireArenaCanServe("the allocator's state region", chunks: slabStateChunks())

	// The other request is a SPAN's chunk run, whose largest is the widest class's page count — Go's table
	// tops out at 10. `__slab_refill` computes it as `ceil(slotSize · totalSlots / SlabChunkBytes)` at run
	// time; the bound is what says every value that formula can produce is one the arena will serve.
	requireArenaCanServe("the widest class's span", chunks: widestSpanChunks)
end 'checkSlabRuntimeGeometry'

function requireMarkTableCoversTheWidestSpan(widestSpanChunks FullRangeInt)
	if (1 shl SlabCensusSlotAlignShift) != SlabCensusSlotAlignBytes 'alignShiftDisagrees'
		panic("slab runtime: 1 shl {SlabCensusSlotAlignShift} is {1 shl SlabCensusSlotAlignShift}, not the slot alignment {SlabCensusSlotAlignBytes}")
	end 'alignShiftDisagrees'

	let widestSpanBytes = widestSpanChunks * SlabChunkBytes
	let needed = widestSpanBytes / SlabCensusSlotAlignBytes

	if needed > SlabCensusSpanMarkBytes 'markTableTooNarrow'
		panic("slab runtime: the widest class's span is {widestSpanBytes} bytes, which needs {needed} mark byte(s) at one per {SlabCensusSlotAlignBytes} bytes of span, and the tally's per-span mark table is {SlabCensusSpanMarkBytes}")
	end 'markTableTooNarrow'
end 'requireMarkTableCoversTheWidestSpan'

function slabTierPins() returns TierGeometryPinArray
	let out = TierGeometryPinArray.create(0)

	out.push(TierGeometryPin.create("the chunk granularity", here: SlabChunkBytes, named: "SlabPageBytes"))
	out.push(TierGeometryPin.create("an OS-direct mapping's prefix", here: OsDirectPrefixBytes, named: "OsDirectPrefixBytes"))
	out.push(TierGeometryPin.create("the exit code for a pointer the OS-direct road never handed out", here: RuntimeAbort.slabOsDirectPointerNotOurs.rawValue, named: "SlabOsDirectPointerNotOursExit"))
	out.push(TierGeometryPin.create("the exit code for a slab-state read before the first allocation committed it", here: RuntimeAbort.slabStateReadBeforeCommit.rawValue, named: "SlabStateReadBeforeCommitExit"))

	out.push(TierGeometryPin.create("the state region's word stride", here: SlabRecordWordBytes as FullRangeInt, named: "SlabWordBytes"))
	out.push(TierGeometryPin.create("the metadata slab's bump cursor", here: SlabStateMetaBumpOffset as FullRangeInt, named: "SlabStateMetaBumpOffset"))
	out.push(TierGeometryPin.create("the metadata slab's limit", here: SlabStateMetaEndOffset as FullRangeInt, named: "SlabStateMetaEndOffset"))
	out.push(TierGeometryPin.create("the metadata slab's free list", here: SlabStateMetaFreeOffset as FullRangeInt, named: "SlabStateMetaFreeOffset"))
	out.push(TierGeometryPin.create("the scheduler word", here: SlabStateSchedulerOffset as FullRangeInt, named: "SlabStateSchedulerOffset"))
	out.push(TierGeometryPin.create("the lock-depth sink", here: SlabStateLockDepthSinkOffset as FullRangeInt, named: "SlabStateLockDepthSinkOffset"))
	out.push(TierGeometryPin.create("the slow-path lock", here: SlabStateLockOffset as FullRangeInt, named: "SlabStateLockOffset"))
	out.push(TierGeometryPin.create("the slow-path lock's width", here: SlabStateLockBytes, named: "SlabStateLockBytes"))

	out.push(TierGeometryPin.create("the size-class count", here: slabClassCount() as FullRangeInt, named: "SlabClassCount"))
	out.push(TierGeometryPin.create("the shard count", here: SlabMaxShards, named: "SlabMaxShards"))
	out.push(TierGeometryPin.create("a row's traffic columns", here: SlabTrafficColumn.allCases.count() as FullRangeInt, named: "SlabTrafficColumns"))

	out.push(TierGeometryPin.create("the mcentral heads", here: SlabStateMcentralOffset as FullRangeInt, named: "SlabStateMcentralOffset"))
	out.push(TierGeometryPin.create("the mcache rows", here: slabStateMcacheOffset(), named: "SlabStateMcacheOffset"))
	out.push(TierGeometryPin.create("the OS-direct census word", here: slabStateOsDirectBytesOffset(), named: "SlabStateOsDirectBytesOffset"))
	out.push(TierGeometryPin.create("the state region's size", here: slabStateBytes(), named: "SlabStateBytes"))
	out.push(TierGeometryPin.create("the state region's chunk run", here: slabStateChunks(), named: "SlabStateChunks"))
	out.push(TierGeometryPin.create("the uncommitted-state sentinel", here: SlabStateUncommitted, named: "SlabStateUncommitted"))
	out.push(TierGeometryPin.create("the census tag-bucket count", here: SlabCensusTagBuckets, named: "SlabCensusTagBuckets"))

	out.push(TierGeometryPin.create("an mspan's metadata slot", here: MspanSlotBytes, named: "MspanSlotBytes"))
	out.push(TierGeometryPin.create("an mspan's base address", here: MspanBaseAddrOffset as FullRangeInt, named: "MspanBaseAddrOffset"))
	out.push(TierGeometryPin.create("an mspan's free list", here: MspanFreeListOffset as FullRangeInt, named: "MspanFreeListOffset"))
	out.push(TierGeometryPin.create("an mspan's bump cursor", here: MspanBumpNextOffset as FullRangeInt, named: "MspanBumpNextOffset"))
	out.push(TierGeometryPin.create("an mspan's free count", here: MspanFreeCountOffset as FullRangeInt, named: "MspanFreeCountOffset"))
	out.push(TierGeometryPin.create("an mspan's class index", here: MspanClassIdxOffset as FullRangeInt, named: "MspanClassIdxOffset"))
	out.push(TierGeometryPin.create("an mspan's owner", here: MspanOwningPOffset as FullRangeInt, named: "MspanOwningPOffset"))
	out.push(TierGeometryPin.create("an mspan's remote free list", here: MspanRemoteHeadOffset as FullRangeInt, named: "MspanRemoteHeadOffset"))
	out.push(TierGeometryPin.create("an mspan's remote free count", here: MspanRemoteCountOffset as FullRangeInt, named: "MspanRemoteCountOffset"))
	out.push(TierGeometryPin.create("a destroyed span's owner sentinel", here: SpanOwnerState.freed.rawValue, named: "MspanOwningPFreed"))
	out.push(TierGeometryPin.create("a parked span's owner sentinel", here: SpanOwnerState.partial.rawValue, named: "MspanStatePartial"))

	out.push(TierGeometryPin.create("the packed geometry's slot-count shift", here: SlabClassGeomSlotShift, named: "SlabClassGeomSlotShift"))
	out.push(TierGeometryPin.create("the packed geometry's slot-size mask", here: SlabClassGeomSlotMask, named: "SlabClassGeomSlotMask"))

	out.push(TierGeometryPin.create("the census kind for live bytes", here: SlabCensusLiveBytes, named: "SlabCensusLiveBytes"))
	out.push(TierGeometryPin.create("the census kind for free bytes", here: SlabCensusFreeBytes, named: "SlabCensusFreeBytes"))
	out.push(TierGeometryPin.create("the census kind for parked bytes", here: SlabCensusParkedBytes, named: "SlabCensusParkedBytes"))
	out.push(TierGeometryPin.create("the census kind for cached free bytes", here: SlabCensusCachedFreeBytes, named: "SlabCensusCachedFreeBytes"))
	out.push(TierGeometryPin.create("the census kind for committed bytes", here: SlabCensusCommittedBytes, named: "SlabCensusCommittedBytes"))

	out.push(TierGeometryPin.create("the tally mode that attributes by tag", here: SlabCensusTagMode, named: "SlabCensusTagMode"))
	out.push(TierGeometryPin.create("the tally mode that attributes by size class", here: SlabCensusClassMode, named: "SlabCensusClassMode"))
	out.push(TierGeometryPin.create("the tally's bucket count", here: SlabCensusTagBuckets, named: "SlabCensusTagBuckets"))
	out.push(TierGeometryPin.create("a tally bucket's words", here: SlabCensusBucketWords, named: "SlabCensusBucketWords"))
	out.push(TierGeometryPin.create("the bucket a slot no packed id explains lands in", here: SlabCensusImplausibleBucket, named: "SlabCensusImplausibleBucket"))
	out.push(TierGeometryPin.create("the bucket a tag past the table lands in", here: SlabCensusOverflowBucket, named: "SlabCensusOverflowBucket"))
	out.push(TierGeometryPin.create("a packed id's tag shift", here: DsMmPackedIdShift, named: "SlabCensusTagShift"))
	out.push(TierGeometryPin.create("a packed id's tag mask", here: DsMmTagIndexMask, named: "SlabCensusTagMask"))
	out.push(TierGeometryPin.create("a slot boundary's alignment", here: SlabCensusSlotAlignBytes, named: "SlabCensusSlotAlignBytes"))
	out.push(TierGeometryPin.create("the shift that divides by it", here: SlabCensusSlotAlignShift, named: "SlabCensusSlotAlignShift"))
	out.push(TierGeometryPin.create("the per-span free-set mark table", here: SlabCensusSpanMarkBytes, named: "SlabCensusSpanMarkBytes"))
	out.push(TierGeometryPin.create("the tally's histogram", here: slabStateCensusTableOffset(), named: "SlabStateCensusTableOffset"))
	out.push(TierGeometryPin.create("the per-span free-set mark table's place", here: slabStateCensusMarksOffset(), named: "SlabStateCensusMarksOffset"))
	out.push(TierGeometryPin.create("the exit code for a tally that cannot be taken soundly", here: RuntimeAbort.slabCensusTallyUnsound.rawValue, named: "SlabCensusTallyUnsoundExit"))

	return out
end 'slabTierPins'

export let HeapStatsStdlibFileName = "HeapStats.maxon"

export function checkHeapStatsBuckets(signatures ProgramSignatures, stdlibPath FilePath)
	let pins = TierGeometryPinArray.create(0)
	pins.push(TierGeometryPin.create("the census tag-bucket count", here: SlabCensusTagBuckets, named: "HeapBucketCount"))
	requireTierGeometryAgrees(pins, signatures: signatures, tierPath: stdlibPath, emitterFile: "Compiler/Runtime/SlabRuntime.maxon", layer: "checkHeapStatsBuckets")
end 'checkHeapStatsBuckets'

// A chunk run this file will ask `__slab_arena_alloc_chunks` for, checked against what an arena can serve on
// each lane. One helper rather than two pairs of `if`s, so a third request cannot arrive checked against one
// geometry only.
function requireArenaCanServe(what String, chunks FullRangeInt)
	requireArenaCanServeOnLane(what, chunks: chunks, hasPageReservation: true)
	requireArenaCanServeOnLane(what, chunks: chunks, hasPageReservation: false)
end 'requireArenaCanServe'

function requireArenaCanServeOnLane(what String, chunks FullRangeInt, hasPageReservation bool)
	let available = slabArenaAllocatableChunks(hasPageReservation)

	if chunks > available 'unsatisfiable'
		panic("slab runtime: {what} needs {chunks} chunk(s) and an arena on {arenaLaneLabel(hasPageReservation)} has {available} after its metadata, so `__slab_arena_alloc_chunks` would abort at run time")
	end 'unsatisfiable'
end 'requireArenaCanServeOnLane'

// Which lane a bound above is talking about. The two produce the same sentence with different numbers, and
// "18 against 63" is only readable once the reader is told which arena was expected — `SlabArena`'s own
// `arenaLaneName` makes the identical argument about its identical pair of panics.
function arenaLaneLabel(hasPageReservation bool) returns String
	if hasPageReservation 'reserves'
		return "a reserving lane"
	end 'reserves'

	return "the non-reserving lane"
end 'arenaLaneLabel'

// One mcache row: one span pointer per class. DERIVED from the class count rather than written as a
// number a regenerated ladder invalidates in silence.
function slabMcacheRowStride() returns FullRangeInt
	return ((slabClassCount() as ByteOffset) * SlabRecordWordBytes) as FullRangeInt
end 'slabMcacheRowStride'

function slabStateMcacheOffset() returns FullRangeInt
	return (SlabStateMcentralOffset + (slabClassCount() as ByteOffset) * SlabRecordWordBytes) as FullRangeInt
end 'slabStateMcacheOffset'

// One row of traffic columns, one word per `SlabTrafficColumn`.
function slabTrafficRowBytes() returns FullRangeInt
	return ((SlabTrafficColumn.allCases.count() as ByteOffset) * SlabRecordWordBytes) as FullRangeInt
end 'slabTrafficRowBytes'

function slabStateTrafficOffset() returns FullRangeInt
	return slabStateMcacheOffset() + SlabMaxShards * slabMcacheRowStride()
end 'slabStateTrafficOffset'

// Nothing here reads the word. The derivation exists so `slabStateBytes()` covers it and
// `checkSlabRuntimeGeometry` can pin it; `runtime/SlabRuntime.maxon` owns the step, the read, and the rule
// that keeps a word added to this region past the last array rather than in the head.
function slabStateOsDirectBytesOffset() returns FullRangeInt
	return slabStateTrafficOffset() + SlabMaxShards * slabTrafficRowBytes()
end 'slabStateOsDirectBytesOffset'

function slabCensusBucketStride() returns FullRangeInt
	return ((SlabCensusBucketWords as ByteOffset) * SlabRecordWordBytes) as FullRangeInt
end 'slabCensusBucketStride'

function slabStateCensusTableOffset() returns FullRangeInt
	return slabStateOsDirectBytesOffset() + (SlabRecordWordBytes as FullRangeInt)
end 'slabStateCensusTableOffset'

function slabStateCensusMarksOffset() returns FullRangeInt
	return slabStateCensusTableOffset() + SlabCensusTagBuckets * slabCensusBucketStride()
end 'slabStateCensusMarksOffset'

// Moving the state region's size takes a STAGED build: `slabTierPins` compares these figures with the
// tier file's restated copies, so the building compiler refuses a tree where the two disagree and no
// single build can change both. `maxon-bin/AGENTS.md` owns the sequence.
function slabStateBytes() returns FullRangeInt
	return slabStateCensusMarksOffset() + SlabCensusSpanMarkBytes
end 'slabStateBytes'

function slabStateChunks() returns FullRangeInt
	return (slabStateBytes() + SlabChunkBytes - 1) / SlabChunkBytes
end 'slabStateChunks'

// --- Shared emitters ---

// ⭐⭐ **THE ONE PLACE THE SMALL/LARGE BOUNDARY IS SPELLED.** A sizing function that mirrored the
// allocator's two routing arms would be one fact written twice, and the failure mode is a size report
// naming a class for a request the allocator sends to the OS. Both callers branch through here, so they
// cannot disagree about where the boundary is.
//
// ⛔ **THE PAGE ROUNDING ON THE OS-DIRECT ARM IS THE OTHER HALF OF THE SAME AGREEMENT AND IT IS NO LONGER
// ONE SPELLING.** `__slab_rounded_size` reaches `MmRuntime.emitAlignUp` naming `SlabChunkBytes`;
// `__slab_os_direct_alloc` is tier source and rounds with `runtime/SlabRuntime.maxon`'s own restated pair.
// A drift between them reports a usable byte count larger than the mapping reserved.
function emitSlabSizeRouting(module StdModule, block IrBlock, size ValueId, osDirect IrBlock, small IrBlock, ids ValueMinter)
	emitBranchOnCompare(module, block: block, value: size, pred: StdCmpPred.gt, against: SlabMaxSmallSize, whenTrue: osDirect, whenFalse: small, ids: ids)
end 'emitSlabSizeRouting'

// The packed geometry word for `classIdx` — ONE indexed `.rdata` load, which is the whole of what storing
// `class_idx` alone costs. See `SlabClassGeomSlotShift`.
function emitClassGeometry(module StdModule, block IrBlock, classIdx ValueId, ids ValueMinter) returns ValueId
	let base = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(block, op: StdOp.rdataAddr(base, label: SlabClassGeomLabelBytes))
	return emitWordSlotLoad(module, block: block, base: base, index: classIdx, ids: ids)
end 'emitClassGeometry'

function emitSpanGeometry(module StdModule, block IrBlock, span ValueId, ids ValueMinter) returns ValueId
	let classIdx = emitLoadField(module, block: block, record: span, offset: MspanClassIdxOffset, ids: ids)

	return emitClassGeometry(module, block: block, classIdx: classIdx, ids: ids)
end 'emitSpanGeometry'

function emitGeomSlotSize(module StdModule, block IrBlock, geom ValueId, ids ValueMinter) returns ValueId
	let mask = emitConst(module, block: block, value: SlabClassGeomSlotMask, ids: ids)
	return emitBin(module, block: block, a: geom, b: mask, opcode: StdBinOpcode.bitAnd, ids: ids)
end 'emitGeomSlotSize'

function emitGeomTotalSlots(module StdModule, block IrBlock, geom ValueId, ids ValueMinter) returns ValueId
	let shift = emitConst(module, block: block, value: SlabClassGeomSlotShift, ids: ids)
	return emitBin(module, block: block, a: geom, b: shift, opcode: StdBinOpcode.shr, ids: ids)
end 'emitGeomTotalSlots'

// The address of `state`'s word-indexed slot at `arrayOffset + index * 8` — the mcentral head for a class,
// and (with the row folded into `index`) an mcache entry. Both arrays are word-indexed off one base, so the
// arithmetic is written once and the callers differ only in what they index by.
function emitStateSlotAddr(module StdModule, block IrBlock, state ValueId, arrayOffset FullRangeInt, index ValueId, ids ValueMinter) returns ValueId
	let arrayBase = emitConst(module, block: block, value: arrayOffset, ids: ids)
	let fromBase = emitBin(module, block: block, a: state, b: arrayBase, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	return emitWordSlotAddr(module, block: block, base: fromBase, index: index, ids: ids)
end 'emitStateSlotAddr'

// `ceil(slotSize * totalSlots / SlabChunkBytes)` — the chunks a span's slot region occupies.
//
// ⭐ **ONE SPELLING, BECAUSE THE CUT AND THE DESTRUCTION MUST AGREE TO THE CHUNK.** `__slab_refill` computes
// it to ask the arena for a run and to register that many pages in the reverse map; `__slab_span_destroy`
// computes it to hand the same run back and to unregister the same pages. Spelled twice, a span could be
// released one chunk short — leaving a chunk claimed forever — or one chunk long, releasing a chunk that
// belongs to its neighbour.
function emitSpanChunkCount(module StdModule, block IrBlock, slotSize ValueId, totalSlots ValueId, ids ValueMinter) returns ValueId
	let dataBytes = emitBin(module, block: block, a: slotSize, b: totalSlots, opcode: StdBinOpcode.mul, ids: ids)
	let pageBump = emitConst(module, block: block, value: SlabChunkBytes - 1, ids: ids)
	let raised = emitBin(module, block: block, a: dataBytes, b: pageBump, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	let pageShift = emitConst(module, block: block, value: SlabPageShift, ids: ids)
	return emitBin(module, block: block, a: raised, b: pageShift, opcode: StdBinOpcode.shr, ids: ids)
end 'emitSpanChunkCount'

// The base of row `row`'s mcache entries. `row * stride` is a MULTIPLY rather than a shift because the
// stride is `classCount * 8` and the class count is Go's 68, which is not a power of two.
//
// ⛔⛔ **A CONSTANT ROW IS FOLDED HERE OR NOT AT ALL**, for `emitSlabTrafficRowAt`'s reason: no Std pass
// runs over a body an installer mints, so a `mul` of two `const`s reaches instruction selection as a real
// `imul` — and this one is on the path of every allocation that pops a slot.
function emitMcacheRowAt(module StdModule, block IrBlock, state ValueId, row FullRangeInt, ids ValueMinter) returns ValueId
	return emitFieldAddr(module, block: block, record: state, offset: (row * slabMcacheRowStride()) as ByteOffset, ids: ids)
end 'emitMcacheRowAt'

function emitMcacheRowBase(module StdModule, block IrBlock, state ValueId, row ValueId, ids ValueMinter) returns ValueId
	let stride = emitConst(module, block: block, value: slabMcacheRowStride(), ids: ids)
	let rowOffset = emitBin(module, block: block, a: row, b: stride, opcode: StdBinOpcode.mul, ids: ids)
	return emitBin(module, block: block, a: state, b: rowOffset, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
end 'emitMcacheRowBase'

// The entry `mcache[row][classIdx]`, off the row base the shard walk handed down.
function emitMcacheSlotAddr(module StdModule, block IrBlock, mcacheRow ValueId, classIdx ValueId, ids ValueMinter) returns ValueId
	return emitStateSlotAddr(module, block: block, state: mcacheRow, arrayOffset: slabStateMcacheOffset(), index: classIdx, ids: ids)
end 'emitMcacheSlotAddr'

// --- The sharding emitters ---
//
// ⭐ **THESE KEEP "WHICH MACHINE, PROCESSOR AND ROW AM I", "STEP MY TRAFFIC COLUMNS", "TAKE THE LOCK",
// "PUSH ONTO A SPAN" AND "PUSH ONTO A QUEUE" SPELLED ONCE EACH.** An ownership gate written out twice inside
// one function is the shape a shared emitter exists to make impossible.

// The scheduler word (`SlabStateSchedulerOffset`). A `singleMachine` target never publishes one, so the
// read is a constant there and the TLS walk behind it is never emitted — the backend has no `tlsSlotLoad`.
function emitSlabSchedulerWord(module StdModule, block IrBlock, state ValueId, model MachineModel, ids ValueMinter) returns ValueId
	return match model 'model'
		singleMachine gives emitConst(module, block: block, value: 0, ids: ids)
		multiMachine gives emitLoadField(module, block: block, record: state, offset: SlabStateSchedulerOffset, ids: ids)
	end 'model'
end 'emitSlabSchedulerWord'

// *"May another OS thread be inside the allocator right now?"* — the scheduler word is monotonic, so a
// non-zero read is permanent. It gates the lock on the raw row's two fast-path arms.
function emitSlabSchedulerExists(module StdModule, block IrBlock, schedulerWord ValueId, ids ValueMinter) returns ValueId
	return emitCompareWithConstant(module, block: block, value: schedulerWord, pred: StdCmpPred.ne, against: 0, ids: ids)
end 'emitSlabSchedulerExists'

// ⭐ **THE SCHEDULER WORD'S SINGLE WRITER — `__sched_init_procs`, beside its store of the same offset into
// `__sched_tls_teb_offset`.** The region is committed through `__slab_state_base` rather than assumed,
// because this runs once and the call is the one spelling of the commit.
function emitSlabPublishScheduler(module StdModule, block IrBlock, tebOffset ValueId, ids ValueMinter)
	let state = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(block, op: StdOp.call(state, callee: SlabStateName, args: ValueIdArray.create(0)))
	emitStoreField(module, block: block, record: state, offset: SlabStateSchedulerOffset, value: tebOffset)
end 'emitSlabPublishScheduler'

// A block a walk bails to, with the arguments its edge carries.
type SlabBailEdge
	export var block as IrBlock
	export let args as ValueIdArray

	static function create(block IrBlock, args ValueIdArray) returns Self
		return Self{block: block, args: args}
	end 'create'
end 'SlabBailEdge'

// ⭐⭐ **THE ONE WALK FROM THE SCHEDULER WORD TO A USABLE MACHINE — the word test, the slot load, the machine
// test — written once for every reader of the slab's state head.** It answers the M, live and non-null in
// `haveMachine`, and takes `noScheduler` when the word is 0 and `noMachine` when the slot is: the two are
// different facts (no thread can exist / this thread is not a machine) and the allocator sends them down
// different arms. ⛔ Neither test is defensive: before the word is published `gs:[0]` is a NON-NULL
// pointer, and a zero slot is a real thread with no machine. Only for a `multiMachine` target — a
// `singleMachine` caller never reaches a TLS op and branches to its bail directly.
function emitSlabMachineOrBail(module StdModule, func IrFunction, cur IrBlock, schedulerWord ValueId, haveMachine IrBlock, noScheduler SlabBailEdge, noMachine SlabBailEdge, ids ValueMinter, label ByteArray, heat BlockHeat = BlockHeat.normal) returns ValueId
	let haveScheduler = module.addBlock(func, label: label, heat: heat)

	// Not `emitSlabSchedulerExists` with its arms swapped: that form lowers to a `setcc` rather than
	// a `jcc`, and moves the `allocator-shape-is-one-in-a-program-with-no-scheduler` pin in
	// `ir-specs/emitted-runtime-body.md` on every lane.
	emitBranchOnCompare(module, block: cur, value: schedulerWord, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, against: 0, whenTrue: noScheduler.block, whenFalse: haveScheduler, ids: ids)
	pushBranchEdge(cur, to: noScheduler.block, values: noScheduler.args.clone())

	let m = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(haveScheduler, op: StdOp.tlsSlotLoad(m, tebOffsetId: schedulerWord))
	emitBranchOnCompare(module, block: haveScheduler, value: m, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, against: 0, whenTrue: noMachine.block, whenFalse: haveMachine, ids: ids)
	pushBranchEdge(haveScheduler, to: noMachine.block, values: noMachine.args.clone())

	return m
end 'emitSlabMachineOrBail'

// ⭐ **THE GREEN THREAD A MACHINE'S ALLOCATIONS BELONG TO — the running GT's OWNER, live in `haveOwner`.**
// The owner and not the running GT, because a coroutine is its owner's work: an `async` frame is a member
// of its owner's strand, so billing it to the coroutine would put a bracket's own allocations outside the
// bracket. A machine with no running GT — its own bring-up, the system monitor for its whole life — takes
// `noThread`.
function emitSlabOwnerThreadOrBail(module StdModule, cur IrBlock, m ValueId, haveOwner IrBlock, noThread SlabBailEdge, ids ValueMinter) returns ValueId
	let gt = emitLoadField(module, block: cur, record: m, offset: MOffCurrentGt, ids: ids)
	emitBranchOnCompare(module, block: cur, value: gt, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, against: 0, whenTrue: noThread.block, whenFalse: haveOwner, ids: ids)
	pushBranchEdge(cur, to: noThread.block, values: noThread.args.clone())

	return emitLoadField(module, block: haveOwner, record: gt, offset: GtOffOwner, ids: ids)
end 'emitSlabOwnerThreadOrBail'

// What the calling green thread's own traffic columns should record for this event, or nothing — the
// per-green-thread columns (`GtRuntime.GtOffThreadAllocs`) are stepped at the slab doors only, once per
// request, and a scaffold door passes `none`.
union ThreadTraffic
	none
	alloc(bytes ValueId)
	free
end 'ThreadTraffic'

// ⭐⭐ **WHAT A DOOR ASKS ITS ARMS TO CARRY BEYOND THE TRAFFIC ROW.** Every value an arm carries is
// materialised in EVERY predecessor of that arm and stays live until its last reader, so a door asks for
// what it reads and nothing else. The oversize doors credit their row and stop there; `__slab_free` routes on the
// P it holds and derives its lock where it takes it; `__slab_alloc` also pops from that row's mcache and
// carries the lock depth across the pop. It is a build-time choice of the CALL SITE, one literal per entry
// point, and no program varies it.
enum SlabShardNeed
	creditsOnly
	holdsProc
	routed
end 'SlabShardNeed'

// What a door that pops reads off its arm: the P it holds (or `SlabNoProcessor`), the base of that
// row's mcache entries, and the lock depth it owes a step down — the serialised arm's machine
// (`MOffLocks`) or the state region's sink, and `SlabNoLockDepth` on the sole-writer arm, which
// takes no lock at all.
//
// ⭐⭐ **THE MCACHE ROW IS AN ADDRESS AND NOT THE ROW INDEX**, for the reason the traffic row is: a row
// index joined from two predecessors is a register, so `row * classCount * 8` stays an `imul` on the path
// of every allocation. Built per predecessor, the constant-row edges fold to one displacement on `state`.
type SlabShardRoute
	export let p as ValueId
	export let mcacheRow as ValueId
	export let depthAddr as ValueId

	static function create(p ValueId, mcacheRow ValueId, depthAddr ValueId) returns Self
		return Self{p: p, mcacheRow: mcacheRow, depthAddr: depthAddr}
	end 'create'
end 'SlabShardRoute'

union SlabShardRouting
	credits
	proc(p ValueId)
	routes(route SlabShardRoute)
end 'SlabShardRouting'

// ⭐⭐ **ONE ARM OF THE SHARD WALK** — the block a thread lands in, the address of its row's traffic
// columns, and the routing values its door asked for.
//
// ⭐⭐ **THE TRAFFIC ROW IS AN ADDRESS AND NOT THE ROW INDEX**, because crediting the row is the one thing
// every door does in the arm itself: addressed in the predecessor, the edge whose row is a constant folds
// the multiply and both adds away, while a row index joined across edges keeps all three on the hot path.
type SlabShardSite
	export var block as IrBlock
	export let trafficRow as ValueId
	export let routing as SlabShardRouting

	static function create(block IrBlock, trafficRow ValueId, routing SlabShardRouting) returns Self
		return Self{block: block, trafficRow: trafficRow, routing: routing}
	end 'create'
end 'SlabShardSite'

// The routing an arm carries. A door reaching either of these asked for it at the one call site that
// chooses the need, so the narrower cases are unreachable rather than unhandled.
function slabShardRoute(site SlabShardSite) returns SlabShardRoute
	match site.routing 'routing'
		routes(route) then return route
		proc then panic("slabShardRoute: an arm built for `SlabShardNeed.holdsProc` carries no mcache row or lock depth — a door that pops asks for `routed`")
		credits then panic("slabShardRoute: an arm built for `SlabShardNeed.creditsOnly` carries no P, mcache row or lock depth — a door that reads one asks for `routed`")
	end 'routing'
end 'slabShardRoute'

// The P an arm carries, for a door that routes by owner and derives its lock where it takes it.
function slabShardProc(site SlabShardSite) returns ValueId
	match site.routing 'routing'
		routes(route) then return route.p
		proc(p) then return p
		credits then panic("slabShardProc: an arm built for `SlabShardNeed.creditsOnly` carries no P — a door that routes by owner asks for `holdsProc`")
	end 'routing'
end 'slabShardProc'

// ⭐⭐ **THE TWO ARMS EVERY DOOR CONVERGES ON — a row with a SOLE WRITER, or the shared raw row that must
// be SERIALISED.**
//
// ⭐⭐ **THE SERIALISED ARM'S ROW IS A CONSTANT OF THE ARM RATHER THAN AN ARGUMENT OF IT.** All three edges
// into it carry `SlabRawShardRow` — that is what "serialised" means here, a thread serialising exactly when
// the row it takes is the shared one — so both of that row's addresses are emitted inside the arm and no
// edge carries either.
type SlabShardArms
	export let schedulerWord as ValueId
	export var sole as SlabShardSite
	export var serial as SlabShardSite

	static function create(module StdModule, func IrFunction, state ValueId, schedulerWord ValueId, need SlabShardNeed, ids ValueMinter, label ByteArray, heat BlockHeat) returns Self
		let soleWriter = module.addBlock(func, label: label, heat: heat)
		let serialised = module.addBlock(func, label: label, heat: heat)

		let soleTrafficRow = ids.mint()
		soleWriter.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(soleTrafficRow, argType: StdType.i64))
		let soleRouting = pushSoleRouting(module, block: soleWriter, need: need, ids: ids)

		let serialRouting = pushSerialRouting(module, block: serialised, state: state, need: need, ids: ids)
		let serialTrafficRow = emitSlabTrafficRowAt(module, block: serialised, state: state, row: SlabRawShardRow, ids: ids)

		let sole = SlabShardSite.create(soleWriter, trafficRow: soleTrafficRow, routing: soleRouting)
		let serial = SlabShardSite.create(serialised, trafficRow: serialTrafficRow, routing: serialRouting)

		return Self{schedulerWord: schedulerWord, sole: sole, serial: serial}
	end 'create'
end 'SlabShardArms'

// The P an arm takes as a block argument, because every edge into either arm carries a different one.
function pushShardProc(block IrBlock, ids ValueMinter) returns ValueId
	let p = ids.mint()
	block.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(p, argType: StdType.i64))

	return p
end 'pushShardProc'

// The sole-writer arm's routing. Its mcache row differs per edge; its lock depth does NOT — a sole writer
// takes no lock, so the arm's own constant answers and no edge carries a fourth value.
function pushSoleRouting(module StdModule, block IrBlock, need SlabShardNeed, ids ValueMinter) returns SlabShardRouting
	return match need 'need'
		creditsOnly gives SlabShardRouting.credits
		holdsProc gives SlabShardRouting.proc(pushShardProc(block, ids: ids))
		routed gives soleRoutes(module, block: block, ids: ids)
	end 'need'
end 'pushSoleRouting'

function soleRoutes(module StdModule, block IrBlock, ids ValueMinter) returns SlabShardRouting
	let p = pushShardProc(block, ids: ids)
	let mcacheRow = ids.mint()
	block.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(mcacheRow, argType: StdType.i64))
	let unlocked = emitConst(module, block: block, value: SlabNoLockDepth, ids: ids)

	return SlabShardRouting.routes(SlabShardRoute.create(p, mcacheRow: mcacheRow, depthAddr: unlocked))
end 'soleRoutes'

// The serialised arm's, where the ROW is the constant every edge into it carries, so only the P and the
// lock depth are arguments.
function pushSerialRouting(module StdModule, block IrBlock, state ValueId, need SlabShardNeed, ids ValueMinter) returns SlabShardRouting
	return match need 'need'
		creditsOnly gives SlabShardRouting.credits
		holdsProc gives SlabShardRouting.proc(pushShardProc(block, ids: ids))
		routed gives serialRoutes(module, block: block, state: state, ids: ids)
	end 'need'
end 'pushSerialRouting'

function serialRoutes(module StdModule, block IrBlock, state ValueId, ids ValueMinter) returns SlabShardRouting
	let p = pushShardProc(block, ids: ids)
	let depthAddr = ids.mint()
	block.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(depthAddr, argType: StdType.i64))
	let mcacheRow = emitMcacheRowAt(module, block: block, state: state, row: SlabRawShardRow, ids: ids)

	return SlabShardRouting.routes(SlabShardRoute.create(p, mcacheRow: mcacheRow, depthAddr: depthAddr))
end 'serialRoutes'

// An edge into the SERIALISED arm carries only what its three predecessors differ in — the P this thread
// holds and, for a door that carries its lock across a pop, the depth word it owes a step down at.
//
// ⛔ **THE ADDRESS IS BUILT IN THE PREDECESSOR AND ONLY WHERE A DOOR READS ONE.** Built where the arms are
// chosen it is a `lea` every allocation and every free in the program executes on its way to a sole-writer
// row that takes no lock; built here it costs the three serialised edges alone, and a door that derives its
// lock where it takes it emits nothing for it at all.
function serialEdgeArgs(module StdModule, block IrBlock, p ValueId, depthRecord ValueId, depthOffset ByteOffset, need SlabShardNeed, ids ValueMinter) returns ValueIdArray
	return match need 'need'
		creditsOnly gives ValueIdArray.create(0)
		holdsProc gives argsOf(p)
		routed gives twoArgs(p, second: emitFieldAddr(module, block: block, record: depthRecord, offset: depthOffset, ids: ids))
	end 'need'
end 'serialEdgeArgs'

// What the sole-writer arm's UNSCHEDULED edge carries: the raw row's traffic columns, and — for a door that
// routes — `SlabNoProcessor` and that row's mcache entries. Both addresses are one displacement on
// `state`, and they are what every allocation in a program that never starts a scheduler uses.
function rawSoleEdgeArgs(module StdModule, block IrBlock, state ValueId, trafficRow ValueId, noProc ValueId, need SlabShardNeed, ids ValueMinter) returns ValueIdArray
	return match need 'need'
		creditsOnly gives argsOf(trafficRow)
		holdsProc gives twoArgs(trafficRow, second: noProc)
		routed gives threeArgs(trafficRow, second: noProc, third: emitMcacheRowAt(module, block: block, state: state, row: SlabRawShardRow, ids: ids))
	end 'need'
end 'rawSoleEdgeArgs'

// And what a live P's own edge carries. Its row is a register rather than a build-time number, so the
// mcache row keeps the multiply the raw row's folds away — which is why it is built on THIS edge and not
// in the clamp test that chooses between them.
function ownSoleEdgeArgs(module StdModule, block IrBlock, state ValueId, trafficRow ValueId, p ValueId, row ValueId, need SlabShardNeed, ids ValueMinter) returns ValueIdArray
	return match need 'need'
		creditsOnly gives argsOf(trafficRow)
		holdsProc gives twoArgs(trafficRow, second: p)
		routed gives threeArgs(trafficRow, second: p, third: emitMcacheRowBase(module, block: block, state: state, row: row, ids: ids))
	end 'need'
end 'ownSoleEdgeArgs'

// ⭐⭐⭐ **THE ONE WALK FROM "WHICH THREAD IS THIS" TO "WHICH ROW MAY IT WRITE ALONE".** It terminates `cur`
// and lands in one of the two arms above:
//
//   * scheduler word 0 — no scheduler has ever existed, this is the lone main thread: the raw row, sole
//     writer, plain counters, no lock.
//   * a machine holding a P whose id is an ordinary row: that row, sole writer.
//   * a P whose id clamps to the raw row, a machine holding no P (the system monitor's), or no machine at all
//     (the main thread inside `__sched_init_procs`, a worker between its birth and its slot store): the raw
//     row, serialised.
//
// ⛔⛔ **NEITHER ZERO TEST IS DEFENSIVE.** Before `__sched_init_procs` runs the offset word is 0 and
// `gs:[0]` is `NT_TIB.ExceptionList` — a NON-NULL pointer — so reading through it hands back a garbage
// machine whose next field load faults; and a zero SLOT is a real thread with no machine.
//
// The calling green thread's own columns are stepped here, on the arm that holds the machine, because
// this is the one walk that has the machine in hand: a second walk for them would be the TLS read twice.
//
// ⚠ **THE RAW ROW'S THREE ROUTES INTO `serialised` ARE ONE RULE**: a thread that is not the sole writer of
// the row it takes must serialise. `SlabRawShardRow` carries why "no P" is a state of its own.
//
// ⭐ **THE UNSCHEDULED EDGE CARRIES ITS ADDRESSES ALREADY BUILT**, which is the fold the site shape exists
// for: the raw row's traffic columns and its mcache entries are each one displacement on `state`, and they
// are what every allocation in a program that never starts a scheduler steps and pops from.
//
// ⛔ **THE P's OWN ADDRESSES ARE BUILT ON ITS EDGE AND NOT IN THE CLAMP TEST'S BLOCK.** Built before the
// test they are work a clamped thread throws away — and, both edges then differing in a value, the backend
// if-converts the test and lengthens the walk by the multiplies, the `lea`s and a `setcc` a straight-line
// block cannot skip. That lengthening sits between a door's entry and the arm every allocation in an
// unscheduled program lands in, which is I-cache the hot path pays for on every call.
function emitSlabResolveShard(module StdModule, func IrFunction, cur IrBlock, state ValueId, model MachineModel, need SlabShardNeed, traffic ThreadTraffic, ids ValueMinter, label ByteArray, heat BlockHeat = BlockHeat.normal) returns SlabShardArms
	let decision = emitSlabRowDecision(module, func: func, cur: cur, state: state, model: model, traffic: traffic, ids: ids, label: label, heat: heat)
	let arms = SlabShardArms.create(module, func: func, state: state, schedulerWord: decision.schedulerWord, need: need, ids: ids, label: label, heat: heat)

	let noProc = emitConst(module, block: decision.unscheduled, value: SlabNoProcessor, ids: ids)
	let rawTrafficRow = emitSlabTrafficRowAt(module, block: decision.unscheduled, state: state, row: SlabRawShardRow, ids: ids)
	let rawSoleArgs = rawSoleEdgeArgs(module, block: decision.unscheduled, state: state, trafficRow: rawTrafficRow, noProc: noProc, need: need, ids: ids)
	emitBranchCarrying(module, from: decision.unscheduled, to: arms.sole.block, values: rawSoleArgs)

	emitSlabSerialRoutes(module, serial: decision.serial, arms: arms, state: state, need: need, ids: ids)
	emitSlabOwnRoute(module, own: decision.own, arms: arms, state: state, need: need, ids: ids)

	return arms
end 'emitSlabResolveShard'

function emitSlabSerialRoutes(module StdModule, serial SlabSerialArm, arms SlabShardArms, state ValueId, need SlabShardNeed, ids ValueMinter)
	match serial 'serial'
		absent then return
		present(exits) then emitSlabSerialExits(module, exits: exits, arms: arms, state: state, need: need, ids: ids)
	end 'serial'
end 'emitSlabSerialRoutes'

function emitSlabSerialExits(module StdModule, exits SlabSerialExits, arms SlabShardArms, state ValueId, need SlabShardNeed, ids ValueMinter)
	// A thread that is not a machine has no depth word of its own, so its step lands on the state region's
	// sink — built HERE, because the sink is otherwise a `lea` on the path of every allocation.
	let noMachineProc = emitConst(module, block: exits.noMachine, value: SlabNoProcessor, ids: ids)
	let sinkArgs = serialEdgeArgs(module, block: exits.noMachine, p: noMachineProc, depthRecord: state, depthOffset: SlabStateLockDepthSinkOffset, need: need, ids: ids)
	emitBranchCarrying(module, from: exits.noMachine, to: arms.serial.block, values: sinkArgs)

	let noProcProc = emitConst(module, block: exits.noProc, value: SlabNoProcessor, ids: ids)
	let noProcArgs = serialEdgeArgs(module, block: exits.noProc, p: noProcProc, depthRecord: exits.machine, depthOffset: MOffLocks, need: need, ids: ids)
	emitBranchCarrying(module, from: exits.noProc, to: arms.serial.block, values: noProcArgs)

	let clampedArgs = serialEdgeArgs(module, block: exits.clamped, p: exits.proc, depthRecord: exits.machine, depthOffset: MOffLocks, need: need, ids: ids)
	emitBranchCarrying(module, from: exits.clamped, to: arms.serial.block, values: clampedArgs)
end 'emitSlabSerialExits'

function emitSlabOwnRoute(module StdModule, own SlabOwnArm, arms SlabShardArms, state ValueId, need SlabShardNeed, ids ValueMinter)
	match own 'own'
		absent then return
		present(row) then emitSlabOwnRow(module, row: row, arms: arms, state: state, need: need, ids: ids)
	end 'own'
end 'emitSlabOwnRoute'

function emitSlabOwnRow(module StdModule, row SlabOwnRow, arms SlabShardArms, state ValueId, need SlabShardNeed, ids ValueMinter)
	let ownTrafficRow = emitSlabTrafficRowBase(module, block: row.block, state: state, row: row.id, ids: ids)
	let ownArgs = ownSoleEdgeArgs(module, block: row.block, state: state, trafficRow: ownTrafficRow, p: row.p, row: row.id, need: need, ids: ids)
	emitBranchCarrying(module, from: row.block, to: arms.sole.block, values: ownArgs)
end 'emitSlabOwnRow'

type SlabOwnRow
	export var block as IrBlock
	export let machine as ValueId
	export let p as ValueId
	export let id as ValueId

	static function create(block IrBlock, machine ValueId, p ValueId, id ValueId) returns Self
		return Self{block: block, machine: machine, p: p, id: id}
	end 'create'
end 'SlabOwnRow'

union SlabOwnArm
	absent
	present(row SlabOwnRow)
end 'SlabOwnArm'

type SlabSerialExits
	export var noMachine as IrBlock
	export var noProc as IrBlock
	export var clamped as IrBlock
	export let machine as ValueId
	export let proc as ValueId

	static function create(noMachine IrBlock, noProc IrBlock, clamped IrBlock, machine ValueId, proc ValueId) returns Self
		return Self{noMachine: noMachine, noProc: noProc, clamped: clamped, machine: machine, proc: proc}
	end 'create'
end 'SlabSerialExits'

union SlabSerialArm
	absent
	present(exits SlabSerialExits)
end 'SlabSerialArm'

type SlabRowDecision
	export let schedulerWord as ValueId
	export var unscheduled as IrBlock
	export var own as SlabOwnArm
	export var serial as SlabSerialArm

	static function create(schedulerWord ValueId, unscheduled IrBlock, own SlabOwnArm, serial SlabSerialArm) returns Self
		return Self{schedulerWord: schedulerWord, unscheduled: unscheduled, own: own, serial: serial}
	end 'create'
end 'SlabRowDecision'

// The one decision of which row this thread may write alone. `emitSlabResolveShard` routes its serial
// exits into the serialised arm and `emitSlabFastWalk` bails on them, so the leaf and the full body cannot
// disagree about a row's sole writer. The P is carried in the own-row arm so `M.currentP` is read once.
function emitSlabRowDecision(module StdModule, func IrFunction, cur IrBlock, state ValueId, model MachineModel, traffic ThreadTraffic, ids ValueMinter, label ByteArray, heat BlockHeat) returns SlabRowDecision
	let schedulerWord = emitSlabSchedulerWord(module, block: cur, state: state, model: model, ids: ids)
	let unscheduled = module.addBlock(func, label: label, heat: heat)

	if model == MachineModel.singleMachine 'oneThread'
		module.setTerminator(cur, op: StdOp.branch(unscheduled.id))

		return SlabRowDecision.create(schedulerWord, unscheduled: unscheduled, own: SlabOwnArm.absent, serial: SlabSerialArm.absent)
	end 'oneThread'

	let haveMachine = module.addBlock(func, label: label, heat: heat)
	let noMachine = module.addBlock(func, label: label, heat: heat)
	let noProc = module.addBlock(func, label: label, heat: heat)
	let haveProc = module.addBlock(func, label: label, heat: heat)
	let clamped = module.addBlock(func, label: label, heat: heat)
	let ownRow = module.addBlock(func, label: label, heat: heat)

	let rawRowUnlocked = SlabBailEdge.create(unscheduled, args: ValueIdArray.create(0))
	let rawRowSerialised = SlabBailEdge.create(noMachine, args: ValueIdArray.create(0))
	let m = emitSlabMachineOrBail(module, func: func, cur: cur, schedulerWord: schedulerWord, haveMachine: haveMachine, noScheduler: rawRowUnlocked, noMachine: rawRowSerialised, ids: ids, label: label, heat: heat)

	let procTest = emitThreadTrafficStep(module, func: func, cur: haveMachine, m: m, traffic: traffic, ids: ids, label: label, heat: heat)
	let p = emitLoadField(module, block: procTest, record: m, offset: MOffCurrentP, ids: ids)
	emitBranchOnCompare(module, block: procTest, value: p, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, against: SlabNoProcessor, whenTrue: noProc, whenFalse: haveProc, ids: ids)

	let clamp = emitSlabRowClamp(module, block: haveProc, p: p, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(haveProc, op: StdOp.condBranch(clamp.beyond, thenBlockId: clamped.id, elseBlockId: ownRow.id))

	return SlabRowDecision.create(schedulerWord, unscheduled: unscheduled, own: SlabOwnArm.present(SlabOwnRow.create(ownRow, machine: m, p: p, id: clamp.id)), serial: SlabSerialArm.present(SlabSerialExits.create(noMachine, noProc: noProc, clamped: clamped, machine: m, proc: p)))
end 'emitSlabRowDecision'

// ⭐ **THE CALLING GREEN THREAD's OWN COLUMNS.** Unlocked and non-atomic because a strand runs on at most
// one machine at a time (`GtRuntime.GtOffThreadAllocs`). Terminates `cur` when there is traffic to record
// and answers the block to continue in.
function emitThreadTrafficStep(module StdModule, func IrFunction, cur IrBlock, m ValueId, traffic ThreadTraffic, ids ValueMinter, label ByteArray, heat BlockHeat) returns IrBlock
	match traffic 'traffic'
		none then return cur
		alloc or
		free then break
	end 'traffic'

	let haveThread = module.addBlock(func, label: label, heat: heat)
	let done = module.addBlock(func, label: label, heat: heat)

	let owner = emitSlabOwnerThreadOrBail(module, cur: cur, m: m, haveOwner: haveThread, noThread: SlabBailEdge.create(done, args: ValueIdArray.create(0)), ids: ids)

	match traffic 'columns'
		alloc(bytes) then emitThreadAllocColumns(module, block: haveThread, owner: owner, bytes: bytes, ids: ids)
		free then emitThreadColumnStep(module, block: haveThread, owner: owner, offset: GtOffThreadFrees, delta: emitConst(module, block: haveThread, value: OneMore, ids: ids), ids: ids)
		none then panic("emitThreadTrafficStep: `none` answers before any block is added")
	end 'columns'

	module.setTerminator(haveThread, op: StdOp.branch(done.id))

	return done
end 'emitThreadTrafficStep'

function emitThreadAllocColumns(module StdModule, block IrBlock, owner ValueId, bytes ValueId, ids ValueMinter)
	emitThreadColumnStep(module, block: block, owner: owner, offset: GtOffThreadAllocs, delta: emitConst(module, block: block, value: OneMore, ids: ids), ids: ids)
	emitThreadColumnStep(module, block: block, owner: owner, offset: GtOffThreadAllocBytes, delta: bytes, ids: ids)
end 'emitThreadAllocColumns'

function emitThreadColumnStep(module StdModule, block IrBlock, owner ValueId, offset ByteOffset, delta ValueId, ids ValueMinter)
	_ = emitFieldAccumulate(module, block: block, record: owner, offset: offset, delta: delta, multiM: false, ids: ids)
end 'emitThreadColumnStep'

// A P's row and whether it clamps: `id >= SlabRawShardRow` names a host with 256+ processors, whose P
// would otherwise index past the mcache. `SlabRawShardRow` carries why the clamp lands on the RAW row.
type SlabRowClamp
	export let id as ValueId
	export let beyond as ValueId

	static function create(id ValueId, beyond ValueId) returns Self
		return Self{id: id, beyond: beyond}
	end 'create'
end 'SlabRowClamp'

function emitSlabRowClamp(module StdModule, block IrBlock, p ValueId, ids ValueMinter) returns SlabRowClamp
	let id = emitLoadField(module, block: block, record: p, offset: POffId, ids: ids)
	return SlabRowClamp.create(id, beyond: emitCompareWithConstant(module, block: block, value: id, pred: StdCmpPred.ge, against: SlabRawShardRow, ids: ids))
end 'emitSlabRowClamp'

// ⭐ **WHERE THIS THREAD KEEPS ITS LOCK DEPTH, FOR A BODY THAT HOLDS NO MACHINE IN HAND** — the three
// cold bodies that take `__slab_lock` whole (`__slab_refill`, `__slab_scavenge`, and `__slab_free`'s two
// serialised arms).
// Terminates `cur` and answers in `addrId`, the merge block's single argument.
function emitSlabLockDepthAddr(module StdModule, func IrFunction, cur IrBlock, state ValueId, model MachineModel, addrId ValueId, ids ValueMinter, label ByteArray, heat BlockHeat = BlockHeat.normal) returns IrBlock
	let merge = module.addBlock(func, label: label, heat: heat)
	merge.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(addrId, argType: StdType.i64))

	let sink = emitFieldAddr(module, block: cur, record: state, offset: SlabStateLockDepthSinkOffset, ids: ids)
	let schedulerWord = emitSlabSchedulerWord(module, block: cur, state: state, model: model, ids: ids)

	if model == MachineModel.singleMachine 'oneThread'
		emitBranchCarrying(module, from: cur, to: merge, values: argsOf(sink))

		return merge
	end 'oneThread'

	let haveMachine = module.addBlock(func, label: label, heat: heat)

	let toSink = SlabBailEdge.create(merge, args: argsOf(sink))
	let m = emitSlabMachineOrBail(module, func: func, cur: cur, schedulerWord: schedulerWord, haveMachine: haveMachine, noScheduler: toSink, noMachine: toSink, ids: ids, label: label, heat: heat)

	let mine = emitFieldAddr(module, block: haveMachine, record: m, offset: MOffLocks, ids: ids)
	emitBranchCarrying(module, from: haveMachine, to: merge, values: argsOf(mine))

	return merge
end 'emitSlabLockDepthAddr'

// The global slow-path lock, at its fixed offset in the state region. One spelling, because a lock reached
// through two different address computations is two locks the day one of them is wrong.
function emitSlabLockAddr(module StdModule, block IrBlock, state ValueId, ids ValueMinter) returns ValueId
	let offset = emitConst(module, block: block, value: SlabStateLockOffset as FullRangeInt, ids: ids)
	return emitBin(module, block: block, a: state, b: offset, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
end 'emitSlabLockAddr'

// The allocator's slow path is as unstoppable as the scheduler's critical sections: a thread
// stopped holding this lock would carry it into a park and never release it. Same word, same order
// as `SchedRuntime.emitSchedLockEnter` — up before the acquire, down after the release. On wasm the
// lock marks are dropped (`expandRuntimeLocks`) and the depth step lands on the sink.
function emitSlabLockEnter(module StdModule, block IrBlock, state ValueId, depthAddr ValueId, ids ValueMinter)
	emitLockEnterWithDepth(module, block: block, lock: emitSlabLockAddr(module, block: block, state: state, ids: ids), kind: RuntimeLockKind.recursive, depthRecord: depthAddr, depthOffset: 0, ids: ids)
end 'emitSlabLockEnter'

function emitSlabLockLeave(module StdModule, block IrBlock, state ValueId, depthAddr ValueId, ids ValueMinter)
	emitLockLeaveWithDepth(module, block: block, lock: emitSlabLockAddr(module, block: block, state: state, ids: ids), kind: RuntimeLockKind.recursive, depthRecord: depthAddr, depthOffset: 0, ids: ids)
end 'emitSlabLockLeave'

// --- The traffic columns ---

// `state + traffic + row * rowBytes` — a row's columns, addressed once per event so the column is a
// displacement on the step rather than a second multiply. One spelling for the writers and the reader.
//
// ⭐⭐ **A WRITER ADDRESSES ITS ROW BEFORE THE ARMS JOIN, AND THAT IS WHAT LETS THE COMMON ARM FOLD.** A row
// INDEX joined from two predecessors is a register by the time this runs, so the multiply and both adds
// survive onto the hot path; computed in each predecessor, the edge whose row is a constant collapses to
// one displacement on `state` and the edge whose row is a P id keeps the arithmetic it needs.
// ⛔⛔ **A CONSTANT ROW IS FOLDED HERE OR NOT AT ALL.** `FoldConstants` runs inside the lowering pipeline,
// which is over before any installer mints a runtime body — so `255 * rowBytes` written as a `mul` of two
// `const`s reaches instruction selection as a real `imul` on the hot path of every allocation. The two
// spellings below differ only in whether the row is a build-time number.
function emitSlabTrafficRowAt(module StdModule, block IrBlock, state ValueId, row FullRangeInt, ids ValueMinter) returns ValueId
	return emitFieldAddr(module, block: block, record: state, offset: (slabStateTrafficOffset() + row * slabTrafficRowBytes()) as ByteOffset, ids: ids)
end 'emitSlabTrafficRowAt'

function emitSlabTrafficRowBase(module StdModule, block IrBlock, state ValueId, row ValueId, ids ValueMinter) returns ValueId
	let rowBytes = emitConst(module, block: block, value: slabTrafficRowBytes(), ids: ids)
	let rowOffset = emitBin(module, block: block, a: row, b: rowBytes, opcode: StdBinOpcode.mul, ids: ids)
	let arrayBase = emitConst(module, block: block, value: slabStateTrafficOffset(), ids: ids)
	let fromBase = emitBin(module, block: block, a: state, b: arrayBase, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	return emitBin(module, block: block, a: fromBase, b: rowOffset, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
end 'emitSlabTrafficRowBase'

function slabTrafficColumnOffset(column SlabTrafficColumn) returns ByteOffset
	return (column.ordinal as ByteOffset) * SlabRecordWordBytes
end 'slabTrafficColumnOffset'

function slabLayerFreesColumn(layer SlabTrafficLayer) returns SlabTrafficColumn
	return match layer 'layer'
		tracked gives SlabTrafficColumn.trackedFrees
		raw gives SlabTrafficColumn.rawFrees
	end 'layer'
end 'slabLayerFreesColumn'

function slabLayerTotalColumn(layer SlabTrafficLayer) returns SlabTrafficColumn
	return match layer 'layer'
		tracked gives SlabTrafficColumn.trackedTotal
		raw gives SlabTrafficColumn.rawTotal
	end 'layer'
end 'slabLayerTotalColumn'

function slabLayerBytesColumn(layer SlabTrafficLayer) returns SlabTrafficColumn
	return match layer 'layer'
		tracked gives SlabTrafficColumn.trackedBytes
		raw gives SlabTrafficColumn.rawBytes
	end 'layer'
end 'slabLayerBytesColumn'

function emitSlabTrafficColumnStep(module StdModule, block IrBlock, rowBase ValueId, column SlabTrafficColumn, delta ValueId, atomic bool, ids ValueMinter)
	_ = emitFieldAccumulate(module, block: block, record: rowBase, offset: slabTrafficColumnOffset(column), delta: delta, multiM: atomic, ids: ids)
end 'emitSlabTrafficColumnStep'

// ⭐⭐ **RECORD ONE EVENT IN THE ROW `rowBase` ADDRESSES.** `atomic` is a build-time literal per emission:
// the two arms of `emitSlabResolveShard` are two emissions, one plain and one `atomicRmw`, so no allocation
// tests which it got.
//
// ⭐ **AN ALLOCATION STEPS TWO COLUMNS AND A FREE ONE, WHICH IS THE WHOLE OF WHAT DERIVING `live` BUYS.**
// Every column is cumulative — a free walks nothing back — so a phase delta may be taken over any of them,
// and a reader that wants a live count subtracts two of them itself.
function emitSlabTrafficCredit(module StdModule, block IrBlock, rowBase ValueId, layer SlabTrafficLayer, event SlabTrafficEvent, atomic bool, ids ValueMinter)
	match event 'event'
		alloc(bytes) then emitSlabAllocColumns(module, block: block, rowBase: rowBase, layer: layer, bytes: bytes, atomic: atomic, ids: ids)
		free then emitSlabTrafficColumnStep(module, block: block, rowBase: rowBase, column: slabLayerFreesColumn(layer), delta: emitConst(module, block: block, value: OneMore, ids: ids), atomic: atomic, ids: ids)
	end 'event'
end 'emitSlabTrafficCredit'

function emitSlabAllocColumns(module StdModule, block IrBlock, rowBase ValueId, layer SlabTrafficLayer, bytes ValueId, atomic bool, ids ValueMinter)
	emitSlabTrafficColumnStep(module, block: block, rowBase: rowBase, column: slabLayerTotalColumn(layer), delta: emitConst(module, block: block, value: OneMore, ids: ids), atomic: atomic, ids: ids)
	emitSlabTrafficColumnStep(module, block: block, rowBase: rowBase, column: slabLayerBytesColumn(layer), delta: bytes, atomic: atomic, ids: ids)
end 'emitSlabAllocColumns'

// --- Reading the traffic columns ---

// ⭐⭐ **WHAT ONE READER ANSWERS, AS AN EXPRESSION OVER A SINGLE ROW'S COLUMNS.** Every figure this family
// reports is a weighted sum of one row's words, so one walk serves them all — and being able to state the
// whole expression per ROW is what keeps a difference honest.
union SlabTrafficFigure
	// One column.
	column(which SlabTrafficColumn)

	// A layer's live count, its total less its frees.
	live(layer SlabTrafficLayer)

	// Two columns added, and `prefixPerBox` bytes for every tracked box on top: the byte reader's prefix,
	// and 0 for a count.
	sum(first SlabTrafficColumn, second SlabTrafficColumn, prefixPerBox FullRangeInt)
end 'SlabTrafficFigure'

// ⭐⭐ **ONE FIGURE OVER EVERY ROW — the process-wide answer, and the only reader shape there is.** The
// read is deliberately racy against a thread mid-step, exactly as asking a running program how much it has
// allocated is: a caller that wants an exact answer reads it where it owns every allocating thread.
//
// ⛔⛔ **ONE WALK, WITH THE DIFFERENCE TAKEN INSIDE IT.** Summing a minuend over 256 rows and then its
// subtrahend over 256 more samples the two thousands of loads apart, and a single allocation landing
// between the walks is credited to the subtrahend alone — which the caller reads as a figure below zero,
// or, through an unsigned typealias, as 1.8e19. Per row the columns are read together and only a running
// total crosses rows, so a figure that is non-negative while the allocator is quiet stays non-negative.
//
// ⛔ **A PROGRAM THAT HAS NEVER ALLOCATED ANSWERS 0 WITHOUT COMMITTING THE REGION.** Committing it to walk
// 256 rows of zeroes would charge every `usesHeap` program a 19-chunk arena request at exit, and it would
// put the commit probe — whose single-writer premise is the allocation doors' — behind every counter
// reader on every green thread.
//
// Terminates `cur` and answers in `answerId`, the merge block's single argument.
function emitSlabTrafficRead(module StdModule, func IrFunction, cur IrBlock, figure SlabTrafficFigure, answerId ValueId, ids ValueMinter, label ByteArray) returns IrBlock
	let walk = module.addBlock(func, label: label, heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	let loopHdr = module.addBlock(func, label: label, heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	let loopBody = module.addBlock(func, label: label, heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	let done = module.addBlock(func, label: label, heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	done.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(answerId, argType: StdType.i64))

	let state = emitSlabStateCommitted(module, block: cur, ids: ids)
	let uncommitted = emitSlabStateUncommitted(module, block: cur, state: state, ids: ids)
	let nothingWalked = emitConst(module, block: cur, value: 0, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(cur, op: StdOp.condBranch(uncommitted, thenBlockId: done.id, elseBlockId: walk.id))
	pushBranchEdge(cur, to: done, values: argsOf(nothingWalked))

	let firstRow = emitConst(module, block: walk, value: 0, ids: ids)
	emitBranchCarrying(module, from: walk, to: loopHdr, values: twoArgs(firstRow, second: firstRow))

	let row = ids.mint()
	let total = ids.mint()
	loopHdr.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(row, argType: StdType.i64))
	loopHdr.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(total, argType: StdType.i64))
	emitBranchOnCompare(module, block: loopHdr, value: row, pred: StdCmpPred.ge, against: SlabMaxShards, whenTrue: done, whenFalse: loopBody, ids: ids)
	pushBranchEdge(loopHdr, to: done, values: argsOf(total))

	let rowBase = emitSlabTrafficRowBase(module, block: loopBody, state: state, row: row, ids: ids)
	let countedHere = emitSlabTrafficRowFigure(module, block: loopBody, rowBase: rowBase, figure: figure, ids: ids)
	let runningTotal = emitBin(module, block: loopBody, a: total, b: countedHere, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	let step = emitConst(module, block: loopBody, value: OneMore, ids: ids)
	let nextRow = emitBin(module, block: loopBody, a: row, b: step, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	emitBranchCarrying(module, from: loopBody, to: loopHdr, values: twoArgs(nextRow, second: runningTotal))

	return done
end 'emitSlabTrafficRead'

function emitSlabTrafficRowFigure(module StdModule, block IrBlock, rowBase ValueId, figure SlabTrafficFigure, ids ValueMinter) returns ValueId
	return match figure 'figure'
		column(which) gives emitSlabColumnLoad(module, block: block, rowBase: rowBase, column: which, ids: ids)
		live(layer) gives emitSlabRowLive(module, block: block, rowBase: rowBase, layer: layer, ids: ids)
		sum(first, second, prefixPerBox) gives emitSlabRowSum(module, block: block, rowBase: rowBase, first: first, second: second, prefixPerBox: prefixPerBox, ids: ids)
	end 'figure'
end 'emitSlabTrafficRowFigure'

function emitSlabColumnLoad(module StdModule, block IrBlock, rowBase ValueId, column SlabTrafficColumn, ids ValueMinter) returns ValueId
	return emitLoadField(module, block: block, record: rowBase, offset: slabTrafficColumnOffset(column), ids: ids)
end 'emitSlabColumnLoad'

function emitSlabRowLive(module StdModule, block IrBlock, rowBase ValueId, layer SlabTrafficLayer, ids ValueMinter) returns ValueId
	let total = emitSlabColumnLoad(module, block: block, rowBase: rowBase, column: slabLayerTotalColumn(layer), ids: ids)
	let frees = emitSlabColumnLoad(module, block: block, rowBase: rowBase, column: slabLayerFreesColumn(layer), ids: ids)
	return emitBin(module, block: block, a: total, b: frees, opcode: StdBinOpcode.sub, ids: ids)
end 'emitSlabRowLive'

function emitSlabRowSum(module StdModule, block IrBlock, rowBase ValueId, first SlabTrafficColumn, second SlabTrafficColumn, prefixPerBox FullRangeInt, ids ValueMinter) returns ValueId
	let firstHere = emitSlabColumnLoad(module, block: block, rowBase: rowBase, column: first, ids: ids)
	let secondHere = emitSlabColumnLoad(module, block: block, rowBase: rowBase, column: second, ids: ids)
	var answer = emitBin(module, block: block, a: firstHere, b: secondHere, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)

	if prefixPerBox != 0 'perBoxPrefix'
		let boxes = emitSlabColumnLoad(module, block: block, rowBase: rowBase, column: SlabTrafficColumn.trackedTotal, ids: ids)
		let prefix = emitConst(module, block: block, value: prefixPerBox, ids: ids)
		let prefixBytes = emitBin(module, block: block, a: boxes, b: prefix, opcode: StdBinOpcode.mul, ids: ids)
		answer = emitBin(module, block: block, a: answer, b: prefixBytes, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	end 'perBoxPrefix'

	return answer
end 'emitSlabRowSum'

// CAS-push `slot` onto `span`'s remote free list — a Treiber stack at `MspanRemoteHeadOffset`, many
// producers and one consumer at a time. The link rides in the slot's own first word, the same word the span
// free list threads its links through and the same word the allocation pop overwrites when it hands the slot
// back out, so the queue costs no storage of its own.
//
// ⛔ The CAS is the last thing this body does to the span, and that is a correctness rule rather than a
// shape. Once the slot is on the chain a collector holding `__slab_lock` may credit it into `free_count`,
// find the span complete and destroy it — recycling this header — so any span access after the CAS would be
// a write through a recycled record. Before the CAS the slot is live and uncounted, so no completion is
// possible; `buildSlabScavenge` carries the count that makes that exact.
//
// No fence is needed and none may be added to be safe. The store of the old head into `[slot]` must be
// visible to the collector before the CAS publishes `slot`, and on both lanes the compiler emits the CAS on,
// the CAS is that barrier: x64's `lock cmpxchg` is a full barrier by architecture and arm64's `StLXR` pair
// (`TargetOp.arm64AtomicCas`) is a store-release. `StdOp.atomicCas` answers the SUCCESS BOOLEAN and not the
// prior value, and a loop written for the other convention spins forever on its first success.
//
// `myP` is credited only when `owner` is a P. A negative `owner` (`SpanOwnerState.partial` on this
// road) is a span no processor caches: its free takes this push because only a CAS may write its
// list, but it crosses no processor. The test is `owner < 0` rather than one sentinel, so a
// sentinel added later is not credited. What `__slab_remote_free_count` reports is therefore frees
// PERFORMED across processors, and a freer that holds no P at all is not counted — a reading of 0
// does not mean the road was never taken. The credit is emitted before `retry` rather than inside
// `retry`, because a contended push runs the loop body several times for one free and a credit in
// there would count CAS attempts.
function emitSlabRemotePush(module StdModule, func IrFunction, cur IrBlock, span ValueId, slot ValueId, owner ValueId, myP ValueId, ids ValueMinter) returns IrBlock
	let retry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"rpush", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	let cacherAsked = module.addBlock(func, label: b"rcacher", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	let credited = module.addBlock(func, label: b"rcredit", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	let done = module.addBlock(func, label: b"rpushed", heat: BlockHeat.normal)

	// Stepped before the head moves, so a reader holding `__slab_lock` that sees the chain also sees a count
	// that covers it. The other order leaves a window where the chain is longer than the count.
	// Atomic, unlike the per-P credit below: frees from several processors land on one span.
	let queued = emitConst(module, block: cur, value: OneMore, ids: ids)
	_ = emitFieldAccumulate(module, block: cur, record: span, offset: MspanRemoteCountOffset, delta: queued, multiM: true, ids: ids)

	let holdsNoProc = emitCompareWithConstant(module, block: cur, value: myP, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, against: SlabNoProcessor, ids: ids)
	let headAddr = emitFieldAddr(module, block: cur, record: span, offset: MspanRemoteHeadOffset, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(cur, op: StdOp.condBranch(holdsNoProc, thenBlockId: retry.id, elseBlockId: cacherAsked.id))

	emitBranchOnCompare(module, block: cacherAsked, value: owner, pred: StdCmpPred.lt, against: 0, whenTrue: retry, whenFalse: credited, ids: ids)

	let credit = emitConst(module, block: credited, value: OneMore, ids: ids)
	_ = emitFieldAccumulate(module, block: credited, record: myP, offset: POffRemoteFreeCount, delta: credit, multiM: false, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(credited, op: StdOp.branch(retry.id))

	let head = emitLoadField(module, block: retry, record: headAddr, offset: 0, ids: ids)
	emitStoreField(module, block: retry, record: slot, offset: 0, value: head)
	let won = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(retry, op: StdOp.atomicCas(won, addrId: headAddr, expectedId: head, replacementId: slot))
	emitBranchOnCasLost(module, block: retry, result: won, lost: retry, won: done, ids: ids)

	return done
end 'emitSlabRemotePush'

// --- The bodies ---

// ⭐⭐ **THE STATE REGION'S BASE WITHOUT A FRAME: the `.data` word, tested, with the COMMIT left as the call
// it already is**. It answers in `stateId`, the merge block's single argument.
//
// ⭐ **THE HOT ARM IS FOUR OPS AND THE COLD ONE IS A CALL, WHICH IS THE WHOLE OF WHY THIS IS NOT A SECOND
// COPY OF THE BODY.** The region is committed by the FIRST allocation a process makes and never released,
// so every subsequent allocation and every free reads a non-zero word and is done. Reproducing the commit
// here — the arena request, the `memFill`, the publishing store, in the order that
// publishes LAST — would be that sequence written twice, and it is the sequence whose ORDER is the
// argument. So the cold arm calls `__slab_state_base`, whose body is `runtime/SlabRuntime.maxon` source; it
// re-reads the word and commits it, and that second read costs one load on the once-per-process path.
//
// ⚠ **THE READ IS SPELLED HERE AND ONCE MORE IN THE TIER, AND THE SENTINEL IS WHAT THE TWO MUST AGREE
// ON.** The LABEL cannot drift — the tier names the word through `__Raw.slabStateAddr`, which lowers to this
// same `SlabStateLabel` — so what a drift could break is the test: a tier that published a different
// "uncommitted" value than this probe tests for would commit the region on every allocation.
//
// `miss` is `leaves` for a leaf, which jumps to its bail instead of calling the commit so the hit path
// holds no call.
function emitSlabStateBase(module StdModule, func IrFunction, cur IrBlock, stateId ValueId, ids ValueMinter, label ByteArray, heat BlockHeat = BlockHeat.normal, miss SlabStateMiss = SlabStateMiss.commits) returns IrBlock
	let merge = module.addBlock(func, label: label, heat: heat)
	merge.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(stateId, argType: StdType.i64))

	let stateAddr = ids.mint()
	let current = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(cur, op: StdOp.globalAddr(stateAddr, label: SlabStateLabel))
	module.appendOp(cur, op: StdOp.loadIndirect(current, addrId: stateAddr, offset: 0, valueType: StdType.i64))

	let uncommitted = emitSlabStateUncommitted(module, block: cur, state: current, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(cur, op: StdOp.condBranch(uncommitted, thenBlockId: emitSlabStateMissRoad(module, func: func, miss: miss, merge: merge, label: label, ids: ids), elseBlockId: merge.id))
	pushBranchEdge(cur, to: merge, values: argsOf(current))

	return merge
end 'emitSlabStateBase'

union SlabStateMiss
	commits
	leaves(exit BlockId)
end 'SlabStateMiss'

function emitSlabStateMissRoad(module StdModule, func IrFunction, miss SlabStateMiss, merge IrBlock, label ByteArray, ids ValueMinter) returns BlockId
	match miss 'miss'
		commits then return emitSlabStateCommitRoad(module, func: func, merge: merge, label: label, ids: ids)
		leaves(exit) then return exit
	end 'miss'
end 'emitSlabStateMissRoad'

function emitSlabStateCommitRoad(module StdModule, func IrFunction, merge IrBlock, label ByteArray, ids ValueMinter) returns BlockId
	let commit = module.addBlock(func, label: label, heat: BlockHeat.cold)

	let committed = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(commit, op: StdOp.call(committed, callee: SlabStateName, args: ValueIdArray.create(0)))
	emitBranchCarrying(module, from: commit, to: merge, values: argsOf(committed))

	return commit.id
end 'emitSlabStateCommitRoad'

// ⭐ *"Has this program allocated anything yet?"* — the region's `.data` word is 0 until the first commit.
// One spelling, because the readers that must NOT commit a region test exactly what the doors that must
// test.
function emitSlabStateUncommitted(module StdModule, block IrBlock, state ValueId, ids ValueMinter) returns ValueId
	return emitCompareWithConstant(module, block: block, value: state, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, against: SlabStateUncommitted, ids: ids)
end 'emitSlabStateUncommitted'

// ⭐ **THE STATE REGION'S BASE WITH NO PROBE — for a door handed a region the allocator already served.**
// Both tiers of every allocation door commit the region before they credit a row, so a free of anything this
// allocator answered, and the box layer's own bookkeeping after its `__slab_alloc` call, read a non-zero
// word by construction. A zero here is a free
// of a pointer no allocation produced, and it faults on the first field read rather than answering.
function emitSlabStateCommitted(module StdModule, block IrBlock, ids ValueMinter) returns ValueId
	let stateAddr = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(block, op: StdOp.globalAddr(stateAddr, label: SlabStateLabel))
	return emitLoadField(module, block: block, record: stateAddr, offset: 0, ids: ids)
end 'emitSlabStateCommitted'

// **`classIdx` = the smallest class whose slot holds `size` bytes, in O(1)** — emitted INTO the caller's
// blocks, answering in `classId`, the merge block's single argument.
//
// ⛔ **SCANNING THE CLASS LADDER IS NOT AN OPTION.** Walking up to 18 classes per allocation costs ~15
// compares for a 1 KiB request before a byte is served, on the hot path of every allocation in the
// language. Go answers the same question with TWO reverse tables and one indexed byte load, which is what
// this emits.
//
// ⭐⭐ **IT IS EMITTED INLINE, BECAUSE A NINE-OP LEAF IS NOT WORTH A FRAME.**
// The whole body is two compares, four ALU ops, an `.rdata` address and a byte load — LESS than the
// prologue, the argument move, the `call`, the `ret` and the epilogue that reached it, and it sits on the
// path of every allocation in the language. Emitting it inline is what makes `__slab_alloc`'s hit path one
// frame; the two callers (the allocator's entry and the sizing answer) are both inside this file, so there
// is no name for anything else to have depended on.
//
// ⚠ **THE TWO TABLES ARE DERIVED, NOT TRANSCRIBED** (`SlabClassTable.slabSizeToClass8Table`), and their
// defining property is asserted at build time.
//
// ⚠ **A CALLER MUST HAVE ROUTED `size > SlabMaxSmallSize` AWAY** — `emitSlabSizeRouting` is what does it.
// A request of 0 reaches class 0, the ladder's sentinel, whose span holds no objects; `__slab_refill`'s
// guard is where that is caught, on the COLD path, so the hot path pays nothing for a case no caller has.
function emitSlabClassIndex(module StdModule, func IrFunction, cur IrBlock, size ValueId, classId ValueId, ids ValueMinter, label ByteArray) returns IrBlock
	let smallTable = module.addBlock(func, label: label, heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	let largeTable = module.addBlock(func, label: label, heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	let merge = module.addBlock(func, label: label, heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	merge.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(classId, argType: StdType.i64))

	emitBranchOnCompare(module, block: cur, value: size, pred: StdCmpPred.gt, against: SlabSmallTableMaxSize, whenTrue: largeTable, whenFalse: smallTable, ids: ids)

	let small = emitReverseTableLookup(module, block: smallTable, size: size, subtract: 0, divShift: SlabSmallSizeDivShift, divisor: SlabSmallSizeDiv, label: SlabSizeToClass8LabelBytes, ids: ids)
	emitBranchCarrying(module, from: smallTable, to: merge, values: argsOf(small))

	let large = emitReverseTableLookup(module, block: largeTable, size: size, subtract: SlabSmallSizeMax, divShift: SlabLargeSizeDivShift, divisor: SlabLargeSizeDiv, label: SlabSizeToClass128LabelBytes, ids: ids)
	emitBranchCarrying(module, from: largeTable, to: merge, values: argsOf(large))

	return merge
end 'emitSlabClassIndex'

// One reverse-table read: `table[ceil((size - subtract) / divisor)]`. Both arms are the same four ops over
// different constants, which is why they are one emitter — writing them out twice is how one of the two
// ends up dividing by the other's stride.
//
// The rounding is `(x + divisor - 1) shr divShift`, and it is exact for both arms because every value
// reaching either is positive: the small arm sees `size <= 1016` and the large arm sees `size >= 1017`, so
// `size - 1024 + 127 >= 120`.
function emitReverseTableLookup(module StdModule, block IrBlock, size ValueId, subtract FullRangeInt, divShift FullRangeInt, divisor FullRangeInt, label ByteArray, ids ValueMinter) returns ValueId
	let bias = emitConst(module, block: block, value: divisor - 1 - subtract, ids: ids)
	let raised = emitBin(module, block: block, a: size, b: bias, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	let shift = emitConst(module, block: block, value: divShift, ids: ids)
	let index = emitBin(module, block: block, a: raised, b: shift, opcode: StdBinOpcode.shr, ids: ids)

	let base = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(block, op: StdOp.rdataAddr(base, label: label))
	return emitLoadByteIndexed(module, block: block, base: base, index: index, ids: ids)
end 'emitReverseTableLookup'

// `__slab_rounded_size(size) -> bytes` — the number of bytes `__slab_alloc(size)` actually serves.
//
// ⭐ **IT SHARES THE ROUTING WITH THE ALLOCATOR (`emitSlabSizeRouting`)**, so it cannot report a class for
// a request the allocator sends to the OS. ⚠ Its OS-direct arm's page rounding and prefix width are
// RESTATED against `runtime/SlabRuntime.maxon` rather than shared — see `emitSlabSizeRouting`.
//
// ⚠ **IT HAS NO CALLER TODAY.** The caller it waits for is an inline-small-array capacity reclaim, which
// turns a class's slack into spare elements. Array records
// have no such path yet — but now that every managed box is a slab slot, the slack between an array's
// requested bytes and the class that serves them is real and measurable, so the reclaim is a question this
// compiler can finally ask. `DFE` drops the function from every program until something does, so it costs
// nothing emitted, and the reason it is here rather than absent is the shared emitters above — they are what
// makes the sizing answer and the allocator's routing one fact rather than two that must be kept in step.
function buildSlabRoundedSize(module StdModule)
	let func = IrFunction.createFromStd(SlabRoundedSizeName, returnType: StdReturnType.value(StdType.i64), paramCount: 1, paramTypes: oneI64Param())
	let ids = ValueMinter.create(1)

	let entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	let osDirect = module.addBlock(func, label: b"osdirect", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	let small = module.addBlock(func, label: b"small", heat: BlockHeat.normal)

	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 1)
	let size = 0

	emitSlabSizeRouting(module, block: entry, size: size, osDirect: osDirect, small: small, ids: ids)

	// The OS-direct answer is the mapping's payload: the request plus the size prefix, rounded to a page,
	// less the prefix again — the bytes a caller may actually use.
	let prefix = emitConst(module, block: osDirect, value: OsDirectPrefixBytes, ids: ids)
	let withPrefix = emitBin(module, block: osDirect, a: size, b: prefix, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	let mapped = emitAlignUp(module, block: osDirect, value: withPrefix, width: SlabChunkBytes, ids: ids)
	let usable = emitBin(module, block: osDirect, a: mapped, b: prefix, opcode: StdBinOpcode.sub, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(osDirect, op: StdOp.ret(usable))

	let class = ids.mint()
	let classReady = emitSlabClassIndex(module, func: func, cur: small, size: size, classId: class, ids: ids, label: b"cls")
	let geom = emitClassGeometry(module, block: classReady, classIdx: class, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(classReady, op: StdOp.ret(emitGeomSlotSize(module, block: classReady, geom: geom, ids: ids)))

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildSlabRoundedSize'

// Put `span` at the head of the mcentral list `mcentralSlot` names, and stamp it `Partial`.
//
// ⛔ The stamp is a RELEASE and is the LAST store: a freer that observes `Partial` without the lock CAS-pushes
// onto the span, so publishing the state before the link would advertise a span that is not yet reachable.
//
// ⭐ **THE LIST IS SINGLY LINKED AND NEEDS NOTHING MORE, BECAUSE NOTHING IS EVER REMOVED FROM THE MIDDLE OF
// IT.** `__slab_refill` pops the HEAD, and `__slab_scavenge` detaches the WHOLE list and rebuilds it out of
// the survivors — the idiom that needs no `prev` carried through the walk.
function emitMcentralLink(module StdModule, block IrBlock, span ValueId, mcentralSlot ValueId, ids ValueMinter)
	let head = emitLoadField(module, block: block, record: mcentralSlot, offset: 0, ids: ids)
	emitStoreField(module, block: block, record: span, offset: MspanNextSpanOffset, value: head)
	emitStoreField(module, block: block, record: mcentralSlot, offset: 0, value: span)
	emitStoreSpanOwnerState(module, block: block, span: span, state: SpanOwnerState.partial, ids: ids)
end 'emitMcentralLink'

function emitStoreSpanOwnerState(module StdModule, block IrBlock, span ValueId, state SpanOwnerState, ids ValueMinter)
	let stamp = emitConst(module, block: block, value: state.rawValue, ids: ids)

	module.appendOp(block, op: StdOp.storeRelease(span, offset: MspanOwningPOffset, valueId: stamp, valueType: StdType.i64))
end 'emitStoreSpanOwnerState'

// `__slab_refill(classIdx, myP, row) -> span` — put a span with free slots in `row`'s mcache entry, stamp it
// as `myP`'s, and answer it.
//
// Two tiers: take the head of `mcentral[classIdx]` if there is one, else cut a fresh span out of the
// arena. Both ends install into the mcache and stamp the owner.
//
// ⭐⭐ **THE WHOLE BODY RUNS UNDER `__slab_lock`, AND EVERYTHING IT CALLS INHERITS THAT.** It reads and
// rewrites an mcentral head, drives the metadata slab, cuts chunks out of the arena and registers pages in
// the reverse map — four structures every P shares. The fast path above it takes nothing.
//
// ⚠ **`myP` AND THE ROW's MCACHE BASE ARE PARAMETERS RATHER THAN RE-DERIVED.** Re-clamping the id to a row
// here would make the caller's row and the refill's row two computations of one fact — and if they ever
// disagreed the span would be stamped for one P and installed in another's row, which is precisely the
// corruption the ownership gate exists to catch. One derivation, in `__slab_alloc`, passed down.
function buildSlabRefill(module StdModule, model MachineModel)
	let func = IrFunction.createFromStd(SlabRefillName, returnType: StdReturnType.value(StdType.i64), paramCount: 3, paramTypes: threeI64Params(), needsGreenThreadStackGuard: true, pinsItsThread: true)
	let ids = ValueMinter.create(3)

	let entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	let popPartial = module.addBlock(func, label: b"poppartial", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	let takePartial = module.addBlock(func, label: b"partial", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	let unlock = module.addBlock(func, label: b"unlock", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	let grow = module.addBlock(func, label: b"grow", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	let emptyClass = module.addBlock(func, label: b"emptyclass", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	let cut = module.addBlock(func, label: b"cut", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	let install = module.addBlock(func, label: b"install", heat: BlockHeat.normal)

	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 3)
	let classIdx = 0
	let myP = 1 as ValueId
	let mcacheRow = 2

	let state = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.call(state, callee: SlabStateName, args: ValueIdArray.create(0)))
	let depthAddr = ids.mint()
	let locked = emitSlabLockDepthAddr(module, func: func, cur: entry, state: state, model: model, addrId: depthAddr, ids: ids, label: b"depth")
	emitSlabLockEnter(module, block: locked, state: state, depthAddr: depthAddr, ids: ids)
	let mcentralSlot = emitStateSlotAddr(module, block: locked, state: state, arrayOffset: SlabStateMcentralOffset as FullRangeInt, index: classIdx, ids: ids)
	let mcacheSlot = emitMcacheSlotAddr(module, block: locked, mcacheRow: mcacheRow, classIdx: classIdx, ids: ids)
	let none = emitConst(module, block: locked, value: 0, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(locked, op: StdOp.branch(popPartial.id))

	// The head is taken and used whatever state it is in, and a fully free one is NOT destroyed here. It is
	// the best span this caller could be handed — warm, registered, header live — and destroying it to cut a
	// replacement out of the arena costs a first-fit scan over every arena's bitmap, a reverse-map
	// registration per page and a memzero, to arrive at what was just thrown away. Reclamation belongs to
	// the spans nobody is about to use, which is `__slab_scavenge`'s walk and the only road to it.
	let partial = emitLoadField(module, block: popPartial, record: mcentralSlot, offset: 0, ids: ids)
	emitBranchOnCompareValues(module, block: popPartial, a: partial, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, b: none, whenTrue: grow, whenFalse: takePartial, ids: ids)

	let following = emitLoadField(module, block: takePartial, record: partial, offset: MspanNextSpanOffset, ids: ids)
	emitStoreField(module, block: takePartial, record: mcentralSlot, offset: 0, value: following)
	emitStoreField(module, block: takePartial, record: partial, offset: MspanNextSpanOffset, value: none)
	module.appendOp(takePartial, op: StdOp.call(ids.mint(), callee: SlabCollectRemoteName, args: argsOf(partial)))
	emitBranchCarrying(module, from: takePartial, to: install, values: argsOf(partial))

	let geom = emitClassGeometry(module, block: grow, classIdx: classIdx, ids: ids)
	let slotSize = emitGeomSlotSize(module, block: grow, geom: geom, ids: ids)
	let totalSlots = emitGeomTotalSlots(module, block: grow, geom: geom, ids: ids)

	// Class 0 is the ladder's sentinel: size 0, no objects. Only a zero-byte request reaches it, and it can
	// only reach it through the class lookup, so this is where such a request is refused — on the COLD path,
	// which is why the class lookup needs no guard of its own.
	emitBranchOnCompare(module, block: grow, value: totalSlots, pred: StdCmpPred.lt, against: 1, whenTrue: emptyClass, whenFalse: cut, ids: ids)

	emitRuntimeAbort(module, func: func, block: emptyClass, abort: RuntimeAbort.slabClassServesNoObject, ids: ids)

	let numChunks = emitSpanChunkCount(module, block: cut, slotSize: slotSize, totalSlots: totalSlots, ids: ids)
	let pageBase = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(cut, op: StdOp.call(pageBase, callee: SlabArenaAllocChunksName, args: argsOf(numChunks)))

	let span = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(cut, op: StdOp.call(span, callee: SlabMetaAllocName, args: ValueIdArray.create(0)))
	emitStoreField(module, block: cut, record: span, offset: MspanBaseAddrOffset, value: pageBase)
	emitStoreField(module, block: cut, record: span, offset: MspanFreeListOffset, value: none)
	emitStoreField(module, block: cut, record: span, offset: MspanFreeCountOffset, value: totalSlots)
	emitStoreField(module, block: cut, record: span, offset: MspanNextSpanOffset, value: none)
	emitStoreField(module, block: cut, record: span, offset: MspanClassIdxOffset, value: classIdx)
	emitStoreField(module, block: cut, record: span, offset: MspanBumpNextOffset, value: pageBase)
	emitStoreField(module, block: cut, record: span, offset: MspanRemoteHeadOffset, value: none)
	emitStoreField(module, block: cut, record: span, offset: MspanRemoteCountOffset, value: none)

	// Every page of the run maps to this span, which is what lets a free of a pointer anywhere INSIDE it
	// find its owner (`__slab_arena_map_set`). Chunks and pages are the same 8 KiB unit.
	module.appendOp(cut, op: StdOp.call(ids.mint(), callee: SlabArenaMapSetName, args: threeArgs(pageBase, second: numChunks, third: span)))
	emitBranchCarrying(module, from: cut, to: install, values: argsOf(span))

	let installed = ids.mint()
	install.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(installed, argType: StdType.i64))
	emitStoreField(module, block: install, record: mcacheSlot, offset: 0, value: installed)

	// The owner is stamped here and nowhere else. A span off the partial list carries
	// `SpanOwnerState.partial` and a freshly cut one carries whatever its recycled header held, so
	// both arms need it.
	//
	// ⚠ It is a release, and the mcache entry above it a plain store. A freer that observes this stamp must
	// see the free list and the free count the arms above wrote; observing a stale free list through a fresh
	// owner hands one slot to two callers.
	module.appendOp(install, op: StdOp.storeRelease(installed, offset: MspanOwningPOffset, valueId: myP, valueType: StdType.i64))
	module.setTerminator(install, op: StdOp.branch(unlock.id))

	emitSlabLockLeave(module, block: unlock, state: state, depthAddr: depthAddr, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(unlock, op: StdOp.ret(installed))

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildSlabRefill'

// **PROBE `row`'s CACHED SPAN, REFILL ON A MISS AND POP A SLOT**, emitted INTO the caller's blocks and
// answering in `slotId`, the returned block's single argument.
//
// ⚠ **NOTHING HERE READS THE STATE REGION'S BASE.** The mcache row and the P arrive already resolved, so
// the pop names no word of it — which is what lets the caller drop that address at the shard arms instead of
// holding a register across every hit.
//
// ⚠ **NOTHING HERE TAKES THE LOCK.** Every word this emits belongs to a span `myP` solely owns
// (`emitSlabProbe`'s ownership gate is what establishes that) or to `row`'s own mcache entry. The one caller
// whose row is NOT solely owned — a thread on the shared raw row — is serialised by `buildSlabAllocEntry`
// before it ever gets here.
function emitSlabAllocClass(module StdModule, func IrFunction, cur IrBlock, classIdx ValueId, myP ValueId, mcacheRow ValueId, zeroed bool, slotId ValueId, ids ValueMinter, label ByteArray) returns IrBlock
	// The miss path is cold, and what that buys is the hit path's registers. A value live across
	// `__slab_refill` in a NORMAL block has to sit in a callee-saved register, so every hit pays the push
	// and the pop for a call it never makes; in a cold block the bracket saves it to a slot instead
	// (`RegisterAllocator`'s cold-run rule) and the hit path is free to use the whole pool.
	let refill = module.addBlock(func, label: label, heat: BlockHeat.cold)
	let ready = module.addBlock(func, label: label, heat: BlockHeat.normal)

	let span = ids.mint()
	ready.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(span, argType: StdType.i64))

	let mcacheSlot = emitMcacheSlotAddr(module, block: cur, mcacheRow: mcacheRow, classIdx: classIdx, ids: ids)

	let cached = emitLoadField(module, block: cur, record: mcacheSlot, offset: 0, ids: ids)
	emitSlabProbe(module, func: func, cur: cur, span: cached, owner: SlabProbeOwner.holder(myP), hit: ready, hitArgs: argsOf(cached), miss: refill, ids: ids, label: label)

	let fresh = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(refill, op: StdOp.call(fresh, callee: SlabRefillName, args: threeArgs(classIdx, second: myP, third: mcacheRow)))
	emitBranchCarrying(module, from: refill, to: ready, values: argsOf(fresh))

	return emitSlabPop(module, func: func, ready: ready, span: span, classIdx: classIdx, evict: SlabEvictSlot.address(mcacheSlot), zeroed: zeroed, abortRoad: SlabAbortRoad.returnsTheDoor, slotId: slotId, ids: ids, label: label)
end 'emitSlabAllocClass'

union SlabEvictSlot
	address(slot ValueId)
	ofOwner
end 'SlabEvictSlot'

enum SlabAbortRoad
	returnsTheDoor
	endsInline
end 'SlabAbortRoad'

function emitSlabAbort(module StdModule, func IrFunction, block IrBlock, road SlabAbortRoad, callee ByteArray, args ValueIdArray, ids ValueMinter)
	match road 'road'
		returnsTheDoor then emitCallAndReturn(module, block: block, callee: callee, args: args, ids: ids)
		endsInline then emitCallThenDead(module, func: func, block: block, callee: callee, args: args, ids: ids)
	end 'road'
end 'emitSlabAbort'

function emitCallThenDead(module StdModule, func IrFunction, block IrBlock, callee ByteArray, args ValueIdArray, ids ValueMinter)
	module.appendOp(block, op: StdOp.call(ids.mint(), callee: callee, args: args))
	emitDeadTerminator(module, func: func, block: block, ids: ids)
end 'emitCallThenDead'

type SlabEvictRoute
	export var entry as IrBlock
	export let slot as ValueId

	static function create(entry IrBlock, slot ValueId) returns Self
		return Self{entry: entry, slot: slot}
	end 'create'
end 'SlabEvictRoute'

// **POP A SLOT FROM `span`**, emitted INTO the caller's blocks and answering in `slotId`. The full body
// (`emitSlabAllocClass`) and the leaf (`emitSlabFastAlloc`) both emit it; `evict` is where the entry that a
// span's last slot clears is found: handed over as an address by the first, read through the span's owner
// by the second.
//
// ⭐⭐ **THE TWO ARMS ARE THE DESIGN.** A RECYCLED slot comes off the free list and is DIRTY, so the
// zeroing build fills it; a VIRGIN slot is carved off the bump cursor and still holds the OS's zeroes, so it
// costs nothing at any size class. That asymmetry is the whole reason the bump region exists — see the
// file header.
//
// ⭐ **`zeroed` IS A BUILD-TIME ARGUMENT, NOT A RUNTIME `needzero` PARAMETER.** Threading a flag through
// the allocator would put a branch on the hot path of every allocation in the language and an extra
// argument in every call. Here the two spellings are two EMISSIONS of one emitter,
// into the entry doors that differ by exactly that bit, so no program carries the arm it never takes and no
// allocation tests for it.
function emitSlabPop(module StdModule, func IrFunction, ready IrBlock, span ValueId, classIdx ValueId, evict SlabEvictSlot, zeroed bool, abortRoad SlabAbortRoad, slotId ValueId, ids ValueMinter, label ByteArray) returns IrBlock
	let recycled = module.addBlock(func, label: label, heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	let virgin = module.addBlock(func, label: label, heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	let handOut = module.addBlock(func, label: label, heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	let exhausted = module.addBlock(func, label: label, heat: BlockHeat.cold)
	let consumed = module.addBlock(func, label: label, heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	let unlink = module.addBlock(func, label: label, heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	let done = module.addBlock(func, label: label, heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	done.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(slotId, argType: StdType.i64))

	let slotSize = emitClassGeometryHalf(module, block: ready, classIdx: classIdx, offset: SlabClassGeomSlotSizeOffset, ids: ids)
	let freeList = emitLoadField(module, block: ready, record: span, offset: MspanFreeListOffset, ids: ids)
	emitBranchOnCompare(module, block: ready, value: freeList, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, against: 0, whenTrue: virgin, whenFalse: recycled, ids: ids)

	// POP. The link lives in the dead slot's first word, so unlinking is one load and one store — and the
	// fill that follows subsumes the link, which is why there is no separate first-word clear.
	let following = emitLoadField(module, block: recycled, record: freeList, offset: 0, ids: ids)
	emitStoreField(module, block: recycled, record: span, offset: MspanFreeListOffset, value: following)

	if zeroed 'restoreTheZeroingContract'
		module.appendOp(recycled, op: StdOp.memFill(freeList, byteCountId: slotSize, value: 0))
	end 'restoreTheZeroingContract'

	emitBranchCarrying(module, from: recycled, to: consumed, values: argsOf(freeList))

	// CARVE. INV-2 says `bump_end == base_addr + slot_size * total_slots`, derived here rather than stored;
	// INV-1 says `free_count > 0` with an empty free list implies `bump_next < bump_end`.
	let bumpNext = emitLoadField(module, block: virgin, record: span, offset: MspanBumpNextOffset, ids: ids)
	let totalSlots = emitClassGeometryHalf(module, block: virgin, classIdx: emitLoadField(module, block: virgin, record: span, offset: MspanClassIdxOffset, ids: ids), offset: SlabClassGeomSlotsOffset, ids: ids)
	let regionBytes = emitBin(module, block: virgin, a: slotSize, b: totalSlots, opcode: StdBinOpcode.mul, ids: ids)
	let baseAddr = emitLoadField(module, block: virgin, record: span, offset: MspanBaseAddrOffset, ids: ids)
	let bumpEnd = emitBin(module, block: virgin, a: baseAddr, b: regionBytes, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)

	// ⚠ **INV-1 TRAPS LOUDLY.** Handing out a slot past the span's end writes into whatever the arena placed
	// in the next chunk, and the corruption surfaces somewhere else, much later, as a slot handed out twice.
	//
	// ⭐ **IT IS ALSO WHERE TWO OS THREADS SHARING ONE mcache ROW SURFACE**: both pop the same span's
	// cursor and one runs past the end — exit 86 (`__slab_past_end`) rather than a fault somewhere
	// downstream.
	emitBranchOnCompareValues(module, block: virgin, a: bumpNext, pred: StdCmpPred.ge, b: bumpEnd, whenTrue: exhausted, whenFalse: handOut, ids: ids)

	emitSlabAbort(module, func: func, block: exhausted, road: abortRoad, callee: SlabPastEndName, args: argsOf(slotSize), ids: ids)

	let nextBump = emitBin(module, block: handOut, a: bumpNext, b: slotSize, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	emitStoreField(module, block: handOut, record: span, offset: MspanBumpNextOffset, value: nextBump)
	emitBranchCarrying(module, from: handOut, to: consumed, values: argsOf(bumpNext))

	let slot = ids.mint()
	consumed.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(slot, argType: StdType.i64))

	// Both arms consumed exactly one slot, which is the other half of INV-1.
	let live = emitLoadField(module, block: consumed, record: span, offset: MspanFreeCountOffset, ids: ids)
	let one = emitConst(module, block: consumed, value: 1, ids: ids)
	let remaining = emitBin(module, block: consumed, a: live, b: one, opcode: StdBinOpcode.sub, ids: ids)
	emitStoreField(module, block: consumed, record: span, offset: MspanFreeCountOffset, value: remaining)

	let route = emitSlabEvictRoute(module, func: func, how: evict, abortRoad: abortRoad, unlink: unlink, span: span, ids: ids, label: label)
	emitBranchOnCompare(module, block: consumed, value: remaining, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, against: 0, whenTrue: route.entry, whenFalse: done, ids: ids)
	pushBranchEdge(consumed, to: done, values: argsOf(slot))

	// The span leaves the cache and its owner in one step. The store that publishes `Full` is what the
	// first remote free into it tests for, and it must not be observable before the mcache entry is clear:
	// a freer that saw `Full` while the entry still pointed here would list a span this processor is about
	// to pop from again.
	let uncached = emitConst(module, block: unlink, value: 0, ids: ids)
	emitStoreField(module, block: unlink, record: route.slot, offset: 0, value: uncached)
	emitStoreSpanOwnerState(module, block: unlink, span: span, state: SpanOwnerState.full, ids: ids)
	emitBranchCarrying(module, from: unlink, to: done, values: argsOf(slot))

	return done
end 'emitSlabPop'

function emitSlabEvictRoute(module StdModule, func IrFunction, how SlabEvictSlot, abortRoad SlabAbortRoad, unlink IrBlock, span ValueId, ids ValueMinter, label ByteArray) returns SlabEvictRoute
	match how 'how'
		address(slot) then return SlabEvictRoute.create(unlink, slot: slot)
		ofOwner then return emitSlabEvictOwnerRoute(module, func: func, abortRoad: abortRoad, unlink: unlink, span: span, ids: ids, label: label)
	end 'how'
end 'emitSlabEvictRoute'

function emitSlabEvictOwnerRoute(module StdModule, func IrFunction, abortRoad SlabAbortRoad, unlink IrBlock, span ValueId, ids ValueMinter, label ByteArray) returns SlabEvictRoute
	let lookup = module.addBlock(func, label: label, heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	let holder = module.addBlock(func, label: label, heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	let rawRow = module.addBlock(func, label: label, heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	let procRow = module.addBlock(func, label: label, heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	let unowned = module.addBlock(func, label: label, heat: BlockHeat.cold)

	let slot = ids.mint()
	unlink.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(slot, argType: StdType.i64))

	let owner = emitLoadAcquireField(module, block: lookup, record: span, offset: MspanOwningPOffset, ids: ids)
	let classIdx = emitLoadField(module, block: lookup, record: span, offset: MspanClassIdxOffset, ids: ids)
	let state = emitSlabStateCommitted(module, block: lookup, ids: ids)
	emitBranchOnCompare(module, block: lookup, value: owner, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, against: SlabNoProcessor, whenTrue: rawRow, whenFalse: holder, ids: ids)

	emitBranchOnCompare(module, block: holder, value: owner, pred: StdCmpPred.lt, against: SlabNoProcessor, whenTrue: unowned, whenFalse: procRow, ids: ids)
	emitSlabAbort(module, func: func, block: unowned, road: abortRoad, callee: SlabEvictUnownedName, args: argsOf(owner), ids: ids)

	let rawBase = emitMcacheRowAt(module, block: rawRow, state: state, row: SlabRawShardRow, ids: ids)
	emitBranchCarrying(module, from: rawRow, to: unlink, values: argsOf(emitMcacheSlotAddr(module, block: rawRow, mcacheRow: rawBase, classIdx: classIdx, ids: ids)))

	let id = emitLoadField(module, block: procRow, record: owner, offset: POffId, ids: ids)
	let procBase = emitMcacheRowBase(module, block: procRow, state: state, row: id, ids: ids)
	emitBranchCarrying(module, from: procRow, to: unlink, values: argsOf(emitMcacheSlotAddr(module, block: procRow, mcacheRow: procBase, classIdx: classIdx, ids: ids)))

	return SlabEvictRoute.create(lookup, slot: slot)
end 'emitSlabEvictOwnerRoute'

// An abort in its own function, reached by tail jump: an inline abort is a call, and a call in a door's hit
// path stops it being a leaf.
function buildSlabAbortDoor(module StdModule, name ByteArray, abort RuntimeAbort)
	let func = IrFunction.createFromStd(name, returnType: StdReturnType.value(StdType.i64), paramCount: 1, paramTypes: oneI64Param())
	let ids = ValueMinter.create(1)

	let entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry", heat: BlockHeat.normal)

	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 1)
	emitRuntimeAbort(module, func: func, block: entry, abort: abort, ids: ids)

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildSlabAbortDoor'

function emitClassGeometryHalf(module StdModule, block IrBlock, classIdx ValueId, offset ByteOffset, ids ValueMinter) returns ValueId
	let base = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(block, op: StdOp.rdataAddr(base, label: SlabClassGeomLabelBytes))
	let entry = emitWordSlotAddr(module, block: block, base: base, index: classIdx, ids: ids)
	let half = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(block, op: StdOp.loadIndirect(half, addrId: entry, offset: offset, valueType: StdType.u32))

	return half
end 'emitClassGeometryHalf'

// ⭐⭐ **ONE PROBE OF THE mcache ENTRY — the three questions that decide hit from miss, spelled ONCE.**
// Two copies of a three-part predicate is two places for the third part to go missing, and the third part
// is the one that prevents heap corruption.
//
// The questions, in increasing cost:
//   1. **Is there a span at all?** The entry is 0 before the first refill and after an eviction.
//   2. **Has it any free slots?** A span whose last slot went out is evicted below, so this can only be a
//      span this row never consumed from — it is still a miss, and testing it costs one load either way.
//   3. **Do I own it?** ⭐ THE ONE THE OTHER TWO CANNOT ANSWER. A span recycled to another P keeps
//      `free_count != 0`, so question 2 reads it as a hit; popping it would mutate a free list whose sole
//      writer is somebody else. Refill instead — which is a MISS, not an error.
//
// The ownership load is an acquire, though a hit means the stamp is this P's own. A P is handed between OS
// threads, so the store this load observes need not have been made by the thread making it; the edge is what
// puts the pop's free-list and bump reads after the verdict. Without it a hit may read the free list the
// span carried before the refill that installed it.
//
// ⚠ **THIS EDGE CARRIES A BLOCK ARGUMENT OFF A CONDITIONAL, WHICH `SlabArena.maxon` DELIBERATELY NEVER
// DOES — and the divergence is deliberate, not an oversight.** That file routes every arg-carrying edge
// through an extra single-successor block, on the stated grounds that it keeps the edge safe. It is safe
// either way: `splitCriticalEdges` runs before coloring and splits exactly this shape (a pred with two
// successors into a succ with two predecessors), so the extra block is BUILT rather than written. Writing
// it by hand duplicates a pass, and a hand-written one is a second place for the edge's arguments to be
// wrong.
//
// `miss` is the refill in the full body and the bail in a leaf. The third question compares the owner
// word with the carried P, or with `SlabNoProcessor` for the lone thread of a program with no scheduler.
function emitSlabProbe(module StdModule, func IrFunction, cur IrBlock, span ValueId, owner SlabProbeOwner, hit IrBlock, hitArgs ValueIdArray, miss IrBlock, ids ValueMinter, label ByteArray, heat BlockHeat = BlockHeat.normal)
	let depleted = module.addBlock(func, label: label, heat: heat)
	let owned = module.addBlock(func, label: label, heat: heat)

	emitBranchOnCompare(module, block: cur, value: span, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, against: 0, whenTrue: miss, whenFalse: depleted, ids: ids)

	let freeCount = emitLoadField(module, block: depleted, record: span, offset: MspanFreeCountOffset, ids: ids)
	emitBranchOnCompare(module, block: depleted, value: freeCount, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, against: 0, whenTrue: miss, whenFalse: owned, ids: ids)

	let ownerWord = emitLoadAcquireField(module, block: owned, record: span, offset: MspanOwningPOffset, ids: ids)
	emitSlabOwnerGate(module, block: owned, ownerWord: ownerWord, holder: owner, bail: miss, hit: hit, ids: ids)
	pushBranchEdge(owned, to: hit, values: hitArgs)
end 'emitSlabProbe'

// `__slab_alloc(size) -> ptr`, its tracked and non-zeroing siblings and the scaffold door.
//
// ⭐ **`zeroed` DECIDES WHETHER A RECYCLED SLOT IS ZEROED**, which is what lets one builder serve
// every door. It is BUILD-time, so it costs no branch and no argument at run time.
//
// ⭐⭐ **THE SIZE TEST IS FIRST, AND EVERY OTHER SETUP SITS BEHIND IT.** The two tiers share only the
// request and the credit their row owes: an OS-direct request has no class, no mcache row and no lock, so
// the state probe, the shard walk and the routing values all belong on the road that reads them. Ahead of
// the test they are work the OS-direct road throws away — and, live across the test, they are the registers
// that push the hit path's frame past the callee-saved pool and into spill slots.
//
// ⭐⭐ **THE SMALL ROAD'S ORDER IS: CLASS, STATE, SHARD, COUNT, LOCK, POP.** The class index is answered off
// `size` alone, so it runs before anything reads the state region; the credit is taken in the arm that knows
// the row, and the lock a serialised thread takes is taken there too, so the pop needs no test of its own.
//
// ⭐ **ON A COUNTED OR TRACKED DOOR, BOTH TIERS CREDIT THEIR ROW — the door's own columns, the raw trio for
// a counted door and the tracked trio for a tracked one** — a request served from a span
// and one served by its own mapping are one allocation each — and both reach the credit through
// `__slab_state_base`, so the FIRST allocation of a process commits the region whichever tier it
// lands on.
//
// ⭐⭐ **THIS BODY IS A DOOR'S FULL PATH: the class lookup, the shard walk, the state region and the pop,
// with a call left only where the path is COLD.** What survives as a `call` is exactly what a hit never
// reaches — `__slab_os_direct_alloc` (a mapping of its own), `__slab_state_base`'s commit (once per
// process) and `__slab_refill` (a miss). Everything else is emitted here, so an allocation that hits its
// mcache entry enters ONE frame and leaves it.
//
// ⭐⭐ **A LEAF SERVES THE HIT AND THIS BODY SERVES THE REST.** `buildSlabAllocDoor` builds the leaf under
// the door's name and this body as `<door>_slow`; the leaf tail-jumps here with its original request for
// anything it cannot finish, and this body walks and probes again from the top. A tracked door in an
// untraced build has no leaf of its own — `__mm_alloc` carries it — and this body keeps the door's name.
//
// ⭐⭐ **THIS IS WHERE "WHICH P AM I" IS ANSWERED, ONCE PER ALLOCATION, AND HANDED DOWN.** The pop and
// `__slab_refill` both need the P and its row and neither re-derives them. Reading the TLS slot in the
// entry and again in the class allocator, clamping in both, would be one fact computed three times, with
// three chances for two of them to disagree about which row a span was stamped for.
//
// ⭐⭐ **AND WHERE THE SHARED RAW ROW IS SERIALISED.** `SlabRawShardRow` belongs to every OS thread that owns
// no P — a subprocess worker, the system monitor — and to any P clamped onto it, so the "sole writer"
// argument the lockless pop rests on does not hold there. Taking the lock around the whole class-alloc is
// what restores it. ⚠ Only ONCE A SCHEDULER EXISTS: before that the raw row belongs to the lone main
// thread, which `emitSlabResolveShard`'s first arm is.
//
// ⚠ **THE LOCK IS RECURSIVE AND THAT IS LOAD-BEARING**: the pop under it can miss
// and call `__slab_refill`, which takes the same lock again. A non-recursive mutex here is a deadlock, not a
// performance question.
//
// ⭐⭐ **THE LOCK IS TAKEN AND RELEASED AROUND ONE COPY OF THE POP, NOT AROUND TWO — `heldDepth` IS WHY.**
// Serialised and sole-writer differ in exactly one thing: whether `__slab_lock` is held across the pop. With
// the pop emitted inline, spelling that as two arms would emit the body TWICE per entry door for a
// difference of two ops. The two arms therefore converge on one `routed` block carrying the depth word this
// call owes a step down at — `SlabNoLockDepth` from the sole writer — and the release reads that one value
// for both halves of the question. That is the honest form of it: *"did I take it"* and *"whose depth word"*
// are one fact about this call, and a flag beside the address would be two values across the pop for it.
function buildSlabAllocEntry(module StdModule, name ByteArray, zeroed bool, counting SlabCounting, model MachineModel)
	let func = IrFunction.createFromStd(name, returnType: StdReturnType.value(StdType.i64), paramCount: 1, paramTypes: oneI64Param())
	let ids = ValueMinter.create(1)

	let entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	let small = module.addBlock(func, label: b"small", heat: BlockHeat.normal)

	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 1)
	let size = 0 as ValueId

	let class = ids.mint()
	let classReady = emitSlabClassIndex(module, func: func, cur: small, size: size, classId: class, ids: ids, label: b"cls")

	let state = ids.mint()
	let stateReady = emitSlabStateBase(module, func: func, cur: classReady, stateId: state, ids: ids, label: b"state")

	let arms = emitSlabResolveShard(module, func: func, cur: stateReady, state: state, model: model, need: SlabShardNeed.routed, traffic: slabThreadTrafficOf(counting, traffic: ThreadTraffic.alloc(size)), ids: ids, label: b"shard")
	let soleRoute = slabShardRoute(arms.sole)
	let serialRoute = slabShardRoute(arms.serial)

	let routed = module.addBlock(func, label: b"routed", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	let myP = ids.mint()
	let mcacheRow = ids.mint()
	let heldDepth = ids.mint()
	routed.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(myP, argType: StdType.i64))
	routed.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(mcacheRow, argType: StdType.i64))
	routed.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(heldDepth, argType: StdType.i64))

	emitSlabCountingCredit(module, block: arms.sole.block, rowBase: arms.sole.trafficRow, event: SlabTrafficEvent.alloc(size), atomic: false, counting: counting, ids: ids)
	emitBranchCarrying(module, from: arms.sole.block, to: routed, values: threeArgs(soleRoute.p, second: soleRoute.mcacheRow, third: soleRoute.depthAddr))

	emitSlabCountingCredit(module, block: arms.serial.block, rowBase: arms.serial.trafficRow, event: SlabTrafficEvent.alloc(size), atomic: true, counting: counting, ids: ids)
	emitSlabLockEnter(module, block: arms.serial.block, state: state, depthAddr: serialRoute.depthAddr, ids: ids)
	emitBranchCarrying(module, from: arms.serial.block, to: routed, values: threeArgs(serialRoute.p, second: serialRoute.mcacheRow, third: serialRoute.depthAddr))

	let slot = ids.mint()
	let served = emitSlabAllocClass(module, func: func, cur: routed, classIdx: class, myP: myP, mcacheRow: mcacheRow, zeroed: zeroed, slotId: slot, ids: ids, label: b"pop")

	let release = module.addBlock(func, label: b"release", heat: BlockHeat.cold)
	let handBack = module.addBlock(func, label: b"handback", heat: BlockHeat.normal)

	emitBranchOnCompare(module, block: served, value: heldDepth, pred: StdCmpPred.ne, against: SlabNoLockDepth, whenTrue: release, whenFalse: handBack, ids: ids)

	// ⭐ **THE REGION IS RE-READ HERE RATHER THAN CARRIED.** This block is its only reader past the arms, so
	// carrying it would hold a register across every hit the sole-writer path serves for one `.data` load on
	// the serialised one. The word is non-zero by construction: this call committed it on its way in.
	let committed = emitSlabStateCommitted(module, block: release, ids: ids)
	emitSlabLockLeave(module, block: release, state: committed, depthAddr: heldDepth, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(release, op: StdOp.ret(slot))

	module.setTerminator(handBack, op: StdOp.ret(slot))

	// ⭐ **THE OS-DIRECT ROAD IS BUILT LAST AND THE SIZE TEST IS SPELLED AFTER IT, SO ITS BLOCKS LAND PAST
	// THE HIT PATH** rather than between the entry and the class lookup every small request runs through.
	let osDirect = module.addBlock(func, label: b"osdirect", heat: BlockHeat.cold)
	let oversized = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(osDirect, op: StdOp.call(oversized, callee: SlabDoors.of(counting).allocOversize, args: argsOf(size)))
	module.setTerminator(osDirect, op: StdOp.ret(oversized))

	emitSlabSizeRouting(module, block: entry, size: size, osDirect: osDirect, small: small, ids: ids)

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildSlabAllocEntry'

let SlabSlowDoorSuffix = b"_slow"

function slabSlowDoorName(door ByteArray) returns immutable ByteArray
	return freeze "{String.from(door)}{String.from(SlabSlowDoorSuffix)}".toByteArray()
end 'slabSlowDoorName'

union SlabProbeOwner
	loneThread
	holder(p ValueId)
end 'SlabProbeOwner'

function slabRawMcacheRowDisp() returns ByteOffset
	return (slabStateMcacheOffset() + SlabRawShardRow * slabMcacheRowStride()) as ByteOffset
end 'slabRawMcacheRowDisp'

function emitSlabFastWalk(module StdModule, func IrFunction, cur IrBlock, state ValueId, model MachineModel, bail IrBlock, ids ValueMinter, label ByteArray) returns SlabRowDecision
	let decision = emitSlabRowDecision(module, func: func, cur: cur, state: state, model: model, traffic: ThreadTraffic.none, ids: ids, label: label, heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	emitSlabSerialBails(module, serial: decision.serial, bail: bail)

	return decision
end 'emitSlabFastWalk'

function emitSlabSerialBails(module StdModule, serial SlabSerialArm, bail IrBlock)
	match serial 'serial'
		absent then return
		present(exits) then emitSlabSerialExitsBail(module, exits: exits, bail: bail)
	end 'serial'
end 'emitSlabSerialBails'

function emitSlabSerialExitsBail(module StdModule, exits SlabSerialExits, bail IrBlock)
	module.setTerminator(exits.noMachine, op: StdOp.branch(bail.id))
	module.setTerminator(exits.noProc, op: StdOp.branch(bail.id))
	module.setTerminator(exits.clamped, op: StdOp.branch(bail.id))
end 'emitSlabSerialExitsBail'

function emitSlabOwnerGate(module StdModule, block IrBlock, ownerWord ValueId, holder SlabProbeOwner, bail IrBlock, hit IrBlock, ids ValueMinter)
	match holder 'holder'
		loneThread then emitBranchOnCompare(module, block: block, value: ownerWord, pred: StdCmpPred.ne, against: SlabNoProcessor, whenTrue: bail, whenFalse: hit, ids: ids)
		holder(p) then emitBranchOnCompareValues(module, block: block, a: ownerWord, pred: StdCmpPred.ne, b: p, whenTrue: bail, whenFalse: hit, ids: ids)
	end 'holder'
end 'emitSlabOwnerGate'

function emitSlabFastProbe(module StdModule, func IrFunction, cur IrBlock, rowBase ValueId, rowDisp ByteOffset, classIdx ValueId, owner SlabProbeOwner, bail IrBlock, spanId ValueId, ids ValueMinter, label ByteArray) returns IrBlock
	let hit = module.addBlock(func, label: label, heat: BlockHeat.normal)

	let slotAddr = emitWordSlotAddr(module, block: cur, base: rowBase, index: classIdx, ids: ids)
	module.appendOp(cur, op: StdOp.loadIndirect(spanId, addrId: slotAddr, offset: rowDisp, valueType: StdType.i64))
	emitSlabProbe(module, func: func, cur: cur, span: spanId, owner: owner, hit: hit, hitArgs: ValueIdArray.create(0), miss: bail, ids: ids, label: label)

	return hit
end 'emitSlabFastProbe'

function emitSlabFastAllocUnscheduled(module StdModule, func IrFunction, block IrBlock, state ValueId, classIdx ValueId, size ValueId, counting SlabCounting, bail IrBlock, pop IrBlock, ids ValueMinter)
	let span = ids.mint()
	let probed = emitSlabFastProbe(module, func: func, cur: block, rowBase: state, rowDisp: slabRawMcacheRowDisp(), classIdx: classIdx, owner: SlabProbeOwner.loneThread, bail: bail, spanId: span, ids: ids, label: b"probe")

	let trafficRow = emitSlabTrafficRowAt(module, block: probed, state: state, row: SlabRawShardRow, ids: ids)
	emitSlabCountingCredit(module, block: probed, rowBase: trafficRow, event: SlabTrafficEvent.alloc(size), atomic: false, counting: counting, ids: ids)
	emitBranchCarrying(module, from: probed, to: pop, values: argsOf(span))
end 'emitSlabFastAllocUnscheduled'

function emitSlabFastAllocOwn(module StdModule, func IrFunction, row SlabOwnRow, state ValueId, classIdx ValueId, size ValueId, counting SlabCounting, bail IrBlock, pop IrBlock, ids ValueMinter)
	let rowBase = emitMcacheRowBase(module, block: row.block, state: state, row: row.id, ids: ids)
	let span = ids.mint()
	let probed = emitSlabFastProbe(module, func: func, cur: row.block, rowBase: rowBase, rowDisp: slabStateMcacheOffset() as ByteOffset, classIdx: classIdx, owner: SlabProbeOwner.holder(row.p), bail: bail, spanId: span, ids: ids, label: b"ownprobe")
	let stepped = emitThreadTrafficStep(module, func: func, cur: probed, m: row.machine, traffic: slabThreadTrafficOf(counting, traffic: ThreadTraffic.alloc(size)), ids: ids, label: b"ownthread", heat: BlockHeat.normal)

	let trafficRow = emitSlabTrafficRowBase(module, block: stepped, state: state, row: row.id, ids: ids)
	emitSlabCountingCredit(module, block: stepped, rowBase: trafficRow, event: SlabTrafficEvent.alloc(size), atomic: false, counting: counting, ids: ids)
	emitBranchCarrying(module, from: stepped, to: pop, values: argsOf(span))
end 'emitSlabFastAllocOwn'

function emitSlabFastAllocOwnArm(module StdModule, func IrFunction, arm SlabOwnArm, state ValueId, classIdx ValueId, size ValueId, counting SlabCounting, bail IrBlock, pop IrBlock, ids ValueMinter)
	match arm 'arm'
		absent then return
		present(row) then emitSlabFastAllocOwn(module, func: func, row: row, state: state, classIdx: classIdx, size: size, counting: counting, bail: bail, pop: pop, ids: ids)
	end 'arm'
end 'emitSlabFastAllocOwnArm'

// The hit path as a leaf. Only loads precede the probe's verdict and the row is credited after it, so a
// bail has stored nothing and the full body that takes over credits the request once.
function emitSlabFastAlloc(module StdModule, func IrFunction, cur IrBlock, size ValueId, zeroed bool, counting SlabCounting, model MachineModel, abortRoad SlabAbortRoad, bail IrBlock, slotId ValueId, ids ValueMinter) returns IrBlock
	let small = module.addBlock(func, label: b"small", heat: BlockHeat.normal)

	emitSlabSizeRouting(module, block: cur, size: size, osDirect: bail, small: small, ids: ids)

	let class = ids.mint()
	let classReady = emitSlabClassIndex(module, func: func, cur: small, size: size, classId: class, ids: ids, label: b"cls")

	let state = ids.mint()
	let stateReady = emitSlabStateBase(module, func: func, cur: classReady, stateId: state, ids: ids, label: b"state", miss: SlabStateMiss.leaves(bail.id))
	let walk = emitSlabFastWalk(module, func: func, cur: stateReady, state: state, model: model, bail: bail, ids: ids, label: b"walk")

	let pop = module.addBlock(func, label: b"pop", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	let span = ids.mint()
	pop.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(span, argType: StdType.i64))

	emitSlabFastAllocUnscheduled(module, func: func, block: walk.unscheduled, state: state, classIdx: class, size: size, counting: counting, bail: bail, pop: pop, ids: ids)
	emitSlabFastAllocOwnArm(module, func: func, arm: walk.own, state: state, classIdx: class, size: size, counting: counting, bail: bail, pop: pop, ids: ids)

	return emitSlabPop(module, func: func, ready: pop, span: span, classIdx: class, evict: SlabEvictSlot.ofOwner, zeroed: zeroed, abortRoad: abortRoad, slotId: slotId, ids: ids, label: b"pop")
end 'emitSlabFastAlloc'

function buildSlabAllocFast(module StdModule, name ByteArray, zeroed bool, counting SlabCounting, model MachineModel)
	let func = IrFunction.createFromStd(name, returnType: StdReturnType.value(StdType.i64), paramCount: 1, paramTypes: oneI64Param())
	let ids = ValueMinter.create(1)

	let entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	let bail = module.addBlock(func, label: b"slow", heat: BlockHeat.cold)

	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 1)
	let size = 0

	let slot = ids.mint()
	let served = emitSlabFastAlloc(module, func: func, cur: entry, size: size, zeroed: zeroed, counting: counting, model: model, abortRoad: SlabAbortRoad.returnsTheDoor, bail: bail, slotId: slot, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(served, op: StdOp.ret(slot))

	emitCallAndReturn(module, block: bail, callee: slabSlowDoorName(name), args: argsOf(size), ids: ids)

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildSlabAllocFast'

function guardSlabStateBase(module StdModule)
	for func in module.functions 'eachFunction'
		if func.name.equals(SlabStateName) 'theTierBody'
			func.needsGreenThreadStackGuard = true
			func.pinsItsThread = true
			return
		end 'theTierBody'
	end 'eachFunction'

	panic("guardSlabStateBase: `{String.from(SlabStateName)}` is a runtime-tier body every allocating program carries, and the module holds no function of that name")
end 'guardSlabStateBase'

// The above-32 KiB road: the region committed, the calling thread's row credited on a counted or tracked door,
// and the request handed to a mapping of its own.
//
// ⚠ **IT TAKES NO LOCK, AND THAT IS A PROPERTY OF THE DESIGN RATHER THAN OF THE CAUTION.** The mapping's
// length lives in a 16-byte PREFIX inside the mapping itself (`OsDirectPrefixBytes`) rather than in a shared
// side table every allocation mutates, so an OS-direct alloc and free share no state with any other thread
// at all: one `osAllocPages`, two stores into pages nobody else can name yet. So the walk here resolves the
// shard only as far as the row it must credit.
function buildSlabAllocOversize(module StdModule, counting SlabCounting, model MachineModel)
	let func = IrFunction.createFromStd(SlabDoors.of(counting).allocOversize, returnType: StdReturnType.value(StdType.i64), paramCount: 1, paramTypes: oneI64Param(), needsGreenThreadStackGuard: true, pinsItsThread: true)
	let ids = ValueMinter.create(1)

	let entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry", heat: BlockHeat.normal)

	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 1)
	buildSlabOsDirectArm(module, func: func, cur: entry, size: 0, counting: counting, model: model, ids: ids)

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildSlabAllocOversize'

function buildSlabOsDirectArm(module StdModule, func IrFunction, cur IrBlock, size ValueId, counting SlabCounting, model MachineModel, ids ValueMinter)
	let state = ids.mint()
	let stateReady = emitSlabStateBase(module, func: func, cur: cur, stateId: state, ids: ids, label: b"state", heat: BlockHeat.cold)

	let arms = emitSlabResolveShard(module, func: func, cur: stateReady, state: state, model: model, need: SlabShardNeed.creditsOnly, traffic: slabThreadTrafficOf(counting, traffic: ThreadTraffic.alloc(size)), ids: ids, label: b"shard", heat: BlockHeat.cold)

	let osCall = module.addBlock(func, label: b"oscall", heat: BlockHeat.cold)
	emitSlabCountingCreditBothArms(module, arms: arms, event: SlabTrafficEvent.alloc(size), counting: counting, next: osCall, ids: ids)

	let mapped = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(osCall, op: StdOp.call(mapped, callee: SlabOsDirectAllocName, args: argsOf(size)))
	module.setTerminator(osCall, op: StdOp.ret(mapped))
end 'buildSlabOsDirectArm'

// The entry both locked doors share. Precondition: the caller holds `__slab_lock`, which is what makes this
// thread the sole writer of a span's free list, free count and class list while no processor owns it.
//
// ⚠ The lock does not order the owner re-read, which is why it is an acquire. An exhausted span is
// published by the processor that popped it dry, lock-free, so `__slab_lock` supplies no edge to that
// store: between the caller's unlocked read and this one a span may have been relisted, cached by another
// processor, popped dry and evicted again. A plain re-read would leave the free-list words under it free to
// be satisfied from before those pops, and this slot would go back onto a list a live slot is already on.
function emitSlabLockedDoorEntry(module StdModule, func IrFunction, entry IrBlock, handled IrBlock, ownerExpected SpanOwnerState, ids ValueMinter)
	let elsewhere = module.addBlock(func, label: b"notowner", heat: BlockHeat.cold)
	let doubleFree = module.addBlock(func, label: b"doublefree", heat: BlockHeat.cold)
	let moved = module.addBlock(func, label: b"moved", heat: BlockHeat.cold)

	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 2)
	let span = 1

	let owner = emitLoadAcquireField(module, block: entry, record: span, offset: MspanOwningPOffset, ids: ids)
	emitBranchOnCompare(module, block: entry, value: owner, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, against: ownerExpected.rawValue, whenTrue: handled, whenFalse: elsewhere, ids: ids)
	emitBranchOnCompare(module, block: elsewhere, value: owner, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, against: SpanOwnerState.freed.rawValue, whenTrue: doubleFree, whenFalse: moved, ids: ids)

	emitRuntimeAbort(module, func: func, block: doubleFree, abort: RuntimeAbort.slabFreeOfParkedSpan, ids: ids)

	let notHandled = emitConst(module, block: moved, value: SlabDoorOwnerMoved, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(moved, op: StdOp.ret(notHandled))
end 'emitSlabLockedDoorEntry'

// `__slab_free_to_full(slot, span) -> handled` — return a slot to an EXHAUSTED span and put that span back
// on its class's partial list, which is the one event per span that makes it reachable again.
//
// The lock is paid once per span rather than once per free. A span is `Full` only between the allocation
// that took its last slot and the first free that brings one back; every later free of that span sees
// `Partial` and takes the lock-free CAS. Routing `Partial` here as well would put one global lock — every
// class, every processor — on essentially every free in a drop-heavy program, which is what the per-span
// queue exists to avoid.
//
// ⛔ Nothing is destroyed here. `__slab_scavenge` is the only caller of `__slab_span_destroy`, and it
// reaches a span through the class list rather than through one of its slots — so no thread is ever inside
// a free of a span at the moment it is taken apart. Keeping the destroy off this path is what lets every
// other free stay lock-free.
function buildSlabFreeToFull(module StdModule)
	let func = IrFunction.createFromStd(SlabFreeToFullName, returnType: StdReturnType.value(StdType.i64), paramCount: 2, paramTypes: twoI64Params(), needsGreenThreadStackGuard: true, pinsItsThread: true)
	let ids = ValueMinter.create(2)

	let entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	let exhausted = module.addBlock(func, label: b"exhausted", heat: BlockHeat.normal)

	emitSlabLockedDoorEntry(module, func: func, entry: entry, handled: exhausted, ownerExpected: SpanOwnerState.full, ids: ids)

	let slot = 0
	let span = 1

	let state = emitSlabStateCommitted(module, block: exhausted, ids: ids)
	let classIdx = emitLoadField(module, block: exhausted, record: span, offset: MspanClassIdxOffset, ids: ids)
	let mcentralSlot = emitStateSlotAddr(module, block: exhausted, state: state, arrayOffset: SlabStateMcentralOffset as FullRangeInt, index: classIdx, ids: ids)

	// The queue is collected first, so a span whose slots came back across processors while it was
	// unreachable arrives on the list carrying them rather than needing a second visit to notice.
	module.appendOp(exhausted, op: StdOp.call(ids.mint(), callee: SlabCollectRemoteName, args: argsOf(span)))
	module.appendOp(exhausted, op: StdOp.call(ids.mint(), callee: SlabFreeToSpanName, args: twoArgs(slot, second: span)))
	emitMcentralLink(module, block: exhausted, span: span, mcentralSlot: mcentralSlot, ids: ids)

	let done = emitConst(module, block: exhausted, value: SlabDoorHandled, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(exhausted, op: StdOp.ret(done))

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildSlabFreeToFull'

// `__slab_free_to_raw(slot, span) -> handled` — push a slot back onto a span of the shared raw row, which
// every P-less OS thread allocates from and any of them may free into.
//
// ⚠ The re-check is what keeps such a span reachable. A thread that read `SpanOwnerState.none` and
// then waited for the lock can arrive after another thread took the span's last slot and evicted
// it: the row entry is clear and the owner reads `SpanOwnerState.full`. Pushing the slot anyway
// would put it on a span that is on no mcentral list and in no row, which no refill finds and no
// scavenge walk reaches — a span leaked for the life of the process once that slot is the last one
// outstanding.
function buildSlabFreeToRaw(module StdModule)
	let func = IrFunction.createFromStd(SlabFreeToRawName, returnType: StdReturnType.value(StdType.i64), paramCount: 2, paramTypes: twoI64Params())
	let ids = ValueMinter.create(2)

	let entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	let onRawRow = module.addBlock(func, label: b"onrawrow", heat: BlockHeat.normal)

	emitSlabLockedDoorEntry(module, func: func, entry: entry, handled: onRawRow, ownerExpected: SpanOwnerState.none, ids: ids)

	let slot = 0
	let span = 1

	module.appendOp(onRawRow, op: StdOp.call(ids.mint(), callee: SlabFreeToSpanName, args: twoArgs(slot, second: span)))

	let done = emitConst(module, block: onRawRow, value: SlabDoorHandled, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(onRawRow, op: StdOp.ret(done))

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildSlabFreeToRaw'

// `__slab_span_destroy(span)` — take a span apart: unregister its pages, hand its chunk run back to the
// arena, and put its header on the metadata free list.
//
// ⚠ The caller must have unlinked it from its class list already, must hold `__slab_lock`, and must have
// established that no slot in it is live. `__slab_scavenge` is the only caller and all three facts are
// structural there: it detaches the whole list before it walks, it holds the lock across its entire body,
// and it reaches this call only where `free_count == total_slots` after a collect.
//
// The reverse map is cleared, and it is not merely tidiness. Left registered, a stale slot points at
// an mspan header that `__slab_meta_free` is about to recycle for an unrelated span at an unrelated address
// — so a `__slab_free` of a dangling pointer into the released region would push that pointer onto a LIVE
// span's free list and hand it out as a slot. Cleared, the same free takes the map's MISS route and hits
// `slabOsDirectPointerNotOurs`: a named abort instead of silent heap corruption. `__slab_arena_map_set` with
// `ArenaMapNoSpan` is the clear, because "no span owns this page" is exactly what a fresh slot says.
function buildSlabSpanDestroy(module StdModule)
	let func = IrFunction.createFromStd(SlabSpanDestroyName, returnType: StdReturnType.void, paramCount: 1, paramTypes: oneI64Param())
	let ids = ValueMinter.create(1)

	let entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry", heat: BlockHeat.normal)

	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 1)
	let span = 0

	let geom = emitSpanGeometry(module, block: entry, span: span, ids: ids)
	let slotSize = emitGeomSlotSize(module, block: entry, geom: geom, ids: ids)
	let totalSlots = emitGeomTotalSlots(module, block: entry, geom: geom, ids: ids)
	let numChunks = emitSpanChunkCount(module, block: entry, slotSize: slotSize, totalSlots: totalSlots, ids: ids)
	let pageBase = emitLoadField(module, block: entry, record: span, offset: MspanBaseAddrOffset, ids: ids)

	let unowned = emitConst(module, block: entry, value: ArenaMapNoSpan, ids: ids)
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.call(ids.mint(), callee: SlabArenaMapSetName, args: threeArgs(pageBase, second: numChunks, third: unowned)))

	// The arena is ASKED rather than remembered — see `SlabArena.__slab_arena_of` for why a stored field
	// would have cost the mspan record its last word to answer a question only a destruction asks.
	let arenaBase = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.call(arenaBase, callee: SlabArenaOfName, args: argsOf(pageBase)))
	let byteOffset = emitBin(module, block: entry, a: pageBase, b: arenaBase, opcode: StdBinOpcode.sub, ids: ids)
	let pageShift = emitConst(module, block: entry, value: SlabPageShift, ids: ids)
	let chunkIndex = emitBin(module, block: entry, a: byteOffset, b: pageShift, opcode: StdBinOpcode.shr, ids: ids)
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.call(ids.mint(), callee: SlabArenaFreeChunksName, args: threeArgs(arenaBase, second: chunkIndex, third: numChunks)))

	// LAST, because everything above reads the header it hands back.
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.call(ids.mint(), callee: SlabMetaFreeName, args: argsOf(span)))
	module.setTerminator(entry, op: StdOp.retVoid)

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildSlabSpanDestroy'

// `__slab_scavenge() -> bytesDecommitted` — the whole reclamation pass, and the public door
// `__Builtins.scavengeMemory()` lowers to.
//
// **Two steps, in one call:** every fully free span on every mcentral list is destroyed, returning its
// chunks to the arena; then `__slab_arena_scavenge` drops the backing of every commit granule those releases
// have left entirely free. The answer is that second step's byte count — the only number this layer can
// honestly report, because whether the pages left the RESIDENT SET is the OS's answer and not the
// allocator's.
//
// ⛔ **THIS IS THE ONLY ROAD TO RECLAMATION.** No free and no refill destroys a span: a refill hands a fully
// free span back to its own class, so a program that never calls this keeps every emptied span on the list
// of the class that emptied it. That is a deliberate trade — it is what keeps the free path lock-free and
// costs the allocator nothing per free — and it is why a class a program has finished with holds its memory
// until somebody asks here.
//
// ⭐⭐ **THE TWO-EPOCH GRACE IS AT THE GRANULE, NOT AT THE SPAN.** `__slab_arena_scavenge` marks an all-free
// granule on one call and decommits it on the next, and a chunk claim clears the mark — so a heap that
// cycles a population pays neither a syscall nor a re-commit, and a caller that wants memory back after a
// burst still calls this TWICE, which the spec says out loud.
//
// ⭐ **THE LIST IS DETACHED AND REBUILT RATHER THAN SPLICED.** Removing a span from the middle of a singly
// linked list needs a `prev` carried through the walk, and a `prev` that is not updated on exactly the
// surviving arm is how a walk drops the rest of its own list. Taking the whole list, walking it and pushing
// the survivors onto a fresh head has no such variable — and it is what lets the mspan record keep the word
// a `prev` field would have cost it.
//
// ⛔ **NO BACKGROUND SCAVENGER, AND THAT IS A PROPERTY OF THIS RUNTIME RATHER THAN A PREFERENCE.** Go's is a
// goroutine paced against the GC's heap goal at 1% of mutator time (`mgcscavenge.go`), and neither half of
// that is reachable here: The compiler has no GC and so no heap goal to pace against, its scheduler is
// cooperative with no timer to hang a background G on, and green threads exist on ONE of the five lanes
// (`GtRuntime`), so a scavenger built on them would not run in a wasm or POSIX program at all. What Go's
// design contributes and this does take is the SHAPE UNDERNEATH the pacing — release at the page layer, in
// physical-page-aligned units, gated on a run having stayed idle — which is `__slab_arena_scavenge`.
//
// ⭐⭐ **THE PASS RUNS UNDER `__slab_lock`, WHICH IS WHY THE ARENA WALK NEEDS NONE.** Both arena bitmaps are
// written by every claim and every chunk release, each of which already runs inside this lock, so an
// unlocked walk would read a granule mid-transition and decommit one a claim had just taken.
function buildSlabScavenge(module StdModule, model MachineModel)
	let func = IrFunction.createFromStd(SlabScavengeName, returnType: StdReturnType.value(StdType.i64), paramCount: 0, paramTypes: StdTypeArray.create(0))
	let ids = ValueMinter.create(0)

	let entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	let classLoop = module.addBlock(func, label: b"classloop", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	let arenas = module.addBlock(func, label: b"arenas", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	let classStart = module.addBlock(func, label: b"classstart", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	let spanLoop = module.addBlock(func, label: b"spanloop", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	let spanBody = module.addBlock(func, label: b"spanbody", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	let keepSpan = module.addBlock(func, label: b"keepspan", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	let destroy = module.addBlock(func, label: b"destroy", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	let classDone = module.addBlock(func, label: b"classdone", heat: BlockHeat.normal)

	let state = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.call(state, callee: SlabStateName, args: ValueIdArray.create(0)))
	let depthAddr = ids.mint()
	let locked = emitSlabLockDepthAddr(module, func: func, cur: entry, state: state, model: model, addrId: depthAddr, ids: ids, label: b"depth")
	emitSlabLockEnter(module, block: locked, state: state, depthAddr: depthAddr, ids: ids)
	let firstClass = emitConst(module, block: locked, value: 0, ids: ids)
	emitBranchCarrying(module, from: locked, to: classLoop, values: argsOf(firstClass))

	let classIdx = ids.mint()
	classLoop.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(classIdx, argType: StdType.i64))

	emitBranchOnCompare(module, block: classLoop, value: classIdx, pred: StdCmpPred.ge, against: slabClassCount() as FullRangeInt, whenTrue: arenas, whenFalse: classStart, ids: ids)

	let decommitted = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(arenas, op: StdOp.call(decommitted, callee: SlabArenaScavengeName, args: ValueIdArray.create(0)))
	emitSlabLockLeave(module, block: arenas, state: state, depthAddr: depthAddr, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(arenas, op: StdOp.ret(decommitted))

	let mcentralSlot = emitStateSlotAddr(module, block: classStart, state: state, arrayOffset: SlabStateMcentralOffset as FullRangeInt, index: classIdx, ids: ids)
	let head = emitLoadField(module, block: classStart, record: mcentralSlot, offset: 0, ids: ids)
	let empty = emitConst(module, block: classStart, value: 0, ids: ids)
	emitStoreField(module, block: classStart, record: mcentralSlot, offset: 0, value: empty)
	emitBranchCarrying(module, from: classStart, to: spanLoop, values: twoArgs(head, second: empty))

	// The span under the walk, and the head of the list being rebuilt out of the ones that survive it.
	let span = ids.mint()
	let kept = ids.mint()
	spanLoop.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(span, argType: StdType.i64))
	spanLoop.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(kept, argType: StdType.i64))

	emitBranchOnCompare(module, block: spanLoop, value: span, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, against: 0, whenTrue: classDone, whenFalse: spanBody, ids: ids)
	pushBranchEdge(spanLoop, to: classDone, values: argsOf(kept))

	// The successor is read BEFORE either arm, because the destroy arm hands the header to the metadata free
	// list, which writes its own link over the span's first word — and a walk that read `next` afterwards
	// would follow the free list instead of the class's.
	let following = emitLoadField(module, block: spanBody, record: span, offset: MspanNextSpanOffset, ids: ids)

	// ⛔ The collect runs first, and `free_count == total_slots` under the lock afterwards is what makes the
	// destroy below safe. Write `free` for the span's free list, `queued` for slots CAS'd onto its stack,
	// `inflight` for a slot whose free has begun but is on neither, and `live` for the rest:
	// `free + queued + inflight + live == total_slots`, every term non-negative. The collect drains the
	// chain, so at the instant the test passes `queued`, `inflight` and `live` are all 0 — no thread is
	// anywhere inside a free of this span and none can start one. That holds only because the CAS in
	// `emitSlabRemotePush` is the LAST touch a freer makes to the span; a post-CAS touch would let this walk
	// credit a pusher's slot and recycle the header while that pusher was still arriving. It does not cover
	// a genuine double free, which drives `live` negative — `slabFreeOfParkedSpan` catches only the common
	// shape of that.
	//
	// The test reads `free_count` and never `remote_count`: a slot reaches `free_count` only through a push,
	// while `remote_count` is stepped BEFORE the CAS and so counts slots staged but not yet chained.
	module.appendOp(spanBody, op: StdOp.call(ids.mint(), callee: SlabCollectRemoteName, args: argsOf(span)))

	let spanGeom = emitClassGeometry(module, block: spanBody, classIdx: classIdx, ids: ids)
	let spanSlots = emitGeomTotalSlots(module, block: spanBody, geom: spanGeom, ids: ids)
	let spanFree = emitLoadField(module, block: spanBody, record: span, offset: MspanFreeCountOffset, ids: ids)
	emitBranchOnCompareValues(module, block: spanBody, a: spanFree, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, b: spanSlots, whenTrue: destroy, whenFalse: keepSpan, ids: ids)

	// A span still holding live slots goes back on the rebuilt list, still `Partial`.
	emitStoreField(module, block: keepSpan, record: span, offset: MspanNextSpanOffset, value: kept)
	emitBranchCarrying(module, from: keepSpan, to: spanLoop, values: twoArgs(following, second: span))

	module.appendOp(destroy, op: StdOp.call(ids.mint(), callee: SlabSpanDestroyName, args: argsOf(span)))
	emitBranchCarrying(module, from: destroy, to: spanLoop, values: twoArgs(following, second: kept))

	let survivors = ids.mint()
	classDone.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(survivors, argType: StdType.i64))
	emitStoreField(module, block: classDone, record: mcentralSlot, offset: 0, value: survivors)
	let one = emitConst(module, block: classDone, value: 1, ids: ids)
	let nextClass = emitBin(module, block: classDone, a: classIdx, b: one, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	emitBranchCarrying(module, from: classDone, to: classLoop, values: argsOf(nextClass))

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildSlabScavenge'

// The walk is tier source and only the lock is built here, because `emitSlabLockDepthAddr` forks on the
// machine model and a tier body has no knob for that. `__slab_census` carries why a read-only walk still
// needs the lock.
union SlabCensusArgument
	constant(kind FullRangeInt)
	forwarded
end 'SlabCensusArgument'

let SlabCensusForwardedParamPosition = 0

function buildSlabCensusEntry(module StdModule, name ByteArray, callee ByteArray, argument SlabCensusArgument, model MachineModel)
	let paramCount = match argument 'arity'
		constant gives 0 as ParamCount
		forwarded gives 1 as ParamCount
	end 'arity'

	let paramTypes = match argument 'paramTypes'
		constant gives StdTypeArray.create(0)
		forwarded gives oneI64Param()
	end 'paramTypes'

	let func = IrFunction.createFromStd(name, returnType: StdReturnType.value(StdType.i64), paramCount: paramCount, paramTypes: paramTypes, needsGreenThreadStackGuard: true, pinsItsThread: true)
	let ids = ValueMinter.create(paramCount)

	let entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: paramCount)

	let state = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.call(state, callee: SlabStateName, args: ValueIdArray.create(0)))

	let depthAddr = ids.mint()
	let locked = emitSlabLockDepthAddr(module, func: func, cur: entry, state: state, model: model, addrId: depthAddr, ids: ids, label: b"depth")
	emitSlabLockEnter(module, block: locked, state: state, depthAddr: depthAddr, ids: ids)

	let calleeArg = match argument 'argument'
		constant(kind) gives emitConst(module, block: locked, value: kind, ids: ids)
		forwarded gives valueIdAtParamPosition(SlabCensusForwardedParamPosition)
	end 'argument'

	let answer = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(locked, op: StdOp.call(answer, callee: callee, args: argsOf(calleeArg)))

	emitSlabLockLeave(module, block: locked, state: state, depthAddr: depthAddr, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(locked, op: StdOp.ret(answer))

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildSlabCensusEntry'

// `__slab_free_to_span(slot, span)` — put one slot back on its span's free list.
//
// **PUSH: `[slot] = free_list; free_list = slot; free_count += 1`** — the other half of INV-1.
//
// ⭐⭐ **THE COMMON SINK FOR EVERY FREE THAT REACHES A SPAN, AND IT IS ONE EMITTER BECAUSE IT HAS CALLERS
// THAT CANNOT SHARE A BLOCK.** `__slab_free`'s own local push, its serialised push for a slot on the shared
// raw row, `__slab_free_to_full`'s push, and the remote-queue replay in `__slab_collect_remote`; spelling
// the sequence at each would be four copies of one push.
//
// ⭐ **THE HOT CALLER EMITS IT INLINE AND THE COLD ONES STILL CALL `__slab_free_to_span`.** A local
// push is ~12 ops — less than the frame that reached it — and it is the arm every free of a program with
// one thread takes. The cold arms all run under `__slab_lock` and keep the call, so the function still
// exists and this is one spelling emitted twice, not two spellings.
//
// ⚠⚠ **EVERY CALLER MUST BE THE SPAN'S SOLE WRITER, AND EACH ESTABLISHES THAT DIFFERENTLY** — that is the
// contract this cannot check and its callers exist to satisfy. `__slab_free`'s local arm has compared
// `owning_p` against its own P; its raw arm and `__slab_free_to_full` hold `__slab_lock`; the collect runs
// either on the owner or inside the lock, which is the same rule its own callers carry.
function emitSlabFreeToSpan(module StdModule, func IrFunction, cur IrBlock, slot ValueId, span ValueId, ids ValueMinter, label ByteArray) returns IrBlock
	let done = module.addBlock(func, label: label, heat: BlockHeat.normal)

	let head = emitLoadField(module, block: cur, record: span, offset: MspanFreeListOffset, ids: ids)
	emitStoreField(module, block: cur, record: slot, offset: 0, value: head)
	emitStoreField(module, block: cur, record: span, offset: MspanFreeListOffset, value: slot)
	let live = emitLoadField(module, block: cur, record: span, offset: MspanFreeCountOffset, ids: ids)
	let one = emitConst(module, block: cur, value: 1, ids: ids)
	let released = emitBin(module, block: cur, a: live, b: one, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	emitStoreField(module, block: cur, record: span, offset: MspanFreeCountOffset, value: released)

	module.setTerminator(cur, op: StdOp.branch(done.id))

	return done
end 'emitSlabFreeToSpan'

// `__slab_free_to_span(slot, span)` — the same push behind a frame, for the COLD arms: the serialised raw-row
// one, `__slab_free_to_full`, and `__slab_collect_remote`'s replay loop. None is on any program's hot path,
// so the frame costs them nothing they would notice and saves the push being written into the emitted image
// three more times.
function buildSlabFreeToSpan(module StdModule)
	let func = IrFunction.createFromStd(SlabFreeToSpanName, returnType: StdReturnType.void, paramCount: 2, paramTypes: twoI64Params())
	let ids = ValueMinter.create(2)

	let entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry", heat: BlockHeat.normal)

	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 2)
	let slot = 0
	let span = 1

	let done = emitSlabFreeToSpan(module, func: func, cur: entry, slot: slot, span: span, ids: ids, label: b"push")
	module.setTerminator(done, op: StdOp.retVoid)

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildSlabFreeToSpan'

// `__slab_collect_remote(span)` — move every slot queued against `span` onto the free list its caching
// processor pops.
//
// One CAS detaches the whole chain, so the walk that follows touches nothing another thread can see and
// the per-slot pushes are the ordinary single-writer ones. Popping node by node would need a CAS per slot
// and would reintroduce the ABA problem a whole-chain detach does not have.
//
// The chain's length leaves `MspanRemoteCountOffset` in one step, before the replay: every node was
// counted before its push's CAS, so subtracting first can only undercount, and a census never sees a
// slot both queued and free.
//
// ⚠ `next` is read before the push, because the push writes the free-list link over the node's first word
// — the same word the queue's link lives in. Reading it afterwards follows the span's free list out of the
// chain being walked.
//
// ⚠ **ONE CONSUMER AT A TIME, ESTABLISHED TWO WAYS.** A span that a processor holds cached is collected by
// that processor and nobody else; a span no processor holds is collected under `__slab_lock`. Both callers
// carry their half, and the two cannot overlap because a span is in exactly one of those two conditions.
//
// The emptiness test precedes the CAS: on a program with no cross-processor traffic the queue is empty
// every time, and a CAS of zero for zero would put an uncontended atomic on every refill.
function buildSlabCollectRemote(module StdModule)
	let func = IrFunction.createFromStd(SlabCollectRemoteName, returnType: StdReturnType.void, paramCount: 1, paramTypes: oneI64Param())
	let ids = ValueMinter.create(1)

	let entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	let swap = module.addBlock(func, label: b"swap", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	let tally = module.addBlock(func, label: b"tally", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	let tallyStep = module.addBlock(func, label: b"tallystep", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	let uncount = module.addBlock(func, label: b"uncount", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	let walk = module.addBlock(func, label: b"walk", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	let replay = module.addBlock(func, label: b"replay", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	let done = module.addBlock(func, label: b"done", heat: BlockHeat.normal)

	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 1)
	let span = 0

	let headAddr = emitFieldAddr(module, block: entry, record: span, offset: MspanRemoteHeadOffset, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(entry, op: StdOp.branch(swap.id))

	let head = emitLoadField(module, block: swap, record: headAddr, offset: 0, ids: ids)
	let emptyQueue = emitConst(module, block: swap, value: 0, ids: ids)
	let nothingQueued = emitCompareWithConstant(module, block: swap, value: head, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, against: 0, ids: ids)

	let detach = module.addBlock(func, label: b"detach", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	module.setTerminator(swap, op: StdOp.condBranch(nothingQueued, thenBlockId: done.id, elseBlockId: detach.id))

	let won = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(detach, op: StdOp.atomicCas(won, addrId: headAddr, expectedId: head, replacementId: emptyQueue))
	let nothingCounted = emitConst(module, block: detach, value: 0, ids: ids)
	emitBranchOnCasLost(module, block: detach, result: won, lost: swap, won: tally, ids: ids)
	pushBranchEdge(detach, to: tally, values: twoArgs(head, second: nothingCounted))

	let counting = ids.mint()
	let counted = ids.mint()
	tally.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(counting, argType: StdType.i64))
	tally.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(counted, argType: StdType.i64))
	emitBranchOnCompare(module, block: tally, value: counting, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, against: 0, whenTrue: uncount, whenFalse: tallyStep, ids: ids)

	let nextCounting = emitLoadField(module, block: tallyStep, record: counting, offset: 0, ids: ids)
	let nextCounted = emitBin(module, block: tallyStep, a: counted, b: emitConst(module, block: tallyStep, value: OneMore, ids: ids), opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	emitBranchCarrying(module, from: tallyStep, to: tally, values: twoArgs(nextCounting, second: nextCounted))

	let queuedOut = emitBin(module, block: uncount, a: emitConst(module, block: uncount, value: 0, ids: ids), b: counted, opcode: StdBinOpcode.sub, ids: ids)
	_ = emitFieldAccumulate(module, block: uncount, record: span, offset: MspanRemoteCountOffset, delta: queuedOut, multiM: true, ids: ids)
	emitBranchCarrying(module, from: uncount, to: walk, values: argsOf(head))

	let node = ids.mint()
	walk.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(node, argType: StdType.i64))
	emitBranchOnCompare(module, block: walk, value: node, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, against: 0, whenTrue: done, whenFalse: replay, ids: ids)

	let following = emitLoadField(module, block: replay, record: node, offset: 0, ids: ids)
	module.appendOp(replay, op: StdOp.call(ids.mint(), callee: SlabFreeToSpanName, args: twoArgs(node, second: span)))
	emitBranchCarrying(module, from: replay, to: walk, values: argsOf(following))

	module.setTerminator(done, op: StdOp.retVoid)

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildSlabCollectRemote'

// `__slab_free(ptr)`, `__slab_free_tracked(ptr)` for a managed box (`__slab_free_box(ptr)` under
// `--poison-freed`) and `__slab_free_scaffold(ptr)` for a scaffold region — return a slot to its span, onto
// that span's remote stack, or a mapping to the OS.
//
// ⭐⭐ **ON A COUNTED OR TRACKED DOOR, BOTH TIERS DEBIT THE DOOR'S OWN LIVE COLUMN** — raw for a counted
// door, tracked for a tracked one — because a mapping and a slot
// are one free each — but each on the road that reaches it, because the reverse-map walk needs
// nothing but the pointer and holding the row, the P and the lock depth across it is what puts this
// frame's working set into spill slots.
//
// ⭐⭐ **THE LOCK DEPTH IS DERIVED WHERE THE LOCK IS TAKEN**, in the one arm that takes it, rather than
// carried from the shard walk: every other road through this body would be paying a `lea` and a live
// register for a lock it never touches.
//
// ⭐⭐ **THE FIRST ROUTING QUESTION IS ONE: DOES THE REVERSE MAP KNOW THIS PAGE?** A hit names the owning
// span; a MISS is the OS-direct sentinel (`SlabArena.ArenaMapNoSpan`), which is why nothing may ever
// register an OS-direct mapping's pages in that map.
//
// ⭐⭐ **THE SECOND IS WHO OWNS THE SPAN**, every arm of which is a fact about which thread may write the
// span's free list rather than a preference:
//
//   * **`SpanOwnerState.none` — the shared raw row.** Any P-less OS thread may hold slots of this
//     span and any thread at all may free one, so the push is serialised under `__slab_lock` — but
//     ONLY once a scheduler exists. Before that the lone main thread is the only writer and the
//     lock would guard nothing.
//   * **the P running me.** The ordinary case, and the one asked first of the four: a lock-free push onto a
//     list I am the sole writer of.
//   * **`SpanOwnerState.full` — exhausted and on no list.** `__slab_free_to_full` takes
//     `__slab_lock` and relists it, and a span is in this state once per cycle.
//   * **`SpanOwnerState.freed` — the header is already back on the metadata slab.** A double free,
//     and it aborts rather than CAS-pushing through a recycled record.
//   * **anything else — another P, or `SpanOwnerState.partial`.** CAS-push onto the SPAN's own
//     stack and leave; whoever collects it next puts the slot on the free list.
//
// ⭐⭐ **A LEAF TAKES THE LOCK-FREE ROADS AND THIS BODY TAKES THE REST.** `buildSlabFreeDoor` builds the leaf
// under the door's name and this body as `<door>_slow`, except for a tracked door in an untraced build,
// which `__mm_free` carries. The leaf frees onto its own P's span, onto the raw row of a program with no
// scheduler, and by the remote push onto a `partial` span or another P's. The push is in the leaf because
// a mass free leaves spans `partial`, and bailing there would pay the leaf's probes and then this body. A
// `full` span (the relist), a `freed` one (the abort), the raw row once a scheduler exists and an unmapped
// pointer leave by tail jump to this body.
//
// Either locked road may answer `SlabDoorOwnerMoved`, meaning the owner changed between the routing read and
// the door's re-read under the lock. The road is then chosen again from `routed`, carrying `myP` unchanged
// rather than re-deriving it: an asynchronous preemption lands only at a safe point in a user frame, and
// `osLockEnter` is `schedulerBound` rather than a bracketed kernel call, so this thread cannot have its
// processor taken while it waits — which is the premise the first routing pass already stands on.
//
// ⚠ **A NULL TARGET CANNOT HAPPEN AND IS NOT GUARDED, WHICH IS THE OTHER HALF OF STORING A POINTER.**
// Reconstructing the P from an id and an array that may not be populated would need the guard; here the
// pointer IS the one a live P stamped, so an owner that is neither `None` nor one of the negative states is
// a P that exists.
//
// ⚠ **`ptr` MUST BE A SLOT BASE, and nothing here can check it.** The free list is threaded through
// the slot's first word, so an INTERIOR pointer would corrupt the slot it lands in and then be
// handed out as a slot base. Every caller passes back exactly what its alloc door answered
// (`__mm_free` passes the box HEADER base, which is that pointer).
function buildSlabFree(module StdModule, door SlabFreeDoor, name ByteArray, model MachineModel)
	let counting = door.counting()
	let func = IrFunction.createFromStd(name, returnType: StdReturnType.void, paramCount: 1, paramTypes: oneI64Param())
	let ids = ValueMinter.create(1)

	let entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry", heat: BlockHeat.normal)

	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 1)
	let ptr = 0

	let span = ids.mint()
	let mapped = emitSlabArenaMapGet(module, func: func, cur: entry, ptr: ptr, spanId: span, ids: ids, label: b"map")
	let unmapped = emitCompareWithConstant(module, block: mapped, value: span, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, against: ArenaMapNoSpan, ids: ids)

	let toSpan = module.addBlock(func, label: b"tospan", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var afterMap = toSpan.id

	if door.poisons() 'poisonsTheSlot'
		afterMap = buildSlabFreedBoxPoison(module, func: func, span: span, ptr: ptr, next: toSpan, ids: ids)
	end 'poisonsTheSlot'

	let state = emitSlabStateCommitted(module, block: toSpan, ids: ids)
	let arms = emitSlabResolveShard(module, func: func, cur: toSpan, state: state, model: model, need: SlabShardNeed.holdsProc, traffic: slabThreadTrafficOf(counting, traffic: ThreadTraffic.free), ids: ids, label: b"shard")

	let routed = module.addBlock(func, label: b"routed", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	let myP = ids.mint()
	routed.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(myP, argType: StdType.i64))

	emitSlabCountingCredit(module, block: arms.sole.block, rowBase: arms.sole.trafficRow, event: SlabTrafficEvent.free, atomic: false, counting: counting, ids: ids)
	emitBranchCarrying(module, from: arms.sole.block, to: routed, values: argsOf(slabShardProc(arms.sole)))

	emitSlabCountingCredit(module, block: arms.serial.block, rowBase: arms.serial.trafficRow, event: SlabTrafficEvent.free, atomic: true, counting: counting, ids: ids)
	emitBranchCarrying(module, from: arms.serial.block, to: routed, values: argsOf(slabShardProc(arms.serial)))

	let rawOwned = module.addBlock(func, label: b"rawowned", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	let rawSerialised = module.addBlock(func, label: b"rawlocked", heat: BlockHeat.cold)
	let notRaw = module.addBlock(func, label: b"notraw", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	let uncached = module.addBlock(func, label: b"uncached", heat: BlockHeat.cold)
	let mine = module.addBlock(func, label: b"mine", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	let notDead = module.addBlock(func, label: b"notdead", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	let parked = module.addBlock(func, label: b"parked", heat: BlockHeat.cold)
	let local = module.addBlock(func, label: b"local", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	let remote = module.addBlock(func, label: b"remote", heat: BlockHeat.normal)

	let owner = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(routed, op: StdOp.loadAcquire(owner, addrId: span, offset: MspanOwningPOffset, valueType: StdType.i64))
	emitBranchOnCompare(module, block: routed, value: owner, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, against: SpanOwnerState.none.rawValue, whenTrue: rawOwned, whenFalse: notRaw, ids: ids)

	let threadsPossible = emitSlabSchedulerExists(module, block: rawOwned, schedulerWord: arms.schedulerWord, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(rawOwned, op: StdOp.condBranch(threadsPossible, thenBlockId: rawSerialised.id, elseBlockId: local.id))

	// The region and this thread's depth word are BOTH re-derived here, in the one arm that takes the lock.
	// The word is non-zero by construction: the allocation that answered `ptr` committed it.
	let lockState = emitSlabStateCommitted(module, block: rawSerialised, ids: ids)
	let depthAddr = ids.mint()
	let locked = emitSlabLockDepthAddr(module, func: func, cur: rawSerialised, state: lockState, model: model, addrId: depthAddr, ids: ids, label: b"rawlocked", heat: BlockHeat.cold)
	emitSlabLockEnter(module, block: locked, state: lockState, depthAddr: depthAddr, ids: ids)
	let rawAnswer = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(locked, op: StdOp.call(rawAnswer, callee: SlabFreeToRawName, args: twoArgs(ptr, second: span)))
	emitSlabLockLeave(module, block: locked, state: lockState, depthAddr: depthAddr, ids: ids)

	let rawReturned = module.addBlock(func, label: b"rawdone", heat: BlockHeat.cold)
	emitBranchOnCompare(module, block: locked, value: rawAnswer, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, against: SlabDoorOwnerMoved, whenTrue: routed, whenFalse: rawReturned, ids: ids)
	pushBranchEdge(locked, to: routed, values: argsOf(myP))

	module.setTerminator(rawReturned, op: StdOp.retVoid)

	// ⭐⭐ **`myP` IS ASKED FIRST OF THE FOUR, BECAUSE IT IS THE ANSWER IN A PROGRAM WITH ONE PROCESSOR AND
	// THE COMMON ONE IN EVERY OTHER.** The two sentinels below it are a state a span is in once per cycle,
	// and `Freed` is a program error; putting either ahead of the ownership test would pay a compare on
	// every free to reach a case almost no free takes.
	emitBranchOnCompareValues(module, block: notRaw, a: owner, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, b: myP, whenTrue: local, whenFalse: mine, ids: ids)

	emitBranchOnCompare(module, block: mine, value: owner, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, against: SpanOwnerState.full.rawValue, whenTrue: uncached, whenFalse: notDead, ids: ids)

	// ⛔ A span whose header has gone back to the metadata slab is a DOUBLE FREE, and a CAS onto it would
	// write through a recycled record. It is tested before the queue push and not after.
	emitBranchOnCompare(module, block: notDead, value: owner, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, against: SpanOwnerState.freed.rawValue, whenTrue: parked, whenFalse: remote, ids: ids)

	emitRuntimeAbort(module, func: func, block: parked, abort: RuntimeAbort.slabFreeOfParkedSpan, ids: ids)

	// ⛔ **THE LOCK IS TAKEN ONCE PER SPAN.** An exhausted span is on no list, so the freer that brings the
	// first slot back is the one that has to relist it — and it takes the lock BEFORE touching the span,
	// which is what keeps the destroy out of reach of an in-flight freer.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE LOCK IS THE RAW ARM'S BARGAIN, NOT A SECOND ONE.** Before a scheduler exists the lone main
	// thread is the only writer of anything, so the two arms are one call with and without the bracket
	// around it.
	let relistLocked = module.addBlock(func, label: b"relistlock", heat: BlockHeat.cold)
	let relistPlain = module.addBlock(func, label: b"relistplain", heat: BlockHeat.cold)
	let relistDone = module.addBlock(func, label: b"relistdone", heat: BlockHeat.cold)

	let othersPossible = emitSlabSchedulerExists(module, block: uncached, schedulerWord: arms.schedulerWord, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(uncached, op: StdOp.condBranch(othersPossible, thenBlockId: relistLocked.id, elseBlockId: relistPlain.id))

	let plainAnswer = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(relistPlain, op: StdOp.call(plainAnswer, callee: SlabFreeToFullName, args: twoArgs(ptr, second: span)))
	emitBranchCarrying(module, from: relistPlain, to: relistDone, values: argsOf(plainAnswer))

	let relistState = emitSlabStateCommitted(module, block: relistLocked, ids: ids)
	let relistDepth = ids.mint()
	let relistReady = emitSlabLockDepthAddr(module, func: func, cur: relistLocked, state: relistState, model: model, addrId: relistDepth, ids: ids, label: b"relist", heat: BlockHeat.cold)
	emitSlabLockEnter(module, block: relistReady, state: relistState, depthAddr: relistDepth, ids: ids)
	let lockedAnswer = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(relistReady, op: StdOp.call(lockedAnswer, callee: SlabFreeToFullName, args: twoArgs(ptr, second: span)))
	emitSlabLockLeave(module, block: relistReady, state: relistState, depthAddr: relistDepth, ids: ids)
	emitBranchCarrying(module, from: relistReady, to: relistDone, values: argsOf(lockedAnswer))

	let relistReturned = module.addBlock(func, label: b"relistret", heat: BlockHeat.cold)
	let tookIt = ids.mint()
	relistDone.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(tookIt, argType: StdType.i64))
	emitBranchOnCompare(module, block: relistDone, value: tookIt, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, against: SlabDoorOwnerMoved, whenTrue: routed, whenFalse: relistReturned, ids: ids)
	pushBranchEdge(relistDone, to: routed, values: argsOf(myP))

	module.setTerminator(relistReturned, op: StdOp.retVoid)

	let locallyPushed = emitSlabFreeToSpan(module, func: func, cur: local, slot: ptr, span: span, ids: ids, label: b"push")
	module.setTerminator(locallyPushed, op: StdOp.retVoid)

	let pushed = emitSlabRemotePush(module, func: func, cur: remote, span: span, slot: ptr, owner: owner, myP: myP, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(pushed, op: StdOp.retVoid)

	// ⚠ **NO LOCK** — see `buildSlabOsDirectArm`: the OS-direct tier keeps its length in the mapping's own
	// prefix and shares no state with any other thread. Built last, so its own credit walk lands past the
	// road every slot free takes.
	let osDirect = module.addBlock(func, label: b"osdirect", heat: BlockHeat.cold)

	module.appendOp(osDirect, op: StdOp.call(ids.mint(), callee: SlabDoors.of(counting).freeOversize, args: argsOf(ptr)))
	module.setTerminator(osDirect, op: StdOp.retVoid)

	module.setTerminator(mapped, op: StdOp.condBranch(unmapped, thenBlockId: osDirect.id, elseBlockId: afterMap))

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildSlabFree'

// Admits `partial` and another P, the owner words a leaf pushes remotely; every other word bails. See
// `buildSlabFree` for what each of the rest needs.
function emitSlabRemoteGate(module StdModule, func IrFunction, cur IrBlock, ownerWord ValueId, bail IrBlock, ok IrBlock, ids ValueMinter)
	let positive = module.addBlock(func, label: b"remotepos", heat: BlockHeat.normal)

	emitBranchOnCompare(module, block: cur, value: ownerWord, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, against: SpanOwnerState.partial.rawValue, whenTrue: ok, whenFalse: positive, ids: ids)
	emitBranchOnCompare(module, block: positive, value: ownerWord, pred: StdCmpPred.gt, against: 0, whenTrue: ok, whenFalse: bail, ids: ids)
end 'emitSlabRemoteGate'

function emitSlabFastFreeRawCredit(module StdModule, block IrBlock, state ValueId, counting SlabCounting, ids ValueMinter)
	let trafficRow = emitSlabTrafficRowAt(module, block: block, state: state, row: SlabRawShardRow, ids: ids)
	emitSlabCountingCredit(module, block: block, rowBase: trafficRow, event: SlabTrafficEvent.free, atomic: false, counting: counting, ids: ids)
end 'emitSlabFastFreeRawCredit'

function emitSlabFastFreeOwnCredit(module StdModule, func IrFunction, cur IrBlock, row SlabOwnRow, state ValueId, counting SlabCounting, ids ValueMinter) returns IrBlock
	let stepped = emitThreadTrafficStep(module, func: func, cur: cur, m: row.machine, traffic: slabThreadTrafficOf(counting, traffic: ThreadTraffic.free), ids: ids, label: b"ownthread", heat: BlockHeat.normal)

	let trafficRow = emitSlabTrafficRowBase(module, block: stepped, state: state, row: row.id, ids: ids)
	emitSlabCountingCredit(module, block: stepped, rowBase: trafficRow, event: SlabTrafficEvent.free, atomic: false, counting: counting, ids: ids)

	return stepped
end 'emitSlabFastFreeOwnCredit'

function emitSlabFastFreeUnscheduled(module StdModule, func IrFunction, block IrBlock, state ValueId, ptr ValueId, span ValueId, counting SlabCounting, bail IrBlock, link IrBlock, ids ValueMinter)
	let local = module.addBlock(func, label: b"rawowned", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	let other = module.addBlock(func, label: b"rawother", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	let gated = module.addBlock(func, label: b"rawgated", heat: BlockHeat.normal)

	let ownerWord = emitLoadAcquireField(module, block: block, record: span, offset: MspanOwningPOffset, ids: ids)
	emitBranchOnCompare(module, block: block, value: ownerWord, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, against: SlabNoProcessor, whenTrue: local, whenFalse: other, ids: ids)

	emitSlabFastFreeRawCredit(module, block: local, state: state, counting: counting, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(local, op: StdOp.branch(link.id))

	emitSlabRemoteGate(module, func: func, cur: other, ownerWord: ownerWord, bail: bail, ok: gated, ids: ids)
	emitSlabFastFreeRawCredit(module, block: gated, state: state, counting: counting, ids: ids)

	let noProc = emitConst(module, block: gated, value: SlabNoProcessor, ids: ids)
	let pushed = emitSlabRemotePush(module, func: func, cur: gated, span: span, slot: ptr, owner: ownerWord, myP: noProc, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(pushed, op: StdOp.retVoid)
end 'emitSlabFastFreeUnscheduled'

function emitSlabFastFreeOwn(module StdModule, func IrFunction, row SlabOwnRow, state ValueId, ptr ValueId, span ValueId, counting SlabCounting, bail IrBlock, link IrBlock, ids ValueMinter)
	let local = module.addBlock(func, label: b"ownowned", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	let other = module.addBlock(func, label: b"ownother", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	let gated = module.addBlock(func, label: b"owngated", heat: BlockHeat.normal)

	let ownerWord = emitLoadAcquireField(module, block: row.block, record: span, offset: MspanOwningPOffset, ids: ids)
	emitBranchOnCompareValues(module, block: row.block, a: ownerWord, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, b: row.p, whenTrue: local, whenFalse: other, ids: ids)

	let counted = emitSlabFastFreeOwnCredit(module, func: func, cur: local, row: row, state: state, counting: counting, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(counted, op: StdOp.branch(link.id))

	emitSlabRemoteGate(module, func: func, cur: other, ownerWord: ownerWord, bail: bail, ok: gated, ids: ids)
	let remoteCounted = emitSlabFastFreeOwnCredit(module, func: func, cur: gated, row: row, state: state, counting: counting, ids: ids)

	let pushed = emitSlabRemotePush(module, func: func, cur: remoteCounted, span: span, slot: ptr, owner: ownerWord, myP: row.p, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(pushed, op: StdOp.retVoid)
end 'emitSlabFastFreeOwn'

function emitSlabFastFreeOwnArm(module StdModule, func IrFunction, arm SlabOwnArm, state ValueId, ptr ValueId, span ValueId, counting SlabCounting, bail IrBlock, link IrBlock, ids ValueMinter)
	match arm 'arm'
		absent then return
		present(row) then emitSlabFastFreeOwn(module, func: func, row: row, state: state, ptr: ptr, span: span, counting: counting, bail: bail, link: link, ids: ids)
	end 'arm'
end 'emitSlabFastFreeOwnArm'

function buildSlabFreeFast(module StdModule, door SlabFreeDoor, model MachineModel)
	let func = IrFunction.createFromStd(door.name(), returnType: StdReturnType.void, paramCount: 1, paramTypes: oneI64Param())
	let ids = ValueMinter.create(1)

	let entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	let bail = module.addBlock(func, label: b"slow", heat: BlockHeat.cold)

	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 1)
	let ptr = 0

	emitSlabFastFree(module, func: func, cur: entry, ptr: ptr, door: door, model: model, bail: bail, ids: ids)

	emitCallAndReturnVoid(module, block: bail, callee: slabSlowDoorName(door.name()), args: argsOf(ptr), ids: ids)

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildSlabFreeFast'

// The free's leaf, under the same rule as `emitSlabFastAlloc`: each bail precedes the credit, so the full
// body credits the free once. The `--poison-freed` fill runs ahead of the walk and a bail repeats it in the
// full body, which writes the same bytes again.
function emitSlabFastFree(module StdModule, func IrFunction, cur IrBlock, ptr ValueId, door SlabFreeDoor, model MachineModel, bail IrBlock, ids ValueMinter)
	let counting = door.counting()

	let span = ids.mint()
	let mapped = emitSlabArenaMapGet(module, func: func, cur: cur, ptr: ptr, spanId: span, ids: ids, label: b"map")

	let toSpan = module.addBlock(func, label: b"tospan", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var entered = toSpan.id

	if door.poisons() 'poisonsTheSlot'
		entered = buildSlabFreedBoxPoison(module, func: func, span: span, ptr: ptr, next: toSpan, ids: ids)
	end 'poisonsTheSlot'

	let unmapped = emitCompareWithConstant(module, block: mapped, value: span, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, against: ArenaMapNoSpan, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(mapped, op: StdOp.condBranch(unmapped, thenBlockId: bail.id, elseBlockId: entered))

	let state = emitSlabStateCommitted(module, block: toSpan, ids: ids)
	let walk = emitSlabFastWalk(module, func: func, cur: toSpan, state: state, model: model, bail: bail, ids: ids, label: b"walk")

	let push = module.addBlock(func, label: b"push", heat: BlockHeat.normal)

	emitSlabFastFreeUnscheduled(module, func: func, block: walk.unscheduled, state: state, ptr: ptr, span: span, counting: counting, bail: bail, link: push, ids: ids)
	emitSlabFastFreeOwnArm(module, func: func, arm: walk.own, state: state, ptr: ptr, span: span, counting: counting, bail: bail, link: push, ids: ids)

	let pushed = emitSlabFreeToSpan(module, func: func, cur: push, slot: ptr, span: span, ids: ids, label: b"link")
	module.setTerminator(pushed, op: StdOp.retVoid)
end 'emitSlabFastFree'

// The fill runs on a span hit only: an OS-direct mapping goes back to the OS whole. It starts at the
// payload, so the prefix (whose first word the free list threads through) is untouched, and it runs to
// the end of the slot, so its cost grows with the class. A class with no room past the prefix is skipped.
function buildSlabFreedBoxPoison(module StdModule, func IrFunction, span ValueId, ptr ValueId, next IrBlock, ids ValueMinter) returns BlockId
	let sized = module.addBlock(func, label: b"poisonsized", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	let filled = module.addBlock(func, label: b"poisonfilled", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	let skip = boxPrefixBytes(module.debugStream)

	let slotSize = emitGeomSlotSize(module, block: sized, geom: emitSpanGeometry(module, block: sized, span: span, ids: ids), ids: ids)
	emitBranchOnCompare(module, block: sized, value: slotSize, pred: StdCmpPred.gt, against: skip, whenTrue: filled, whenFalse: next, ids: ids)

	let poisonFrom = emitBin(module, block: filled, a: ptr, b: emitConst(module, block: filled, value: skip, ids: ids), opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	let room = emitBin(module, block: filled, a: slotSize, b: emitConst(module, block: filled, value: skip, ids: ids), opcode: StdBinOpcode.sub, ids: ids)
	module.appendOp(filled, op: StdOp.memFill(poisonFrom, byteCountId: room, value: SlabFreedBoxPoisonByte))
	module.setTerminator(filled, op: StdOp.branch(next.id))

	return sized.id
end 'buildSlabFreedBoxPoison'

function buildSlabFreeOversize(module StdModule, counting SlabCounting, model MachineModel)
	let func = IrFunction.createFromStd(SlabDoors.of(counting).freeOversize, returnType: StdReturnType.void, paramCount: 1, paramTypes: oneI64Param(), needsGreenThreadStackGuard: true, pinsItsThread: true)
	let ids = ValueMinter.create(1)

	let entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry", heat: BlockHeat.normal)

	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 1)
	let ptr = 0

	let osState = emitSlabStateCommitted(module, block: entry, ids: ids)
	let osArms = emitSlabResolveShard(module, func: func, cur: entry, state: osState, model: model, need: SlabShardNeed.creditsOnly, traffic: slabThreadTrafficOf(counting, traffic: ThreadTraffic.free), ids: ids, label: b"shard", heat: BlockHeat.cold)

	let osCall = module.addBlock(func, label: b"oscall", heat: BlockHeat.cold)
	emitSlabCountingCreditBothArms(module, arms: osArms, event: SlabTrafficEvent.free, counting: counting, next: osCall, ids: ids)

	module.appendOp(osCall, op: StdOp.call(ids.mint(), callee: SlabOsDirectFreeName, args: argsOf(ptr)))
	module.setTerminator(osCall, op: StdOp.retVoid)

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildSlabFreeOversize'

// `ptr` must be exactly what `emitSlabAlloc` answered for the same `kind`, and never 0: a region
// freed under another kind's counting drifts a frees column from its total.
function emitSlabFree(module StdModule, block IrBlock, ptr ValueId, kind SlabBufferKind, ids ValueMinter)
	module.appendOp(block, op: StdOp.call(ids.mint(), callee: slabFreeDoorOf(module, kind: kind).name(), args: argsOf(ptr)))
end 'emitSlabFree'

function emitSlabAlloc(module StdModule, block IrBlock, bytes ValueId, kind SlabBufferKind, ids ValueMinter) returns ValueId
	let region = ids.mint()
	emitSlabAllocInto(module, block: block, result: region, bytes: bytes, kind: kind)
	return region
end 'emitSlabAlloc'

function emitSlabAllocInto(module StdModule, block IrBlock, result ValueId, bytes ValueId, kind SlabBufferKind)
	module.appendOp(block, op: StdOp.call(result, callee: slabAllocDoorOf(kind), args: argsOf(bytes)))
end 'emitSlabAllocInto'

export function slabAllocDoorOf(kind SlabBufferKind) returns immutable ByteArray
	return SlabDoors.of(slabCountingOf(kind)).alloc
end 'slabAllocDoorOf'

let SpanOwnerCheckName = "assertSpanOwnerAccessIsOrdered"

enum SpanOwnerAccess
	none
	orderedRead
	orderedPublication
end 'SpanOwnerAccess'

type SpanOwnerAccessCensus
	export var freeReads as bool
	export var freeToFullReads as bool
	export var freeToRawReads as bool
	export var allocReads as bool
	export var allocRawReads as bool
	export var refillPublishes as bool
	export var allocPublishes as bool
	export var allocRawPublishes as bool
	export var boxAllocReads as bool
	export var boxAllocPublishes as bool
	export var boxFreeReads as bool

	static function create() returns Self
		return Self{freeReads: false, freeToFullReads: false, freeToRawReads: false, allocReads: false, allocRawReads: false, refillPublishes: false, allocPublishes: false, allocRawPublishes: false, boxAllocReads: false, boxAllocPublishes: false, boxFreeReads: false}
	end 'create'

	function noteFunction(name ByteArray, reads bool, publishes bool)
		self.freeReads = self.freeReads or (reads and name == SlabFreeName)
		self.freeToFullReads = self.freeToFullReads or (reads and name == SlabFreeToFullName)
		self.freeToRawReads = self.freeToRawReads or (reads and name == SlabFreeToRawName)
		self.allocReads = self.allocReads or (reads and name == SlabAllocName)
		self.allocRawReads = self.allocRawReads or (reads and name == SlabAllocRawName)
		self.refillPublishes = self.refillPublishes or (publishes and name == SlabRefillName)
		self.allocPublishes = self.allocPublishes or (publishes and name == SlabAllocName)
		self.allocRawPublishes = self.allocRawPublishes or (publishes and name == SlabAllocRawName)
		self.boxAllocReads = self.boxAllocReads or (reads and name == MmAllocName)
		self.boxAllocPublishes = self.boxAllocPublishes or (publishes and name == MmAllocName)
		self.boxFreeReads = self.boxFreeReads or (reads and name == MmFreeName)
	end 'noteFunction'
end 'SpanOwnerAccessCensus'

// The owner word's ordering, checked rather than trusted: a plain load or store at that displacement in any
// body installed above is refused before the module reaches a backend. So are `__mm_alloc` and `__mm_free`,
// installed before the slab, when an untraced build carries the slab's hit path in them.
//
// The displacement alone is the key, and it is sound only because no other record these bodies read has a
// field at 48 — the mspan's neighbours are 40 and 56, the state head's lock spans that offset, a P is read
// at `POffId` and `POffRemoteFreeCount`, and the arena map is walked through computed slot addresses at
// displacement 0. A record that gains a field there is what would make this refuse a body that is correct.
//
// ⚠ It sees the plain and the ordered forms only. An atomic carries no displacement to key on, and the
// bodies lowered from `runtime/SlabRuntime.maxon` are installed before `firstFuncIndex` and are outside the
// walk — `__slab_meta_free`'s `Freed` stamp and the census walk's read of the word among them.
function assertSpanOwnerAccessIsOrdered(module StdModule, firstFuncIndex ElementIndex)
	let census = SpanOwnerAccessCensus.create()
	let opIndices = OpIndexArray.create(0)
	let access = StdAccessOperands.create()

	for index in 0 upto firstFuncIndex 'eachEarlierFunc'
		let name = module.getFunc(index).name

		if name == MmAllocName or name == MmFreeName 'aBoxBody'
			requireOrderedSpanOwnerAccessIn(module, func: module.getFunc(index), census: census, opIndices: opIndices, access: access)
		end 'aBoxBody'
	end 'eachEarlierFunc'

	for index in firstFuncIndex upto module.funcCount() 'eachFunc'
		requireOrderedSpanOwnerAccessIn(module, func: module.getFunc(index), census: census, opIndices: opIndices, access: access)
	end 'eachFunc'

	requireSpanOwnerCensusIsComplete(census, boxBodies: slabBoxDoorsAreInlined(module))
end 'assertSpanOwnerAccessIsOrdered'

function requireOrderedSpanOwnerAccessIn(module StdModule, func IrFunction, census SpanOwnerAccessCensus, opIndices OpIndexArray, access StdAccessOperands)
	module.collectLiveOpIndices(func, into: opIndices)

	var reads = false
	var publishes = false

	for opIdx in opIndices 'eachOp'
		classifyAccessOperands(liveRuntimeOpAt(module, opIdx: opIdx, reader: SpanOwnerCheckName), access: access)

		match spanOwnerAccessOf(access, func: func) 'access'
			none then break
			orderedRead then reads = true
			orderedPublication then publishes = true
		end 'access'
	end 'eachOp'

	census.noteFunction(func.name, reads: reads, publishes: publishes)
end 'requireOrderedSpanOwnerAccessIn'

function spanOwnerAccessOf(access StdAccessOperands, func IrFunction) returns SpanOwnerAccess
	match access.shape 'k'
		load then return spanOwnerReadOf(access, func: func)
		store then return spanOwnerWriteOf(access, func: func)
		neither or
		constant or
		globalAddress or
		atomic then return SpanOwnerAccess.none
	end 'k'
end 'spanOwnerAccessOf'

function spanOwnerReadOf(access StdAccessOperands, func IrFunction) returns SpanOwnerAccess
	if access.offset != MspanOwningPOffset 'notTheOwnerWord'
		return SpanOwnerAccess.none
	end 'notTheOwnerWord'

	match access.ordering 'ordered'
		plain then panic("{SpanOwnerCheckName}: '{String.from(func.name)}' reads a span's owning-P word at offset {MspanOwningPOffset} with a PLAIN load. Another OS thread publishes that word with a release store — the P that evicts an exhausted span, the refill that installs one, the relist that hands it back — and a plain load carries no ordering edge to it, so the span's free-list head and free count read after it may be satisfied from before the publication and this thread links a slot ahead of one already handed out. Read it through `emitLoadAcquireField`")
		ordered then return SpanOwnerAccess.orderedRead
	end 'ordered'
end 'spanOwnerReadOf'

function spanOwnerWriteOf(access StdAccessOperands, func IrFunction) returns SpanOwnerAccess
	if access.offset != MspanOwningPOffset 'notTheOwnerWord'
		return SpanOwnerAccess.none
	end 'notTheOwnerWord'

	match access.ordering 'ordered'
		plain then panic("{SpanOwnerCheckName}: '{String.from(func.name)}' publishes a span's owning-P word at offset {MspanOwningPOffset} with a PLAIN store. Another OS thread reads that word with an acquire load and then walks the span's free list, and a plain store gives it no edge to the list and count writes that precede it — so the reader can observe the new owner and a stale list. Emit it through `StdOp.storeRelease`")
		ordered then return SpanOwnerAccess.orderedPublication
	end 'ordered'
end 'spanOwnerWriteOf'

function requireSpanOwnerCensusIsComplete(census SpanOwnerAccessCensus, boxBodies bool)
	if boxBodies 'boxBodiesCarryThePath'
		requireSpanOwnerSiteWasSeen(census.boxAllocReads, funcName: MmAllocName, ordering: "acquire load")
		requireSpanOwnerSiteWasSeen(census.boxAllocPublishes, funcName: MmAllocName, ordering: "release store")
		requireSpanOwnerSiteWasSeen(census.boxFreeReads, funcName: MmFreeName, ordering: "acquire load")
	end 'boxBodiesCarryThePath'

	requireSpanOwnerSiteWasSeen(census.freeReads, funcName: SlabFreeName, ordering: "acquire load")
	requireSpanOwnerSiteWasSeen(census.freeToFullReads, funcName: SlabFreeToFullName, ordering: "acquire load")
	requireSpanOwnerSiteWasSeen(census.freeToRawReads, funcName: SlabFreeToRawName, ordering: "acquire load")
	requireSpanOwnerSiteWasSeen(census.allocReads, funcName: SlabAllocName, ordering: "acquire load")
	requireSpanOwnerSiteWasSeen(census.allocRawReads, funcName: SlabAllocRawName, ordering: "acquire load")
	requireSpanOwnerSiteWasSeen(census.refillPublishes, funcName: SlabRefillName, ordering: "release store")
	requireSpanOwnerSiteWasSeen(census.allocPublishes, funcName: SlabAllocName, ordering: "release store")
	requireSpanOwnerSiteWasSeen(census.allocRawPublishes, funcName: SlabAllocRawName, ordering: "release store")
end 'requireSpanOwnerCensusIsComplete'

function requireSpanOwnerSiteWasSeen(seen bool, funcName ByteArray, ordering String)
	if seen 'itWasSeen'
		return
	end 'itWasSeen'

	panic("{SpanOwnerCheckName}: no {ordering} of a span's owning-P word at offset {MspanOwningPOffset} was found in '{String.from(funcName)}', so the walk above refused nothing there and a module that has lost the ordering reads exactly like one that keeps it. Either that access moved to a spelling this walk does not recognise, or the door no longer touches the word")
end 'requireSpanOwnerSiteWasSeen'
